onale": "Assists upward rotation and controls excessive scapular elevation, providing a smaller but mechanically relevant component of scapulothoracic torque.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.14}, {"E_LT": 0.8600, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.7200, "E_mech": 0.2898, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Provides a secondary humeral-flexion component in the anterior pressing plane, but its alignment and leverage are inferior to those of the anterior deltoid.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.92}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 48 kg nominal load represents two 24 kg dumbbells.", "The effective force includes the dumbbells and the relevant gravitational contribution of the elevated forearms and upper arms.", "A supported near-upright backrest, neutral-to-moderately pronated grip, and elbows positioned slightly anterior to the frontal plane are assumed.", "The dumbbells remain approximately above the elbows, limiting but not eliminating the external elbow-flexion moment.", "Representative bilateral external moment arms are approximately 0.157 m at the shoulders and 0.083 m at the elbows."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 83.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 44.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 29.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 48.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 530.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 130.65, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 130.65}
40	single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl	Dumbbell Preacher Curl	Single-Arm Dumbbell Preacher Curl	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Dumbbell	28.00	68.68	{"brachialis": 30.83, "biceps_brachii": 27.06, "brachioradialis": 4.24, "pronators_supinators": 6.55}	{}	{}	{}	Unilateral	{"slug": "single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl", "notes": ["This exercise is the prompt's own Example 2 calibration case, so the result is a direct check on whether the model reproduces its stated guidance. All three specified muscles land inside their prescribed bands: brachialis at 0.6999 within 0.65 to 0.90, biceps_brachii at 0.6798 within 0.50 to 0.85, and brachioradialis at 0.3781 within 0.15 to 0.45. No band required stretching, which suggests the four-dimensional framework and the prompt's intuitions are consistent for single-joint upper-body work.", "Pronators_supinators is included beyond the three muscles the prompt names. The prompt excludes wrist muscles, rotator cuff and trunk stabilisers unless they generate meaningful task-relevant demand, and this entry is justified under that exception rather than in conflict with it: an offset dumbbell mass applies a real pronation moment and resisting it is required for the supinated position the biceps leverage assumes. At 6.55 cm2 it is 9.5 percent of the total. If the calibration example is meant to be exhaustive, this entry should be dropped and the total falls to 62.13 cm2.", "This completes a four-point curl series that isolates shoulder position and grip cleanly. The brachialis-to-biceps ratio runs 0.44 for the hammer preacher, 1.14 here, 1.11 for the incline curl, and 0.96 for the Bayesian curl. Ordering by biceps favourability gives hammer preacher worst, then supinated preacher, then incline, then Bayesian best, which matches the biomechanical prediction that shoulder extension favours the long head and shoulder flexion penalises it.", "The direct comparison with preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl is the cleanest grip isolation in the library: identical bench, identical geometry, identical limiting phase, differing only in forearm orientation and load. Supination raises biceps_brachii from E_mech 0.2830 to 0.6798, a 2.4-fold increase, while brachioradialis falls from 0.6413 to 0.3781. Brachialis is nearly unchanged at 0.5818 against 0.6999, the residual difference reflecting the heavier load rather than grip.", "Total demand of 68.68 cm2 sits below the incline curl at 76.66 and the Bayesian curl at 85.10 despite the incline curl carrying the same 28 kg. The preacher geometry is the reason: the limiting phase occurs at 160 degrees of extension where internal moment arms are poor, so more of the available FCSA is consumed producing the same joint moment. The model reproduces without special-casing why preacher curls feel hardest at the bottom.", "The 28 kg load is taken as given from the prompt rather than independently derived. Note that this is the same load assigned to the incline curl, whereas preacher geometry loads the weakest point of the strength curve with no momentum available; an athlete curling 28 kg on an incline bench would typically manage somewhat less on a preacher bench for the same rep count.", "Deltoid_anterior was deliberately excluded, in contrast to the incline and Bayesian curls where it held the humerus against a shoulder extension moment. The preacher pad absorbs the entire shoulder moment mechanically, so no active shoulder torque is required at all.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded. At 279 N the grip demand is modest and the dumbbell sits in the palm rather than being suspended from a hook grip, so grip is not a meaningful transmission bottleneck.", "An EZ-bar or barbell preacher curl must not inherit these values. A symmetric implement removes the rotational moment that admits pronators_supinators here, and an EZ-bar's semi-supinated grip would sit between this and the hammer variant on biceps allocation.", "A machine preacher curl must not inherit these values either. Cam-driven resistance alters the strength curve so the limiting phase may no longer fall at full extension, which is the single assumption driving the low E_MA values throughout this entry."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deeply extended bottom: elbow near 160 degrees of extension with the upper arm fixed against the preacher pad, where the 45 degree pad angle places the forearm close to horizontal and the external moment arm is greatest while internal leverage is worst.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 68.68, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6999, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Major elbow-flexion contributor as the prompt specifies, maximally lengthened at the extended bottom and unaffected by shoulder position or forearm orientation. The poor moment arm at 160 degrees of extension is the binding constraint rather than any alignment issue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 30.83}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.6798, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Major contributor affected by shoulder and forearm position exactly as the prompt describes: the supinated grip gives near-optimal alignment, but the preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, shortening the long head and costing length-tension relative to an incline or Bayesian curl.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.06}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.4020, "E_mech": 0.1076, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the offset dumbbell mass applies a continuous pronation moment that must be actively resisted for the supinated position the biceps leverage depends on. Demand exceeds the incline curl because the near-horizontal forearm at the limiting phase maximises the rotational lever.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6690, "E_mech": 0.3781, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Secondary contributor dependent on grip orientation as the prompt specifies: the fully supinated grip moves it away from its optimal mid-position, and the extended elbow angle compounds the disadvantage.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.24}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention as stated in the prompt: 28 kg total is interpreted as 14 kg per arm.", "Dumbbell weight 275 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~279 N effective, ~140 N per limb.", "Standard preacher bench with the pad inclined at ~45 degrees; the upper arm is fully supported and immobilised against the pad.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to dumbbell centre ~0.155 m; at the extended bottom the forearm approaches horizontal and ~0.150 m acts as the effective external moment arm.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.028 m at 160 degrees of extension, substantially reduced from the ~0.042 m available at 90 degrees.", "Supinated grip held throughout, placing biceps_brachii in its most favourable forearm orientation.", "The preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, which shortens the biceps long head across that joint and partially offsets the advantage the supinated grip confers.", "The pad immobilises the humerus and absorbs all shoulder moment, so no glenohumeral or scapular torque is required and no deltoid contribution is needed.", "The bench supports the torso fully, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque and no cheat-swing contribution is available.", "The limiting phase is the stretched bottom rather than the mid-range, because the preacher geometry combines a near-maximal external moment arm with the worst internal leverage of the range.", "28 kg total is the load specified in the prompt's own calibration example for this exercise and is treated as given rather than independently derived."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 41.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 28.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 279.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 68.68, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 68.50}
