at the sticking point, with favorable leverage in the mid-range of the press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.97}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3500, "E_mech": 0.0870, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabilizer: with a free barbell, cuff force is required to compress and centre the humeral head so that the ~736 N external load can be transmitted through the glenohumeral joint; subscapularis also adds a small internal-rotation/adduction moment. Most of its capacity is non-propulsive, hence the low engagement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8123, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor, mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.66}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.6000, "E_mech": 0.2268, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabilizer: scapular fixation against the thorax is required to transfer the humeral load into the trunk, and it contributes protraction torque late in the press. Much of its force is isometric rather than propulsive.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.17}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.5852, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Assists horizontal adduction, but the flat bench angle demands less humeral flexion, lowering task-action alignment relative to an incline press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.91}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3600, "E_mech": 0.1499, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Only the anterior fibres add a small flexion component; the abduction line of action is largely orthogonal to the required net humeral moment, and part of the force is used for joint control.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.86}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.5000, "E_mech": 0.1275, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Small mechanically necessary contribution to scapular anchoring during load transmission; minimal net task torque.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.38}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Barbell weight 706 N plus ~30 N from the accelerated mass of forearms and hands.", "Load split evenly: ~368 N per limb at the hand.", "Grip ~1.5x biacromial width, moderately tucked elbows; perpendicular distance from GH joint to the vertical load line ~0.14 m per side.", "Forearms near-vertical at the sticking point; elbow external moment arm ~0.06 m per side.", "Effective internal moment arm for combined humeral horizontal adduction/flexion ~0.05 m; triceps elbow-extension moment arm ~0.025 m.", "Free barbell: no guidance, so glenohumeral force transmission and scapular fixation are mechanically required to carry the load.", "72 kg equals 72% of the 100 kg reference 1RM, consistent with an 8-12 rep set at RIR 1-2."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 103.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 44.20}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 22.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 72.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 736.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 175.61, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 176.00}
11	neutral_grip_lat_pulldown	Neutral-Grip Lat Pulldown	Selectorized Neutral-Grip Lat Pulldown	Upper	Back_V	Secondary_Compound	Selectorized_Machine	75.00	191.51	{"rhomboids": 15.63, "brachialis": 32.78, "teres_major": 3.51, "rotator_cuffs": 8.55, "wrist_flexors": 27.75, "biceps_brachii": 24.32, "brachioradialis": 7.34, "trapezius_lower": 10.87, "latissimus_dorsi": 34.50, "trapezius_middle": 14.49, "deltoid_posterior": 8.31, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.46}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "neutral_grip_lat_pulldown", "notes": ["Trapezius_lower reaches E_mech 0.7254, the highest scapular value in the library so far, against 0.4358 in the chest-supported row and 0.2090 in the face pull. The mechanism is direct: a pulldown requires scapular depression from an elevated start, which is precisely its line of action, whereas rows require retraction where middle trapezius and rhomboids dominate. This is the clearest scapular differentiation the model has produced and is the mechanical basis for the common programming distinction between vertical and horizontal pulling.", "The comparison with chest_supported_dumbbell_row is close to a controlled experiment: 191.51 against 175.19 cm2 at 75 versus 60 kg, both anchored, both without straps. Latissimus rises only from 32.19 to 34.50 cm2 despite a 25 percent heavier load, because the overhead position maximises its length while the row exploits a longer external moment arm. The scapular allocation reorders entirely, with trapezius_lower moving from fourth to first among the retractors.", "Biceps_brachii at E_mech 0.6108 is the highest in any pulling exercise here, exceeding the pronated Pendlay row at 0.5148 despite similar elbow moments. Two factors combine: the neutral grip removes the pronation leverage penalty, and the overhead start means the long head's shoulder-extension action assists the required humeral moment rather than being irrelevant to it. This is the mechanical reason neutral and supinated pulldowns are perceived as more biceps-intensive than wide overhand variants.", "Pectoralis_major_sternal is included as a genuine low-level agonist rather than an antagonist, which may look counterintuitive. From full overhead elevation its fibres do produce humeral adduction toward the torso, and the contribution is real though small at 3.46 cm2. It would be excluded outright in a row, where the humerus never elevates above shoulder height.", "No muscle saturates and the top value is 0.7867, so this is a well-balanced exercise mechanically. Elbow flexors reach 64.44 cm2 combined against 95.08 available, and the shoulder extensors 46.32 against 73.00, meaning neither group is the sole bottleneck at this load.", "A wide pronated-grip pulldown must not inherit these values. Pronation reduces biceps_brachii leverage by roughly 20 percent, the wider grip shortens the effective humeral moment arm, and the emphasis shifts from adduction toward horizontal abduction, raising deltoid_posterior and lowering teres_major.", "A pull-up or chin-up must not inherit these values either. Those are bodyweight exercises where the moved mass is roughly 85 kg rather than a selectable stack, and the trunk and hip musculature contribute genuine anti-swing torque that the thigh pads eliminate here.", "Grip is the least certain assumption. If performed with straps, wrist_flexors would drop out entirely and total demand would fall to roughly 164 cm2. Consider a strap-usage flag if the library will distinguish strapped from unstrapped variants."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the fully stretched top position: humerus flexed and abducted overhead at roughly 160 degrees of elevation, elbows near-extended, scapulae elevated and upwardly rotated, before the elbows drive down and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 191.51, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9740, "E_mech": 0.7867, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 141 N-m glenohumeral adduction and extension moment, at maximal stretched length in the fully overhead start, which is the defining mechanical advantage of the pulldown pattern. The required humeral action matches its line of pull almost exactly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.50}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6983, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 42.3 N-m elbow moment, lengthened at the near-extended start and unaffected by forearm orientation. Moment arm is reduced at the extended elbow relative to a mid-range curl.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.78}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8828, "E_mech": 0.5102, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps, finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the 735 N reaches the arm. Demand scales above the chest-supported row in proportion to the heavier load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.75}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6108, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Highly engaged elbow flexor: the neutral grip removes the pronation penalty of a wide overhand pulldown, and the overhead start lengthens the long head across the shoulder while its shoulder-extension action assists rather than opposes the required humeral moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 24.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.7610, "E_mech": 0.5531, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine task torque: its downward-rotation action directly matches the scapular motion required as the elevated scapula is pulled down from the overhead start. Alignment is partial because retraction is secondary to depression in this movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.63}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8035, "E_mech": 0.5535, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Contributes to the 66 N-m scapulothoracic moment, though its horizontal retraction vector only partly matches the predominantly downward scapular motion of a pulldown.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.49}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9980, "E_mech": 0.7254, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Best-aligned scapular muscle in the exercise: its depression vector matches the required downward scapular moment directly, and it is well lengthened by the elevated overhead start. Highest E_mech of any scapular muscle evaluated in the library so far.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.87}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2800, "E_mech": 0.0500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the overhead position with 735 N of cable tension applies a large distraction load to the humeral head, and cuff compression is required for that force to transmit into the scapula. Almost entirely non-propulsive.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.5570, "E_mech": 0.3373, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Assists humeral extension from the overhead position, but its horizontal abduction vector contributes little to the predominantly adduction-direction moment required by a neutral-grip pulldown.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6543, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral grip places it in its most mechanically favourable forearm orientation, offsetting the reduced moment arm at the near-extended elbow.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.34}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7800, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral adduction and extension, both directly required here, and is maximally lengthened at the overhead start.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.51}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.1620, "E_mech": 0.0646, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Contributes a genuine humeral adduction component from the fully overhead position, where its fibres act to pull the arm down toward the torso. Alignment is low because that action applies only in the earliest part of the range and its horizontal adduction vector is largely irrelevant to the task.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.46}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial selectorized lat pulldown with a 1:1 cable ratio; 75 kg nominal yields ~735 N at the handle after friction, ~368 N per limb.", "Seated with thigh pads engaged, torso held near-vertical with a slight backward lean of ~10 degrees.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the overhead start the cable line runs close to the humeral long axis, giving a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.192 m per side for the adduction and extension component.", "Elbows near-extended at the limiting phase with the cable line close to perpendicular to the forearm, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.057 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension and adduction ~0.058 m in the overhead position, elbow flexors ~0.030 m at the near-extended elbow, scapular depressors and retractors ~0.055 m.", "Neutral parallel-grip handle: the forearm is in mid-position, which favours brachialis and brachioradialis and places biceps_brachii at moderate leverage without a supination demand.", "Scapular depression and downward rotation from the elevated overhead start is genuine task torque, not stabilisation: the scapula must be actively pulled down against the load before and during humeral adduction.", "Thigh pads anchor the body against the upward pull of the stack, transmitting the reaction force through the skeleton to the seat, so no trunk or hip musculature generates net task torque.", "The handle is gripped without straps, so grip force is mechanically required to transmit the full 735 N.", "75 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for the reference athlete, consistent with a pulldown being performed at roughly 85 to 90 percent of bodyweight."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 141.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 66.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 42.30}], "nominal_load_kg": 75.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 735.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 191.51, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 191.50}
2	plate_loaded_converging_incline_chest_press	Converging Incline Chest Press	Plate-Loaded Converging Incline Chest Press	Upper	Chest_Clav_AD	Secondary_Compound	Plate_Loaded_Machine	70.00	141.68	{"rotator_cuffs": 6.32, "deltoid_lateral": 3.90, "deltoid_anterior": 23.14, "pectoralis_minor": 0.96, "serratus_anterior": 6.12, "triceps_long_head": 21.53, "triceps_medial_head": 11.46, "triceps_lateral_head": 26.53, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 31.90, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 9.82}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "plate_loaded_converging_incline_chest_press", "notes": ["Pectoralis_major_clavicular reaches E_mech 0.7262, the highest value it receives in any pressing exercise in the library, against 0.7711 in the Smith incline and 0.5852 in the flat barbell bench. The converging path is the reason: it adds a genuine horizontal adduction component at the finish that a fixed parallel bar path cannot provide, so these fibres get both the favourable incline angle and the adduction vector they are best oriented for.", "The converging geometry also partly rescues pectoralis_major_sternal, at E_LoA 0.7760 against 0.8200 on the Smith incline despite the same bench angle. Without convergence the incline penalises the sternal fibres purely; here the inward handle travel restores some of the adduction demand they are aligned with.", "This completes a four-point press comparison at matched effort. Flat barbell 175.61, Smith incline 157.13, flat plate-loaded 148.89, and converging incline 141.68 cm2. The ordering is driven almost entirely by machine lever ratios and by how much stabilisation the seat back and rails absorb, not by the incline angle itself, and it confirms that nominal stack or plate load is a poor cross-modality demand proxy.", "Machine lever ratio at 0.72 is the least certain input and drives the result directly. If the actual machine runs closer to 0.85, total demand would rise to roughly 165 cm2 and this would overtake the Smith incline. A per-manufacturer lever ratio field would materially improve confidence across the whole plate-loaded and selectorized category.", "Rotator_cuffs at E_mech 0.0370 is now the lowest value assigned to it in any pressing exercise, below the flat plate-loaded machine at 0.0428 and well below the free barbell at 0.0870. The progression across the library tracks path constraint cleanly: free barbell, then Smith rail, then fully guided carriage.", "A parallel-path plate-loaded incline press must not inherit these values. Removing convergence would drop pectoralis_major_clavicular to roughly the Smith incline level and reduce total demand by around 3 cm2, with the loss concentrated entirely in the pectoral allocation.", "A flat converging press must not inherit these values either. Convergence and bench angle interact rather than adding independently, since at flat the sternal fibres already receive their favourable adduction vector and convergence adds proportionally less."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the stretched position: handles at upper-chest level, elbows flexed to roughly 90 to 100 degrees, humerus horizontally abducted and elevated toward the incline angle, before the converging path draws the handles inward and the machine lever arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 141.68, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.7760, "E_mech": 0.5965, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Largest single contributor to the humeral moment, aided by the converging path which restores some horizontal adduction demand at the finish. Alignment remains below a flat press because the incline shifts the required action toward flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 31.90}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9340, "E_mech": 0.7348, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor acting directly against the 36 N-m bilateral elbow flexion moment at a favourable joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9150, "E_mech": 0.7597, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "The 35 to 40 degree incline places the required humeral moment squarely in its line of action, and the guided carriage permits near-full expression of that capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.14}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.8060, "E_mech": 0.4944, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Contributes elbow-extension torque, but the elevated humeral position both shortens it across the shoulder and increases the degree to which its shoulder-extension action opposes the required flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 21.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9340, "E_mech": 0.7348, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.46}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9230, "E_mech": 0.7262, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "The incline angle combined with the converging inward path is close to optimal for these fibres, giving the highest engagement they receive in any pressing exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.82}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2420, "E_mech": 0.0370, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: glenohumeral compression is required to transmit ~530 N through the joint at an elevated humeral position. Below the free-barbell and Smith cases because the guided carriage removes path control entirely.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.5030, "E_mech": 0.1698, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: the inclined humeral position raises the upward-rotation and scapular fixation requirement, though the seat back absorbs part of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.12}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0853, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Only the anterior fibres assist the flexion component; the abduction line of action remains largely orthogonal to the required net humeral moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.90}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.4350, "E_mech": 0.0888, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Minor contribution to scapular anchoring; its depression action partly conflicts with the upward rotation the incline requires, and the seat back carries much of the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 0.96}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial plate-loaded converging incline chest press; exact cam and lever geometry unavailable and approximated.", "Machine lever ratio ~0.72 at the handles in the stretched position, plus ~5 kg of carriage mass: 70 kg nominal resolves to ~54 kg of effective handle resistance, ~530 N total, ~265 N per limb.", "Seat back set to ~35 to 40 degrees from horizontal, consistent with a dedicated incline press frame.", "Handles at upper-chest height with moderately flared elbows; perpendicular distance from the GH joint to the handle force line ~0.142 m per side.", "Forearm roughly aligned with the push vector; elbow external moment arm ~0.068 m per side.", "Effective internal moment arm for combined humeral flexion and horizontal adduction ~0.05 m; triceps elbow-extension moment arm ~0.025 m.", "Converging path: the handles travel inward as well as forward, adding a genuine horizontal adduction component at the finish that a fixed parallel path does not provide.", "Seat back and the rail-guided carriage fix the torso and scapulae and control the movement path, so balance and free-weight path control are not mechanical requirements.", "70 kg on a machine with this lever ratio corresponds to roughly 54 kg of free-weight-equivalent incline pressing resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the 100 kg reference flat-bench presser."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 75.30}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 36.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 15.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 70.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 530.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 141.68, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 141.50}
39	single_arm_kneeling_wide_cable_row	One-Arm Kneeling Cable Row	Single-Arm Kneeling Wide-Elbow Cable Row	Upper	Back_3D	Secondary_Compound	Cable	70.00	178.36	{"obliques": 26.14, "rhomboids": 16.53, "brachialis": 23.79, "rotator_cuffs": 6.83, "wrist_flexors": 22.62, "biceps_brachii": 9.75, "erector_spinae": 10.65, "brachioradialis": 3.30, "gluteus_maximus": 3.58, "trapezius_lower": 5.72, "latissimus_dorsi": 22.60, "trapezius_middle": 15.71, "deltoid_posterior": 14.72, "transverse_abdominis": 2.42}	{}	{}	{}	Unilateral	{"slug": "single_arm_kneeling_wide_cable_row", "notes": ["This is the first exercise in the library where a trunk muscle is the largest single contributor. Obliques at 26.14 cm2 exceed every pulling muscle, and the 55 N-m rotational moment is the highest anti-rotation demand recorded, above standing_cable_pallof_press at 44 N-m and side_facing_reverse_pec_deck at 42 N-m. The kneeling base is why: it removes the foot platform and seat that a standard row uses to transmit the reaction force, so the trunk must carry it.", "The comparison with wide_grip_seated_cable_row is close to a controlled experiment on bracing. Totals land at 178.36 against 178.24 cm2, near-identical, but composition differs sharply. The seated version at 80 kg allocates 27.86 cm2 to latissimus and 6.71 to erector spinae; this at 70 kg on one arm allocates 22.60 and 10.65, and adds 28.56 cm2 of oblique and transverse abdominis work the seat and foot platform eliminate. Equal total demand, substantially different distribution.", "This is the third exercise requiring the unilateral-asymmetric reading, after the Pallof press and the side-facing reverse pec deck. The pattern is now firm enough to state as a rule: when a single-sided load generates a rotational moment that is central to the exercise rather than incidental, mirroring it under the bilateral convention deletes the defining demand. Three exercises in the library now depend on an interpretation the stated convention does not sanction, and the laterality flag should be treated as a required schema change rather than an optional one.", "Wrist_flexors at 22.62 cm2 represents the highest per-hand grip demand among the rows, since the full 686 N passes through one hand rather than being split. In absolute terms it sits below the two-handed rows only because those carry heavier total loads. With straps, total demand would fall to roughly 156 cm2 and the oblique contribution would become an even larger share.", "Latissimus_dorsi at E_LoA 0.7920 sits close to the wide seated row at 0.7620 and well below the vertical pulls at 0.97 to 0.98. Both wide-elbow rows trade lat alignment for retractor alignment, and the consistency across two independently evaluated exercises supports the treatment.", "Gluteus_maximus enters on transverse-plane grounds at only 3.58 cm2, its smallest contribution in the library by a wide margin against 103.99 in the Pendlay row. The inclusion is marginal and defensible only under the load-transmission exception; it should be dropped if the stabiliser criterion is tightened.", "A seated single-arm cable row with a foot platform must not inherit these values. Bracing the feet transmits the rotational reaction through the legs, removing most of the 28.56 cm2 of trunk contribution and permitting a heavier load per arm.", "A standing single-arm cable row must not inherit these values either. A standing base is wider and permits a counterlean, which reduces the trunk demand relative to kneeling without eliminating it.", "A close-elbow kneeling variant must not inherit these values. Narrowing the elbow path would raise latissimus and biceps substantially while cutting deltoid_posterior and the retractors, converting it into a different exercise in the same way the close and wide seated rows differ.", "A half-kneeling variant with the outside leg forward would differ from tall kneeling in anti-rotation demand, since the staggered base provides a longer lever against rotation. The values here assume a symmetric kneeling base; a half-kneeling version would sit somewhere between this and the standing case."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-pull: handle drawn toward the lower ribs with the elbow flared to roughly 60 degrees of humeral horizontal abduction, elbow flexed to ~110 degrees, before the scapula reaches full retraction and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 178.36, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.4820, "E_mech": 0.3199, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Largest single contributor and the defining feature of this variant: the 55 N-m rotational moment is the highest anti-rotation demand in the library, and without it the torso simply rotates toward the stack and no pulling work occurs. E_LoA is capped near 0.48 because the aggregate slug spans both sides and both layers, only half of which act in the required direction at any instant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.14}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.7840, "E_mech": 0.5400, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 20.6 N-m unilateral elbow moment at favourable mid-range leverage. Alignment reduced because the wide-elbow path angles the forearm away from the pull line.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.79}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7190, "E_mech": 0.4158, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps the entire 686 N passes through a single hand, giving the highest per-hand grip demand of any row in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.62}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7920, "E_mech": 0.5153, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Major contributor to the 62 N-m glenohumeral moment, but the wide-elbow path shifts the required action toward horizontal abduction and away from its optimal adduction and extension line, matching the pattern established by the wide-grip seated row.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.8690, "E_mech": 0.5851, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Near-optimal alignment: the wide-elbow path makes scapular retraction the dominant scapulothoracic demand, which is precisely its line of action.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.8910, "E_mech": 0.6000, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Its pure horizontal retraction vector closely matches the required scapulothoracic moment in the wide-elbow position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.71}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.9180, "E_mech": 0.5971, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "The wide-elbow path requires humeral horizontal abduction, which is its primary action, giving it strong task alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.72}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.2180, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: its deep rotatores and multifidus assist the anti-rotation demand directly, and the superficial fibres hold the unsupported upright torso against a pull with no seat back or foot brace.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.4520, "E_mech": 0.2450, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Penalised by the wide-elbow path which angles the forearm away from the pull line, and its shoulder-flexion action provides no assistance in a horizontal pull unlike in overhead pulling. The neutral handle spares it the pronation penalty of a wide barbell grip.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.75}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2240, "E_mech": 0.0400, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: 686 N of cable tension applies a distraction load to the humerus that cuff compression must resist for force to transmit into the scapula, with a small external-rotation component in the elbows-out position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.83}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6600, "E_mech": 0.3815, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists scapular elevation during the pull, though its depression vector only partly matches the predominantly horizontal scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.72}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.0824, "E_mech": 0.0250, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: its external-rotation and pelvic-stabilising action resists the pelvis being pulled into rotation on the narrow kneeling base. Its dominant extension function is irrelevant to the transverse-plane demand, hence the very low alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.58}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.4270, "E_mech": 0.2940, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Excellent moment arm at the 110 degree limiting angle and favoured by the neutral handle, but the wide-elbow forearm angle diverts much of its action from the pull direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.30}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.3140, "E_mech": 0.1102, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the hoop tension and intra-abdominal pressure that stiffen the trunk cylinder against the rotational moment, the same mechanism as the Pallof press at higher load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.42}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Read as unilateral-asymmetric following the Pallof press and side-facing reverse pec deck precedent: 70 kg is a single stack acting on one arm, not two mirrored 35 kg loads. Mirroring would cancel the anti-rotation demand that is central to the kneeling single-arm setup.", "Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and D-handle; 70 kg yields ~686 N at the handle after friction.", "Half-kneeling or tall-kneeling position facing a chest-height pulley, torso upright, with no chest pad, seat back or foot platform to brace against.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the mid-pull with the elbow flared wide, the cable line gives a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.181 m.", "Wide-elbow path shifts the required humeral action substantially toward horizontal abduction, favouring deltoid_posterior and the scapular retractors over the latissimus relative to a close-elbow row.", "The 686 N acting at ~0.08 m from the trunk axis generates a ~55 N-m rotational moment that must be actively resisted for the arm to have a stable base to pull from.", "Kneeling narrows the base of support relative to standing and removes the foot platform of a seated row, so the anti-rotation demand cannot be transmitted through braced legs and must be met at the trunk.", "Forearm angled inward with the wide elbow rather than aligned with the cable, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.056 m.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension ~0.058 m, elbow flexors ~0.042 m at 110 degrees, scapular retractors ~0.055 m, oblique rotation ~0.055 m.", "Neutral D-handle grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the full 686 N reaches the arm.", "70 kg on a single arm is a realistic 8-12 rep load precisely because the kneeling position limits how much the athlete can brace; a seated row with a foot platform would permit substantially more per arm."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 62.00}, {"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 55.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}, {"joint": "elbow_unilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 20.60}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 27.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 70.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 686.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 178.36, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 178.00}
