35	wide_pronated_grip_pull_up	Wide-Grip Pull-Up	Wide Pronated-Grip Pull-Up	Upper	Back_V	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	85.00	190.75	{"obliques": 4.09, "iliopsoas": 2.04, "rhomboids": 15.36, "brachialis": 28.86, "teres_major": 3.44, "rotator_cuffs": 10.42, "wrist_flexors": 29.13, "biceps_brachii": 15.13, "brachioradialis": 4.51, "trapezius_lower": 9.51, "latissimus_dorsi": 34.31, "rectus_abdominis": 5.15, "trapezius_middle": 15.19, "deltoid_posterior": 12.32, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 1.29}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "wide_pronated_grip_pull_up", "notes": ["This completes a three-point vertical pull comparison that isolates two variables cleanly. Against weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up at 245.71 cm2, removing 15 kg and switching to a wide pronated grip drops total demand to 190.75. Against neutral_grip_lat_pulldown at 191.51 cm2, the totals are nearly identical but the composition differs sharply: the pulldown allocates 24.32 cm2 to biceps against 15.13 here, while this exercise adds 11.28 cm2 of trunk and hip rigging that the thigh pads eliminate.", "Biceps_brachii falls to E_mech 0.3800, the lowest of any vertical pull in the library, against 0.6318 in the weighted neutral pull-up and 0.6108 in the neutral pulldown. Pronation is doing almost all of that work, with E_MA at 0.5500 against 0.7000 for neutral. Brachialis correspondingly takes a larger share, and the brachialis-to-biceps ratio rises to 1.91 against 1.34 in the neutral pull-up. This reproduces without special-casing the standard coaching claim that wide pronated pull-ups are the least arm-dependent vertical pull.", "Deltoid_posterior nearly doubles its share relative to neutral-grip pulls, reaching E_mech 0.4997 against 0.3470. The wide abducted arm path shifts the required humeral moment toward horizontal abduction, which is its line of action. Trapezius_middle rises for the same reason while trapezius_lower falls, since the wide grip trades scapular depression emphasis for retraction emphasis.", "Latissimus_dorsi barely moves across all three vertical pulls at 34.50, 35.83 and 34.31 cm2 despite effective loads ranging from 735 to 902 N. The muscle is close to its usable ceiling in every overhead pulling position, so load differences within this range are absorbed by the surrounding musculature rather than by the prime mover. This is a useful signature: exercises that saturate their prime mover discriminate poorly on load.", "The 85 kg input matched body mass exactly, so no interpretation was needed here. This contrasts with the weighted variant where 100 kg required assuming 85 plus 15 added. The ambiguity flagged there remains: a separate added_load_kg column would make bodyweight exercises unambiguous and is worth adding before dips and belt squats enter the library.", "Trunk and hip contribution totals 11.28 cm2 against 40.55 in the weighted variant, a much larger drop than the 15 percent load reduction alone implies. The mechanism is that a dip belt hangs and swings below the pelvis, creating a genuine dynamic moment, whereas unweighted bodyweight is distributed along the body axis and requires only modest bracing. Any future weighted bodyweight exercise should apply this distinction rather than scaling trunk demand with total load.", "Pronators_supinators and the leg-rigging muscles from the weighted variant were deliberately excluded here. A pronated grip on a fixed straight bar mechanically constrains forearm rotation, so no active rotational control is required, and without a belt load there is no swinging mass requiring the legs to be held as a rigid unit.", "A wide neutral-grip or wide supinated pull-up must not inherit these values, since grip orientation and width interact rather than adding independently. A narrow pronated chin-up would sit between this and the neutral variants on biceps allocation.", "An assisted or band-assisted pull-up must not inherit these values. Assistance reduces effective load non-linearly through the range, being greatest at the stretched bottom where this evaluation places the limiting phase."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the dead hang: humerus fully elevated and abducted overhead at roughly 165 degrees in the scapular-to-frontal plane, elbows near-extended, scapulae elevated and upwardly rotated, before the elbows drive down and out and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 190.75, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9690, "E_mech": 0.7824, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover, at maximal stretched length in the dead hang. Marginally below the neutral-grip variants because the abducted wide-grip position shifts part of the required moment out of its optimal adduction line, though the reduction is small since adduction remains dominant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.8950, "E_mech": 0.5355, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the entire 766 N passes through the grip with no anchoring. Pronated grip on a straight bar is less secure than parallel handles, partly offsetting the lighter load relative to the weighted neutral variant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9390, "E_mech": 0.6552, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor and entirely unaffected by pronation, which is why it takes a substantially larger share here than in any neutral-grip pull. Alignment slightly reduced because the inward forearm angle diverts part of its action from pure vertical pulling.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 28.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7320, "E_mech": 0.5438, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine task torque via downward rotation of the elevated scapula. The wide grip adds a retraction component that suits it slightly better than a narrow neutral grip, partly offsetting the lighter load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.36}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8240, "E_mech": 0.5802, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Higher alignment than in neutral-grip pulls because the wide abducted arm path introduces a real horizontal retraction component alongside the scapular depression.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8080, "E_mech": 0.3800, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Sharply penalised by full pronation, which reduces its flexion leverage by roughly a quarter. This is the defining difference from the neutral-grip variants and the mechanical reason wide pronated pull-ups are the least biceps-intensive vertical pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8250, "E_mech": 0.4997, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Substantially more engaged than in neutral-grip pulls: the wide abducted arm path shifts the required humeral moment toward horizontal abduction and extension, which is its line of action.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2990, "E_mech": 0.0610, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the dead hang applies the full 766 N as distraction to the fully elevated humeral head. The wide abducted position is a less congruent joint configuration than a neutral hang, which raises the compression requirement relative to the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8540, "E_mech": 0.6346, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Well aligned with the required scapular depression and maximally lengthened at the dead hang, though the wide grip's retraction emphasis dilutes its share relative to a neutral pull-up.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.51}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.5545, "E_mech": 0.2121, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: active trunk flexion torque holds the body rigid and prevents swing. Roughly half the weighted variant's demand because no belt load hangs and swings below the pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.15}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.6720, "E_mech": 0.4023, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Full pronation moves it away from its optimal mid-position orientation, cutting its contribution to roughly 60 percent of the neutral-grip value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.51}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.2180, "E_mech": 0.0500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: bilateral tension resists trunk extension and rotational drift so the body remains a rigid link. Half the weighted variant because there is no swinging external mass.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.09}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.7639, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral adduction and extension, maximally lengthened at the dead hang and well suited to the abducted starting position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.44}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0891, "E_mech": 0.0200, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element holding the pelvis and legs in a fixed position, at a quarter of the weighted variant's demand because no belt load creates a hanging moment below the pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.0661, "E_mech": 0.0242, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Retains a small humeral adduction component from the overhead dead hang, but the wide abducted grip position substantially reduces the alignment it had in a narrower neutral-grip pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.29}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["85 kg is interpreted as body mass alone with no added external load, matching the reference athlete exactly.", "The arms and forearms above the elbow are not lifted by the pulling musculature, so the moved mass is ~78 kg, giving ~766 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Wide pronated grip at approximately 1.5 times biacromial width on a straight fixed bar.", "The wide grip places the humerus in a more abducted position, moving the movement toward the frontal plane and shortening the effective humeral moment arm for adduction to ~0.174 m per side.", "The wide grip also reduces the vertical range of motion, so the athlete travels less distance but through a mechanically less favourable humeral path.", "Elbows near-extended at the limiting phase; with the wide grip the forearm is angled inward rather than vertical, reducing the elbow external moment arm to ~0.048 m per side.", "Pronated grip fixes the forearm in full pronation, which reduces biceps_brachii flexion leverage by roughly 20 to 25 percent relative to a neutral grip and eliminates any supination contribution.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder adduction ~0.058 m overhead, elbow flexors ~0.030 m at the near-extended elbow, scapular depressors and retractors ~0.055 m.", "Scapular depression and downward rotation from the elevated dead hang is genuine task torque against the full suspended body mass.", "No thigh pads and no added load: the trunk and hip musculature generate real but modest torque to hold the body rigid and prevent swing, substantially less than the weighted variant where a belt load hangs and swings below the pelvis.", "Grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 766 N reaches the arm, and the pronated position on a straight bar is a less secure grip than parallel handles.", "Bodyweight alone at 85 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced athlete of this mass in the wide pronated position, which is mechanically harder than a neutral grip."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 133.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 71.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 36.80}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 16.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 85.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 766.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 190.75, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 190.50}
