8	dead_hang	Dead Hang	Pronated-Grip Bar Dead Hang	Upper	Forearms	Stability_Isometric	Bodyweight	85.00	41.01	{"wrist_flexors": 41.01}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "dead_hang", "notes": ["The modeled wrist-flexor contribution represents approximately 1025 N of tendon-force capacity at the reference specific tension and produces about 16.7 Nm through a representative aggregate finger-flexor moment arm.", "The bodyweight traction transmitted through the elbows and shoulders is primarily supported by skeletal alignment, connective tissues, and passive shoulder elevation in a true dead hang; it is therefore not converted into active latissimus or scapular-muscle demand.", "Rotator cuffs are excluded because humeral-head stabilization does not generate a meaningful net moment opposing the vertical bodyweight load in the assumed passive shoulder position.", "Wrist extensors and pronators-supinators are excluded because any co-contraction or maintenance of forearm orientation does not provide a meaningful share of the required net gripping moment.", "Grip demand is sensitive to bar diameter, bar surface, chalk use, thumb position, hand size, and whether the athlete uses an open, closed, or hook-style grip."], "limiting_phase": "Static full-body suspension from a pronated bar grip, with the fingers maintaining sufficient flexion force to prevent the bar from rolling out of the hands.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 41.01, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9400, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9900, "E_mech": 0.7538, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "The aggregate includes the principal finger and wrist flexors that generate the sustained gripping force required to prevent the loaded bar from opening the hands.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 41.01}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 85 kg nominal load represents the athlete's full body mass suspended from both hands.", "Each hand supports approximately half of the bodyweight under symmetric loading.", "A standard straight bar of approximately 28-32 mm diameter is assumed.", "The effective external moment arm from the distributed bar contact force to the finger-flexion axes is approximately 0.020 m.", "The shoulders are allowed to elevate in a passive dead-hang position rather than being actively depressed into an active hang."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "finger_flexion_grip_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 16.68}], "nominal_load_kg": 85.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 833.85}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 41.01, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 41.01}
42	bodyweight_neck_extension	Neck Extension	Prone Bench Bodyweight Neck Extension	Upper	Neck	Isolation	Bodyweight	5.00	8.49	{"deep_neck_extensors": 8.49}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_neck_extension", "notes": ["This exercise inverts the usual E_mech interpretation. The four dimensional factors all sit in the 0.85 to 0.95 range, but their product would allocate 60.5 cm2 against an external demand of only 8.5 cm2. E_mech was therefore set by the top-down requirement rather than by the dimensional product, since a muscle cannot utilise capacity the task does not demand.", "The E_mech multiplication rule as written assumes the muscle is the constraint. Where FCSA capacity greatly exceeds task demand, the product overstates utilisation by an order of magnitude. Consider adding an explicit rule to the prompt: after computing the dimensional product, cap each muscle's contribution so the group total satisfies but does not exceed the top-down target.", "The result is near-identical to bodyweight_neck_curl at 8.55 cm2, which is mechanically correct. Both movements lift the same head mass through the same external moment arm, so external demand is symmetric even though the available musculature is not.", "The two neck exercises reach the same total by opposite routes. Flexion is capacity-constrained, with sternocleidomastoid at E_mech 0.6156 and the deep flexors missing from MUSCLE_FCSA entirely; extension is demand-constrained, with a large muscle group only lightly utilised. Any future model that reads E_mech as a proxy for training stimulus will misread this pair.", "Trapezius upper was deliberately excluded. Its dominant action is scapular elevation, and its small cervical extension component is redundant at a load the deep extensors cover using a tenth of their capacity.", "A weighted variant with a head harness must not inherit these values. The deep extensors have roughly ten times the reserve needed here, so demand scales close to linearly with added load before any saturation appears."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range with the head near the horizontal, where the head's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the cervical extension axis; the moment falls away as the head rises toward the neutral or extended end position.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 8.49, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "rationale": "Sole prime mover for the 5.9 N-m cervical extension moment, at favourable length and leverage in the mid-range. E_mech is low not because of any mechanical disadvantage but because the group's 84.88 cm2 capacity vastly exceeds the requirement: it can generate roughly 95 N-m at full capacity against a 5.9 N-m demand, so only about a tenth of its available force is utilised.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.49}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["5.00 kg is interpreted as head mass alone, roughly 5.9 percent of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Prone on a bench with the head and neck extending past the end, shoulders and thorax supported by the bench surface.", "Horizontal distance from the upper cervical extension axis to the head's centre of mass ~0.12 m at the limiting phase, mirroring the supine neck curl geometry.", "Deep neck extensor effective cervical extension moment arm ~0.045 m, larger than the sternocleidomastoid flexion moment arm because the posterior musculature acts further from the vertebral bodies via the spinous processes.", "Trapezius upper is excluded from the cervical moment: its line of pull produces scapular elevation and, at the cervical spine, acts mainly on the skull via the nuchal ligament with a small extension component that the deep extensors already cover at this trivial load.", "The bench supports the trunk, so no thoracolumbar extension moment is required and no hip or trunk musculature bears load.", "Slow controlled tempo with negligible acceleration beyond gravity."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 5.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 49.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 8.49, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 8.50}
13	cable_face_pull	Face Pull	Rope Cable Face Pull	Upper	Back_3D	Isolation	Cable	30.00	46.53	{"rhomboids": 6.61, "brachialis": 3.86, "rotator_cuffs": 9.09, "biceps_brachii": 2.54, "trapezius_lower": 3.13, "trapezius_middle": 8.31, "deltoid_posterior": 12.99}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "cable_face_pull", "notes": ["This is the first exercise in the library where rotator_cuffs enters as a prime mover rather than a stabiliser. End-range external rotation is an explicit task requirement here, not a by-product of load transmission, so the inclusion rationale differs fundamentally from the press and row entries where cuff force merely compresses the joint.", "The rotator_cuffs slug is mechanically heterogeneous and this exercise exposes the problem. Infraspinatus and teres minor are agonists, supraspinatus is roughly neutral, and subscapularis is a direct antagonist of the required external rotation. The 170.80 cm2 aggregate was handled by setting E_LoA to approximately 0.17, but a split into external and internal rotator slugs would model this and any rotator-cuff-specific exercise far more cleanly.", "Total demand of 46.53 cm2 is low relative to the 30 kg stack number because the very long end-range external moment arm, around 0.225 m at the shoulder, converts modest cable force into a demanding joint moment. This is the mechanically correct reason face pulls are performed with light loads, and the model reproduces it without special-casing.", "As with the cable fly, the elbow moment here is an extension moment, so the elbow flexors appear as load-transmitting stabilisers rather than agonists. This is now the second cable exercise showing that pattern and is likely general to any cable movement where the forearm is roughly perpendicular to the cable line.", "The exercise loads four joints simultaneously at modest magnitudes rather than one joint heavily, which is why no muscle approaches saturation and no muscle exceeds E_mech 0.53. Contrast the Pendlay row, where three groups saturated.", "A high-to-low or seated face pull variant must not inherit these values. Changing the pulley height alters the balance between the horizontal abduction and external rotation moments and shifts the relative posterior deltoid and cuff allocation.", "Trunk and lower-limb musculature were deliberately excluded. The backward lean transmits the cable reaction through the skeleton to the floor and is postural rather than net task torque under the stated inclusion rules."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric end-range: rope split at the level of the face, humerus horizontally abducted to roughly 90 degrees and externally rotated, scapulae approaching full retraction, where the shoulder external rotation and horizontal abduction moments are simultaneously greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 46.53, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8600, "E_mech": 0.5267, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 33.1 N-m horizontal abduction moment. Shortened at end-range, which costs length-tension, and part of its line of action is pure extension rather than the horizontal abduction plus external rotation the movement requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.99}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.1739, "E_mech": 0.0532, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a genuine prime mover rather than a stabiliser: infraspinatus and teres minor generate the required 12.6 N-m external rotation moment against a small internal moment arm. E_LoA is low because only the two external rotators of the four-muscle group act in the required direction, and subscapularis is a direct antagonist here.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.5185, "E_mech": 0.3173, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Direct horizontal retractor supplying a large share of the 22 N-m scapulothoracic moment, shortened at end-range retraction. E_LoA reflects that the scapular moment is a secondary component of the total task demand rather than the primary one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.4078, "E_mech": 0.2340, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Shares scapular retraction with middle trapezius, but its downward-rotation component partly opposes the upward rotation required by the elbows-high finish position, reducing net alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.61}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1733, "E_mech": 0.0876, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: the rope creates an elbow extension moment of 11.8 N-m that must be resisted for hand force to reach the humerus. Excellent leverage at 90 degrees, but the demand is small relative to available capacity and the work is largely isometric.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.86}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.3616, "E_mech": 0.2090, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and the upward rotation that permits the elbows-high finish, though its depression vector only partly matches the required scapular moment direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.13}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1430, "E_mech": 0.0638, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow hold alongside brachialis. The neutral rope grip and the supination demand at end-range give it a modest role, but the elbow moment is the smallest of the four loaded joints.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.54}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and rope attachment; 30 kg nominal yields ~147 N at the rope after friction, split ~73.5 N per hand.", "High pulley set slightly above head height, athlete standing with a staggered stance in a light backward lean, elbows tracking at or above shoulder height.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at end-range horizontal abduction the glenohumeral external moment arm for the horizontal component is ~0.225 m per side.", "External rotation moment estimated from the forearm acting as the lever: rope force ~73.5 N at ~0.086 m effective rotation moment arm per side.", "Elbow held at roughly 90 degrees; the rope line is close to perpendicular to the forearm, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.080 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: posterior deltoid horizontal abduction ~0.045 m, infraspinatus and teres minor external rotation ~0.021 m, scapular retractors ~0.055 m, elbow flexors ~0.040 m at 90 degrees.", "The face pull is the one horizontal-pull pattern in which the rotator cuff is a genuine prime mover rather than a stabiliser, because end-range external rotation is an explicit requirement of the movement rather than a by-product.", "The athlete counterbalances the cable by leaning back against it, so lower-limb and trunk musculature transmit body weight to the floor rather than generating task torque; this is postural and excluded.", "30 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very long external moment arm at end-range makes this a low-load exercise relative to its stack number."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 33.10}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 22.00}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_rotation_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 12.60}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 11.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 30.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 147.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 46.53, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 46.50}
37	straight_arm_cable_pulldown	Straight-Arm Pulldown	Standing Straight-Arm Cable Pulldown	Upper	Back_V	Isolation	Cable	40.00	105.31	{"obliques": 1.61, "rhomboids": 4.72, "teres_major": 3.53, "rotator_cuffs": 9.60, "wrist_flexors": 3.81, "trapezius_lower": 5.72, "latissimus_dorsi": 33.03, "rectus_abdominis": 6.13, "deltoid_posterior": 13.55, "triceps_long_head": 15.30, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 8.31}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "straight_arm_cable_pulldown", "notes": ["This is the only single-joint shoulder extension exercise in the library and it isolates the lat contribution more cleanly than any compound pull. Latissimus reaches 33.03 cm2, within 7 percent of the weighted pull-up at 35.83, but total demand is 105.31 against 245.71. The lat share rises from 15 percent of total to 31 percent, which is the mechanical justification for programming straight-arm pulldowns as a lat isolation movement.", "The elbow flexors are almost entirely absent, which is the defining structural difference from every other pull in the library. Brachialis contributes 27 to 34 cm2 in the compound pulls and zero here, because the fixed near-straight elbow means the cable line runs roughly along the forearm axis rather than perpendicular to it. This is also why the exercise permits only 40 kg despite loading the same prime mover as an 80 kg seated row.", "Triceps_long_head appears as a shoulder extensor rather than an elbow extensor, its first such appearance in the library. In the overhead triceps extension it contributed 37.94 cm2 as an elbow extensor with the shoulder held static; here the roles invert. The muscle is credited on E_LoA 0.8800 for humeral extension specifically, and this dual-function handling is worth noting as a pattern any future straight-arm movement should follow.", "Teres_major reaches E_mech 0.7846, its highest value anywhere, because pure humeral extension is precisely its function with no competing demand. Its 4.50 cm2 maximum FCSA keeps the absolute contribution small, but the engagement factor is a clean signal that the model is reading joint action correctly rather than defaulting to load-proportional allocation.", "Pectoralis_major_sternal at 8.31 cm2 is its largest contribution in any pulling exercise, more than double the pull-up value. From full overhead elevation the sternal fibres genuinely extend the humerus toward the torso, and the straight-arm lever amplifies that contribution through the upper range. It would be excluded outright in any row where the humerus never elevates above shoulder height.", "Total demand of 105.31 cm2 places this almost exactly alongside the low-to-high cable fly at 105.44, and the parallel is structural rather than coincidental. Both are standing single-joint cable movements with a near-straight arm, a very long external moment arm, and a modest stack load. The pattern is now consistent enough that any straight-arm cable exercise should be expected to land in the 95 to 115 cm2 range at typical working loads.", "Wrist_flexors at 3.81 cm2 is the lowest grip contribution among all exercises where grip was included at all, against 34.03 in the weighted pull-up. The cable pulls along the arm axis rather than trying to open the hand, so grip is a minor transmission requirement rather than a bottleneck. Straps would change nothing material here.", "A bench-supported or kneeling straight-arm pulldown must not inherit these values. Removing the standing counterbalance requirement would eliminate the rectus abdominis and oblique contribution of 7.74 cm2 and permit a modestly heavier load.", "A straight-arm pullover on a bench or with a dumbbell must not inherit these values either. Gravity acting vertically produces a resistance curve that peaks at a different point in the range than a cable pulling from a fixed high anchor.", "A bent-arm variant performed with 90 degrees of elbow flexion must not inherit these values under any circumstances. That version halves the external moment arm, permits roughly double the load, and reintroduces the elbow flexors as major contributors, effectively converting it into a different exercise."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the overhead stretched position: humerus flexed to roughly 150 degrees with the arms near-straight and reaching forward and up, before the arms sweep down to the thighs and the external moment arm collapses toward zero.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 105.31, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9840, "E_mech": 0.7532, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover and the defining muscle of this exercise: maximally lengthened at the overhead start and acting in pure shoulder extension, its primary function, with no competing elbow demand to divert the movement. Slightly below the pull-up value because the single-joint pattern limits how much total force the shoulder can express without elbow assistance.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.3513, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Included as a genuine contributor rather than a stabiliser: it crosses the shoulder and produces humeral extension directly, which is the required joint action, while simultaneously holding the elbow near-extended. Stretched at the overhead start, though its shoulder moment arm is well below the latissimus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.30}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8510, "E_mech": 0.5497, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Strong contributor to sagittal-plane shoulder extension, well lengthened overhead. Alignment is high but not perfect because part of its action is horizontal abduction, which the narrow sagittal arm path does not require.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2755, "E_mech": 0.0562, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the very long straight-arm lever places the humeral head under a large distracting moment at full elevation, requiring substantial cuff compression to transmit load into the scapula. Teres minor and infraspinatus add a small extension component.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3115, "E_mech": 0.1554, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Genuine agonist here rather than an incidental contributor: from full overhead elevation its fibres produce humeral extension toward the torso through the upper portion of the range, though the action fades as the arm passes the vertical.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.6000, "E_mech": 0.2525, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the high cable pulls the torso upward and into extension, and active trunk flexion torque is required to hold the hinged position that the shoulder moment is generated from.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.6280, "E_mech": 0.3818, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes scapular depression as the arms sweep down from the elevated overhead start, though the scapulothoracic moment is small relative to the glenohumeral demand in a straight-arm movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.72}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.3095, "E_mech": 0.1671, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Assists downward rotation of the scapula from the elevated position, but its retraction vector is largely irrelevant to a sagittal-plane arm sweep.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.72}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2060, "E_mech": 0.0700, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element, but at only 196 N total and with the cable line running roughly along the arm axis rather than pulling the bar out of the hands, grip demand is a small fraction of what compound pulls require.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.81}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7846, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on pure humeral extension, its exact function, and is maximally lengthened at the overhead start. Highest E_mech it receives in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.0860, "E_mech": 0.0197, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to trunk rigidity against the upward cable pull, assisting rectus abdominis in maintaining the hinged position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.61}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and straight bar or rope attachment; 40 kg nominal yields ~196 N at the handle after friction, ~98 N per hand.", "High pulley set above head height, athlete standing at arm's length with a slight forward hip hinge of ~20 degrees and a fixed near-straight elbow of ~165 degrees.", "Single-joint shoulder movement: the near-straight arm gives a very large external moment arm, ~0.55 m from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line at the overhead limiting phase.", "The required humeral action is pure shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, sweeping the straight arm from overhead down to the thigh.", "Latissimus shoulder extension internal moment arm ~0.058 m at the overhead position.", "The cable force acts roughly along the forearm long axis rather than perpendicular to it, so the elbow moment is small and the elbow flexors are not meaningfully loaded; the triceps hold the elbow near-extended against a modest flexion tendency.", "The exercise is a genuine single-joint isolation of shoulder extension, which is unique among the pulling movements in the library and the reason the elbow flexors are almost entirely absent.", "Standing unsupported with a forward lean against a high cable: a real lumbar extension moment is required to hold the hinged torso position against the upward and forward pull.", "The athlete counterbalances the cable by leaning back and bracing through the legs; the reaction transmits through the skeleton to the floor, so lower-limb musculature is postural and excluded.", "40 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very long straight-arm moment arm converts modest cable force into a demanding shoulder moment, so the stack number substantially overstates the achievable load relative to compound pulls."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 108.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 24.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 41.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 9.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 40.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 196.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 105.31, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 105.00}
