48	bodyweight_crunch	Crunch	Floor Bodyweight Abdominal Crunch	Core	Core	Isolation	Bodyweight	22.00	36.40	{"obliques": 9.71, "rectus_abdominis": 20.77, "sternocleidomastoid": 5.92}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_crunch", "notes": ["Rectus abdominis saturates: it supplies roughly 36 N-m of the 47.5 N-m requirement at near-maximal E_mech, so the obliques were allocated the residual moment rather than the rectus being scaled beyond capacity.", "Iliopsoas was deliberately excluded. With knees flexed and feet unanchored the pelvis stays grounded and no hip flexion moment is required, so the hip flexors produce no net task torque.", "Transverse abdominis was deliberately excluded. It generates intra-abdominal pressure but no spinal flexion moment, and unlike a loaded hinge there is no saturated extensor moment here for IAP to offload.", "A sit-up or anchored-feet variant must not inherit these values. In those versions iliopsoas becomes a large prime mover and total demand roughly doubles.", "At 36.40 cm2 this exercise serves as a useful low-end calibration point, about 6 percent of the barbell Pendlay row and 23 percent of the flat barbell bench press."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation: shoulder blades just clearing the floor with the trunk still near-horizontal, where the moved segment's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the thoracolumbar flexion axis.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 36.40, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 1.0000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8550, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 47.5 N-m spinal flexion moment, slightly lengthened at the flat starting position and acting directly along the required joint-moment direction. Effectively saturated: it supplies roughly 36 N-m of the requirement, close to its full mechanical capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 20.77}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.4000, "E_mech": 0.1188, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Supplies the residual flexion moment the rectus cannot cover, but bilateral contraction cancels the rotation and lateral-flexion components that dominate its line of action, and its flexion moment arm is smaller than the rectus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.71}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.4284, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "rationale": "Genuine task torque rather than stabilization: the head is part of the 22 kg moved mass, and its ~5 N-m cervical flexion moment must be actively generated for the segment to be lifted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.92}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["22 kg is interpreted as the moved segment mass (head, both arms, and upper trunk above the flexion axis), equal to ~26% of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Supine on the floor, knees flexed ~90 degrees with feet unanchored, hands at the temples: the classic crunch restricts motion to spinal flexion and holds the pelvis on the floor.", "Horizontal distance from the thoracolumbar flexion axis to the moved segment's centre of mass ~0.22 m at the limiting phase, shortening rapidly as the trunk curls.", "Rectus abdominis internal flexion moment arm ~0.070 m; oblique effective flexion moment arm ~0.045 m.", "Head mass ~5 kg at ~0.10 m from the cervical flexion axis; sternocleidomastoid internal moment arm ~0.032 m.", "The pelvis is held down by gravity and the flexed-knee position, so no hip-flexion moment is required to complete the movement.", "Short range of motion with no external resistance: the limiting phase is reached almost immediately after the shoulders leave the floor."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.50}, {"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 22.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 216.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 36.40, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 36.00}
12	chest_supported_dumbbell_row	Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row	Incline Bench Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row	Upper	Back_3D	Secondary_Compound	Dumbbell	60.00	175.19	{"rhomboids": 17.79, "brachialis": 30.02, "teres_major": 0.97, "rotator_cuffs": 7.32, "wrist_flexors": 24.09, "biceps_brachii": 19.24, "brachioradialis": 5.32, "trapezius_lower": 6.53, "latissimus_dorsi": 32.19, "trapezius_middle": 16.49, "deltoid_posterior": 15.23}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "chest_supported_dumbbell_row", "notes": ["This exercise is the cleanest available isolation of the upper-back pulling demand from the Pendlay row, and the comparison validates the model. Total demand falls from 579.05 to 175.19 cm2, a 70 percent reduction, entirely because the chest support eliminates the hip and lumbar moments. The upper-body allocation itself is nearly proportional to load: latissimus contributes 32.19 against 33.64 cm2 at two thirds the weight, since the shorter effective moment arm from the more upright bench angle partly offsets the lighter dumbbells.", "Gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, the hamstrings, and erector spinae were all deliberately excluded, having contributed a combined 347.45 cm2 to the Pendlay row. The chest pad and bench carry both the trunk mass and the reaction force from the pull, so these muscles generate no net task torque under the stated inclusion rules.", "Obliques and transverse abdominis were likewise excluded. There is no saturated extensor moment for intra-abdominal pressure to offload here, so the load-transmission rationale that admitted them in the Pendlay row and pull-through does not apply.", "No muscle saturates and none exceeds E_mech 0.74, in contrast to the Pendlay row where erector spinae, gluteus maximus, and the shoulder extensors all hit their ceilings. The bottleneck in a chest-supported row is genuinely the pulling musculature rather than trunk capacity, which is the mechanical basis for programming it when upper-back demand should be isolated.", "Elbow flexor demand at 54.58 cm2 combined is a large share of the total, and the neutral dumbbell grip raises the biceps and brachioradialis allocation relative to a pronated barbell row. E_mech for biceps_brachii rises from 0.5148 to 0.4833 in absolute terms only because the load is lighter; the grip advantage is visible in the ratio to brachialis, which improves from 0.71 to 0.64.", "A true single-arm chest-supported row must not inherit these values. The bilateral convention cancels the anti-rotation demand that a unilateral version imposes on the obliques, even with the chest supported.", "A flatter bench angle, closer to 15 degrees, would lengthen the effective humeral moment arm and raise glenohumeral demand while further reducing the achievable load; a near-vertical bench would shift the pattern toward a seated row. Neither variant should inherit these values."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-pull: dumbbells roughly level with the torso, elbow flexed to ~110 degrees, humerus extended ~40 degrees from vertical, before the scapulae reach full retraction and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 175.19, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9570, "E_mech": 0.7341, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 111 N-m glenohumeral extension moment, well lengthened at the bottom of the stroke, with the required humeral action closely matching its line of pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9375, "E_mech": 0.6815, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest elbow-flexion contributor and unaffected by forearm orientation, making it the dominant flexor against the 38.4 N-m elbow moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 30.02}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7663, "E_mech": 0.4428, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps, finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the 600 N reaches the arm. Demand is lower than the Pendlay row in proportion to the reduced load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 24.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8362, "E_mech": 0.4833, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Meaningful elbow flexor, favoured by the neutral grip relative to a pronated barbell row, though some capacity is diverted to controlling forearm rotation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.24}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9147, "E_mech": 0.6298, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Scapular retraction is genuine task torque: the scapula must be actively drawn back against a load pulling it into protraction, and the chest pad does not block that motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9147, "E_mech": 0.6298, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Primary horizontal scapular retractor, directly aligned with the required scapulothoracic moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.49}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8974, "E_mech": 0.6179, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Contributes shoulder extension and horizontal abduction as the elbow travels back; strongly aligned with the required humeral moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.23}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2800, "E_mech": 0.0428, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the hanging dumbbells apply a distraction load to the humerus that cuff compression must resist for force to transmit into the scapula. Engagement is below the Pendlay row because the load is lighter and the supported torso removes trunk-driven perturbation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7539, "E_mech": 0.4358, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists the downward pull on the scapula in the inclined torso position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.7745, "E_mech": 0.4740, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Effective elbow flexor at the ~110 degree limiting angle, and the neutral grip places it near its optimal mechanical position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.2965, "E_mech": 0.2155, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on the humeral extension moment; small cross-section limits absolute contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 0.97}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 60 kg total is interpreted as 30 kg per dumbbell.", "Barbell-equivalent weight 588 N plus ~2 percent for controlled acceleration: ~600 N effective, ~300 N per limb.", "Incline bench set to ~30 degrees from horizontal with the chest and abdomen fully supported; the dumbbells hang and travel close to vertical.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at 40 degrees of extension the glenohumeral external moment arm is ~0.185 m per side.", "Forearms near-vertical at the limiting phase, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.064 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension ~0.06 m, elbow flexors ~0.042 m at 110 degrees, scapular retractors ~0.055 m.", "Neutral or semi-pronated dumbbell grip without straps: grip force is mechanically required to transmit the full 600 N.", "Chest support removes all hip, lumbar, and lower-limb moment requirements; the bench carries the trunk mass and the reaction force from the pull, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque.", "The pad restricts torso movement but does not restrict scapular retraction against the load, so scapulothoracic torque remains genuine task demand.", "60 kg total on a chest-supported row is a realistic 8-12 rep working load precisely because the eliminated trunk demand allows the upper-back musculature to be the limiter."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 111.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 42.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.40}], "nominal_load_kg": 60.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 600.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 175.19, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 175.00}
43	incline_dumbbell_curl	Incline Dumbbell Curl	Seated Incline Bench Dumbbell Curl	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Dumbbell	28.00	76.66	{"brachialis": 32.65, "biceps_brachii": 29.31, "brachioradialis": 6.90, "deltoid_anterior": 2.69, "pronators_supinators": 5.11}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "incline_dumbbell_curl", "notes": ["This is the direct incline counterpart to the preacher curl calibration example in the prompt, at the same 28 kg bilateral load, and the contrast is instructive. The preacher example places biceps_brachii in the 0.50 to 0.85 band with a note that shoulder position penalises it; the incline position does the opposite, holding the shoulder in extension so the long head sits at a favourable stretched length. E_mech of 0.7361 sits near the top of that band for exactly that reason.", "The result closely tracks the prompt's stated pattern for the preacher curl, with brachialis at 0.7413 within the 0.65 to 0.90 band and brachioradialis at 0.6148 above the stated 0.15 to 0.45 range. The brachioradialis excess is deliberate: that band assumes a preacher bench where the limiting angle occurs in deeper flexion, whereas the 90 degree limiting angle here is precisely where its moment arm peaks.", "Pronators_supinators enters for the first time in the library, as a load-transmitting element rather than an agonist. A dumbbell's mass is offset from the forearm axis and applies a continuous pronation moment; resisting it is mechanically required for the supinated position the biceps leverage depends on. This rationale is specific to dumbbells and should not carry over to barbell or EZ-bar curls where the implement is symmetric about the grip.", "Deltoid_anterior is included on stabiliser grounds under the explicit exception in the inclusion rules, since it transmits a real component of the external load by holding the humerus against 11 N-m of extension moment. Its 2.69 cm2 is small enough that omitting it would not materially change the total, and it should be dropped if the model later tightens the stabiliser criterion.", "Total demand of 76.66 cm2 places this between the bodyweight crunch and the face pull. The elbow flexors as a group reach 68.86 cm2 against a combined maximum FCSA of 95.08, so utilisation is high without saturation, which is the expected signature of a well-loaded single-joint exercise.", "A standing dumbbell curl must not inherit these values. Removing the incline shortens the biceps long head at the shoulder, lowering its E_LT, and permits a heavier load plus some torso contribution.", "A neutral-grip or hammer variant on the same bench must not inherit these values either. Neutral grip raises brachioradialis substantially, lowers biceps_brachii leverage, and largely removes the pronators_supinators demand."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 90 degrees of elbow flexion, where the forearm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the elbow axis to the dumbbell is at its maximum.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 76.66, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9130, "E_mech": 0.7413, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest single contributor to the 43.2 N-m elbow flexion moment and the only major flexor unaffected by shoulder position or forearm orientation, operating near-optimal length and leverage at the 90 degree limiting angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.65}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.7361, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "The incline position places the long head at a favourable stretched length across the shoulder, which is the defining mechanical feature of this variant, and the supinated grip gives near-optimal alignment. Slightly below brachialis because part of its capacity is spent maintaining supination and because active insufficiency limits it toward the top of the range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8930, "E_mech": 0.6148, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Well positioned at the 90 degree limiting angle where its moment arm is largest, though the fully supinated grip is less favourable for it than a neutral one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.90}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2820, "E_mech": 0.0839, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the supinator and pronator teres must actively resist the pronation moment the offset dumbbell applies, since maintaining supination is required for the biceps to act at its modelled leverage. Almost all of the capacity is non-propulsive.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.11}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3365, "E_mech": 0.0883, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it resists the 11 N-m moment that would otherwise drag the humerus further into extension, holding the upper arm in the fixed position the exercise geometry depends on. Shortened and poorly positioned in shoulder extension, so utilisation is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.69}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 28 kg total is interpreted as 14 kg per dumbbell.", "Dumbbell weight 275 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~279 N effective, ~140 N per limb.", "Bench set to ~60 degrees from horizontal, seated with the back supported and the upper arms hanging behind the torso line at roughly 20 to 30 degrees of shoulder extension.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to dumbbell centre ~0.155 m; at the horizontal-forearm limiting phase the full length acts as the external moment arm.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.042 m at 90 degrees, close to maximal for this joint.", "Supinated grip held throughout, placing biceps_brachii in its most mechanically favourable forearm orientation.", "The incline position holds the shoulder in extension, which lengthens the biceps long head across the shoulder and reduces the range over which it can shorten actively.", "The upper arm hangs approximately vertical, so the glenohumeral external moment is small and arises mainly from holding the humerus against the dumbbell rather than from any required shoulder motion.", "The bench supports the torso fully, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque and no cheat-swing contribution is available.", "28 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for an advanced 85 kg athlete in the incline position, which permits less weight than a standing curl."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 43.20}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 11.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 28.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 279.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 76.66, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 76.50}
10	weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up	Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up	Weighted Neutral-Grip Pull-Up	Upper	Back_V	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	100.00	245.71	{"obliques": 8.17, "iliopsoas": 8.18, "rhomboids": 16.10, "brachialis": 33.63, "teres_major": 3.60, "rotator_cuffs": 11.10, "wrist_flexors": 34.03, "biceps_brachii": 25.14, "rectus_femoris": 3.66, "brachioradialis": 7.56, "trapezius_lower": 11.09, "latissimus_dorsi": 35.83, "rectus_abdominis": 10.44, "trapezius_middle": 14.92, "deltoid_posterior": 8.55, "pronators_supinators": 3.04, "transverse_abdominis": 2.86, "adductor_longus_brevis": 4.24, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 3.57}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up", "notes": ["This is the direct free-weight counterpart to neutral_grip_lat_pulldown and the pair isolates the cost of removing the anchor. Total demand rises from 191.51 to 245.71 cm2, a 28 percent increase for a 33 percent heavier effective load. The upper-body allocation scales almost exactly with load, with latissimus moving 34.50 to 35.83 and trapezius_lower 10.87 to 11.09, while the entire 40.55 cm2 of trunk and hip contribution is new. That block is the mechanical price of the missing thigh pads, and it is the clearest quantification the model has produced of why pull-ups and pulldowns are not interchangeable.", "Nine muscles enter here that do not appear in the pulldown: rectus_abdominis, obliques, transverse_abdominis, iliopsoas, rectus_femoris, adductor_longus_brevis, pronators_supinators, and the trunk group generally. All are admitted under the load-transmission exception rather than as agonists, because the dip belt hangs freely below the pelvis and the body must be held rigid for force to reach the bar. A strict-form unweighted pull-up would admit these at roughly half the magnitude; a kipping pull-up would not admit them at all in this form, since the swing is deliberate.", "Wrist_flexors at 34.03 cm2 and E_mech 0.6256 is the highest grip demand in the library, above the pulldown at 27.75 and the Pendlay row at 31.44. The full 902 N passes through the hands with no anchoring, and this is the mechanical basis for grip being the common failure point on weighted pull-ups. With straps, total demand would fall to roughly 212 cm2 and the limiter would shift decisively to the lats and elbow flexors.", "Biceps_brachii reaches E_mech 0.6318, the highest value in any pulling exercise in the library, marginally above the neutral-grip pulldown at 0.6108. The same two mechanisms apply, neutral grip plus overhead shoulder position, amplified by the heavier load. The model reproduces without special-casing the common observation that neutral and supinated pull-ups are the most biceps-intensive vertical pulls.", "Rotator_cuffs at 11.10 cm2 is its highest stabiliser contribution in the library, exceeding the Pendlay row at 12.20 only slightly on E_mech terms at 0.0650 against 0.0714. The dead hang applies the entire bodyweight-plus-load as glenohumeral distraction at full humeral elevation, which is the most demanding joint-transmission scenario evaluated so far.", "The 100 kg input required interpretation. Under the bodyweight convention it was read as 85 kg body mass plus 15 kg added, with the arms above the elbow subtracted from the moved mass to give 92 kg effective. If the intended reading was 100 kg of added load on top of bodyweight, total demand would be roughly 420 cm2 and would exceed any exercise in the library, which would be implausible for an 8-12 rep set at this athlete's strength level. Consider a separate added_load_kg column for bodyweight exercises to remove this ambiguity, since it will recur for dips, weighted chin-ups, and belt squats.", "Rectus_femoris and adductor_longus_brevis appear for the first time, both purely as rigging. Their inclusion is defensible under the stated exception but they are the most marginal entries in the library at 3.66 and 4.24 cm2, and both should be dropped if the stabiliser criterion is later tightened.", "A wide pronated pull-up must not inherit these values. Pronation cuts biceps leverage by roughly 20 percent, the wider grip shortens the effective humeral moment arm, and the emphasis shifts from adduction toward horizontal abduction.", "An unweighted pull-up must not inherit these values either. Removing the belt eliminates the swinging mass that admits most of the trunk and hip contribution, so total demand would fall by more than the 15 percent load reduction alone would suggest."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the dead hang: humerus fully elevated overhead at roughly 165 degrees, elbows near-extended, scapulae elevated and upwardly rotated, before the elbows drive down and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 245.71, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9800, "E_LoA": 0.9810, "E_mech": 0.8171, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 168 N-m glenohumeral adduction and extension moment, at maximal stretched length in the dead hang. Highest engagement it receives in the library, marginally above the pulldown because the heavier effective load pushes it closer to full utilisation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 35.83}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6256, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element and near-saturated: the entire 902 N passes through the grip with no anchoring or straps, making this the highest grip demand in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7635, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 50 N-m elbow moment, lengthened at the near-extended dead hang and unaffected by forearm orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.63}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6318, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Highest engagement of any pulling exercise in the library: the neutral grip removes the pronation penalty and the overhead dead hang means its shoulder-extension action assists the required humeral moment rather than being irrelevant to it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.14}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7670, "E_mech": 0.5698, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine task torque: its downward-rotation action matches the scapular motion required as the elevated scapula is pulled down from the dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8095, "E_mech": 0.5700, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Contributes to the 79 N-m scapulothoracic moment, though its horizontal retraction vector only partly matches the predominantly downward scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.92}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.3185, "E_mech": 0.0650, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the dead hang applies the full 902 N as a distraction load to the fully elevated humeral head, the largest glenohumeral distraction in the library, requiring substantial cuff compression to transmit force into the scapula.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9970, "E_mech": 0.7406, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Best-aligned scapular muscle: its depression vector matches the required downward scapular moment directly, and it is maximally lengthened by the elevated dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.8640, "E_mech": 0.4297, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: with no thigh pads, active trunk flexion torque is required to hold the body rigid against the swinging belt load and to resist the extension moment created by the suspended lower body.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.5730, "E_mech": 0.3470, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Assists humeral extension from the overhead position, but its horizontal abduction vector contributes little to the predominantly adduction-direction moment of a neutral-grip pull-up.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3175, "E_mech": 0.0800, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: it holds the pelvis and legs in a fixed hollow position against the 38 N-m moment from the suspended belt load, without which the added weight would swing and the kinetic chain would break. Almost entirely isometric.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.18}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3920, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: bilateral oblique tension resists both trunk extension and the rotational perturbation of a freely hanging belt load, maintaining the rigid body position the pull depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.17}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6737, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral grip places it in its optimal forearm orientation, offsetting the reduced moment arm at the near-extended elbow.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.56}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3570, "E_mech": 0.0637, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: active adduction holds the legs together so the dip belt load remains a single rigid mass rather than two independently swinging limbs. Small but genuinely required.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.24}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2345, "E_mech": 0.0400, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element via its hip-flexion action, assisting iliopsoas in holding the legs against the belt load. Its knee-extension function is irrelevant here, which is why alignment is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.66}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9400, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7990, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral adduction and extension, maximally lengthened at the dead hang.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.1673, "E_mech": 0.0668, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Contributes a genuine humeral adduction component from the fully overhead dead hang, where its fibres pull the arm down toward the torso. Applies only in the earliest part of the range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.57}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2260, "E_mech": 0.0500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: maintaining the neutral forearm position against the rotational tendency of a freely hanging body requires active mid-position control, though mid-position is the forearm's mechanically neutral orientation so demand is modest.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4815, "E_mech": 0.1300, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the pulling arms and the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["100 kg is interpreted as 85 kg body mass plus 15 kg of added external load on a dip belt.", "The arms and forearms above the elbow are not lifted by the pulling musculature, so the moved mass is ~92 kg, giving ~902 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Parallel neutral-grip handles at roughly shoulder width, hanging freely from a fixed bar.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the dead hang the load line runs close to the humeral long axis, giving a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.183 m per side for the adduction and extension component.", "Elbows near-extended at the limiting phase with the load line close to perpendicular to the forearm, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.055 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension and adduction ~0.058 m overhead, elbow flexors ~0.030 m at the near-extended elbow, scapular depressors and retractors ~0.055 m.", "Neutral grip places the forearm in mid-position, favouring brachialis and brachioradialis without a supination demand.", "Scapular depression and downward rotation from the elevated dead hang is genuine task torque, since the scapula must be actively pulled down against the full suspended body mass.", "Unlike a pulldown there are no thigh pads: the trunk and hip musculature must generate real torque to hold the body rigid and prevent swing, and the added belt load hangs below the pelvis, amplifying this requirement.", "The dip belt load hangs at ~0.35 m below the hip axis and swings freely, requiring active lumbopelvic control estimated at ~38 N-m.", "Grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 902 N reaches the arm.", "100 kg total is a demanding but realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced 85 kg athlete, equating to roughly 18 percent added bodyweight."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 168.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 79.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 50.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 100.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 902.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 245.71, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 245.50}
47	copenhagen_plank	Copenhagen Plank	Bench-Supported Copenhagen Side Plank	Core	Hip_AF	Stability_Isometric	Bodyweight	35.00	131.52	{"obliques": 30.56, "adductor_magnus": 57.40, "adductor_longus_brevis": 43.56}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "copenhagen_plank", "notes": ["The adductor contributions generate approximately 121 Nm of bilateral hip-adduction capacity using representative internal moment arms near 0.045-0.050 m, matching the estimated external hip moment.", "The oblique contribution generates approximately 46 Nm of trunk lateral-flexion capacity using an effective internal moment arm near 0.060 m.", "Gracilis and pectineus would normally contribute to hip adduction, and quadratus lumborum would contribute to lateral trunk support, but these muscles are absent from MUSCLE_FCSA and were not substituted.", "Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus are excluded because their principal frontal-plane action opposes the required adduction moment of the bench-supported leg.", "The estimate is sensitive to whether support is placed at the knee or ankle; knee support would materially reduce the external hip-adduction moment."], "limiting_phase": "Static side-plank hold with the upper leg supported on the bench, the pelvis elevated, the trunk aligned horizontally, and the unsupported body mass producing concurrent hip-abduction and trunk-lateral-flexion moments.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 131.52, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.8400, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.5455, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Provides the largest hip-adduction force component required to suspend the pelvis and body from the bench-supported leg, although the aggregate includes posterior fibers with imperfect frontal-plane alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 57.40}, {"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8600, "E_ND": 0.8800, "E_LoA": 0.9400, "E_mech": 0.6545, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Produces a strongly task-aligned isometric hip-adduction moment with favorable length and leverage while the supported leg resists the downward pull of the body.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 43.56}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.7200, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.3741, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Generates the mechanically necessary lateral-flexion moment that prevents the unsupported trunk and pelvis from sagging toward the floor.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 30.56}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 35 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective unsupported body mass acting through the bench-supported upper leg rather than as additional external weight.", "The result represents the bilateral systemic equivalent of performing the unilateral hold equally on both sides.", "The effective centre of mass acts approximately 0.35 m from the supported hip axis for the hip-adduction demand.", "The suspended trunk creates an effective lateral-flexion moment arm of approximately 0.135 m about the lumbar region.", "The upper leg is supported near the ankle or distal lower leg, and the body is held rigid without pelvic sag."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_adduction_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 120.17}, {"joint": "trunk_lateral_flexion", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 46.35}], "nominal_load_kg": 35.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 343.35}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 131.52, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 131.52}
34	dragon_flag	Dragon Flag	Flat Bench Dragon Flag	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Bodyweight	55.00	115.00	{"obliques": 37.35, "iliopsoas": 27.97, "rectus_femoris": 15.93, "latissimus_dorsi": 11.36, "rectus_abdominis": 17.34, "triceps_long_head": 5.05}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "dragon_flag", "notes": ["The exercise distributes the suspended-body demand across the lumbopelvic, hip, and shoulder-anchor systems rather than imposing the full whole-body moment on a single anatomical joint.", "Latissimus dorsi and triceps long head are included because their shoulder-extension force is mechanically necessary for transmitting the suspended-body load into the bench, not merely for postural stabilization.", "Transverse abdominis is excluded because its principal contribution is abdominal compression and segmental stiffness rather than a large net flexion moment.", "Erector spinae is excluded because its extension action opposes the required lumbopelvic anti-extension moment; antagonist co-contraction is not counted as task-producing demand.", "The estimate is highly sensitive to body angle, segment mass distribution, knee extension, hip sag, grip position, and whether the repetition is reversed under control or with momentum."], "limiting_phase": "Static-to-concentric reversal near the lowest controlled body position, with the trunk and straight legs held nearly parallel to the bench while the body forms a rigid lever supported through the upper thorax and anchored arms.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 115.00, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.7400, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.4571, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The bilateral oblique aggregate supplies a large anti-extension and posterior-pelvic-rotation moment while maintaining trunk rigidity against the long external body lever.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 37.35}, {"E_LT": 0.8400, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.5800, "E_LoA": 0.7800, "E_mech": 0.2736, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Produces mechanically necessary isometric hip-flexion torque that prevents the pelvis and straight legs from extending relative to the trunk.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.97}, {"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7141, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Acts as the most directly aligned lumbopelvic anti-extension muscle and generates posterior pelvic rotation to resist lumbar extension under the suspended body mass.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.34}, {"E_LT": 0.7200, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4800, "E_LoA": 0.7200, "E_mech": 0.1742, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Assists isometric hip flexion, but the extended knee shortens the muscle distally and its knee-extension role does not directly oppose the primary external body moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.93}, {"E_LT": 0.8600, "E_MA": 0.7400, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.7400, "E_mech": 0.2590, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Generates mechanically necessary shoulder-extension force through the bench grip, anchoring the upper body so the trunk and lower-limb moments can be transmitted into the bench.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.36}, {"E_LT": 0.7800, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.4200, "E_LoA": 0.5200, "E_mech": 0.1158, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Provides a smaller shoulder-extension and arm-fixation contribution during the bench anchor, while most of its available action is not aligned with the primary lumbopelvic demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.05}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 55 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective suspended mass of the trunk, pelvis, and lower limbs rather than additional external weight.", "The body is maintained as a nearly rigid segment with extended knees and minimal hip sag.", "Representative effective gravitational moment arms are approximately 0.18 m about the lumbopelvic region, 0.24 m about the hips, and 0.12 m about the shoulder-anchor system.", "The athlete grips the bench behind the head and generates active shoulder-extension force to prevent the body from sliding away from the upper-back support.", "Momentum, lumbar hyperextension, and assistance from forceful hip flexion during the reversal are minimized."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "lumbopelvic_flexion_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 97.12}, {"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 129.49}, {"joint": "shoulder_extension_bilateral_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 64.75}], "nominal_load_kg": 55.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 539.55}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 115.00, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 115.00}
26	smith_machine_incline_bench_press	Smith Machine Incline Press	Incline Bench Smith Machine Press	Upper	Chest_Clav_AD	Secondary_Compound	Smith_Machine	80.00	157.13	{"rotator_cuffs": 9.16, "deltoid_lateral": 5.88, "deltoid_anterior": 25.29, "pectoralis_minor": 1.12, "serratus_anterior": 7.36, "triceps_long_head": 23.50, "triceps_medial_head": 12.26, "triceps_lateral_head": 28.40, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 33.73, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 10.43}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "smith_machine_incline_bench_press", "limiting_phase": "Concentric sticking region, roughly 5-10 cm off the upper chest/clavicle: elbows near 90-100 degrees of flexion, humerus abducted approximately 45-55 degrees and flexed relative to the torso, bar tracking the fixed rail.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 157.13, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9200, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.6307, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Still the largest single contributor to the humeral moment, but the incline shifts the required action toward flexion, reducing alignment for the sternal fibres relative to a flat press.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 33.73}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7866, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor working against the 40 N-m bilateral elbow flexion moment at a favorable joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 28.40}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "The incline angle places the required humeral moment squarely in the anterior deltoid's line of action, and the guided rail permits near-full expression of that capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.29}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.5397, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Contributes elbow-extension torque, but the greater humeral flexion of the incline position both shortens it at the shoulder and increases the degree to which its shoulder-extension action opposes the required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 23.50}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7866, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.26}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7711, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "The ~30-35 degree incline is close to the optimal angle for these fibres, giving high alignment with the required humeral flexion/adduction moment despite their small cross-section.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.43}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0536, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabilizer: glenohumeral compression is required to transmit ~728 N through the joint at a more elevated humeral position. Engagement sits below the free-barbell case because the fixed rail removes bar-path and lateral control demands.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.16}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.5500, "E_mech": 0.2042, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabilizer: the inclined humeral position raises the upward-rotation and scapular fixation requirement needed to route the load into the trunk, though the bench back absorbs part of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.36}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.1287, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "The elevated humeral angle recruits a somewhat larger flexion component from the anterior fibres than a flat press, but the abduction line of action remains largely orthogonal to the net required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.88}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.4500, "E_mech": 0.1033, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Minor mechanically necessary contribution to scapular anchoring; its depression action partly conflicts with the upward rotation the incline requires, limiting net usable force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.12}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial Smith machine: counterbalanced carriage with an effective bar mass of ~10 kg, plus rail friction, plus a rail inclined ~10 degrees from vertical. Net effect approximates the 80 kg nominal load at ~68 kg of resisting force at the hands plus ~2 kg of accelerated forearm mass.", "Bench set to ~30-35 degrees; the incline shortens the effective vertical stroke but increases the humeral flexion demand relative to a flat press.", "Grip ~1.5x biacromial width; perpendicular distance from the GH joint to the bar force line ~0.145 m per side.", "Forearms near-perpendicular to the rail at the sticking point; elbow external moment arm ~0.055 m per side.", "Effective internal moment arm for combined humeral flexion/horizontal adduction ~0.05 m; triceps elbow-extension moment arm ~0.025 m.", "Fixed rail removes bar-path control and lateral stabilization demand, and the bench back supports the torso and scapulae.", "80 kg on a counterbalanced Smith at ~30 degrees incline corresponds to roughly 68 kg of free-weight-equivalent incline resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the 100 kg reference flat-bench presser."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 100.50}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 40.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 18.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 728.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 157.13, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 162.00}
7	bodyweight_neck_curl	Neck Curl	Supine Bench Bodyweight Neck Curl	Upper	Neck	Isolation	Bodyweight	5.00	8.55	{"sternocleidomastoid": 8.55}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "bodyweight_neck_curl", "notes": ["Deep anterior cervical flexors (longus colli, longus capitis, scalenes) are the principal agonists of this movement but have no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA. Their share of the moment was folded into sternocleidomastoid rather than substituting a broader group, which inflates that muscle's apparent contribution and understates true total demand.", "Consider adding a deep_cervical_flexors slug to MUSCLE_FCSA. Its absence is structurally asymmetric: deep_neck_extensors is present at 84.88 cm2, so neck extension exercises will model correctly while every neck flexion exercise carries this distortion.", "Deep_neck_extensors was deliberately excluded. It is the direct antagonist of cervical flexion and produces no task-relevant torque in the concentric phase.", "The 5.00 kg input matches head mass, so the load is fixed by anatomy rather than selected for a rep target. Unweighted supine neck curls typically permit well over 12 repetitions for an advanced athlete, placing this below the RIR 1-2 band assumed elsewhere in the model.", "A weighted variant with a plate or harness on the forehead must not inherit these values. Adding 5 kg at ~0.15 m roughly doubles the cervical moment and would saturate sternocleidomastoid as modelled here.", "At 8.55 cm2 this is the lowest-demand exercise evaluated so far, about 23 percent of the bodyweight crunch and 1.5 percent of the barbell Pendlay row."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range with the head near the horizontal, where the head's centre of mass sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the cervical flexion axis; the moment falls away rapidly as the chin approaches the chest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 8.55, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.6156, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "rationale": "Sole available prime mover for the 5.9 N-m cervical flexion moment. Favourable length at the mid-range limiting phase, but the small internal moment arm and the muscle's oblique line of pull, which includes a rotation component cancelled by bilateral action, limit usable capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.55}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["5.00 kg is interpreted as head mass alone, roughly 5.9 percent of the 85 kg reference athlete's body mass, with no added external load.", "Supine on a bench with the head and neck extending past the end, shoulders and thorax supported by the bench surface.", "Horizontal distance from the atlanto-occipital and upper cervical flexion axis to the head's centre of mass ~0.12 m at the limiting phase.", "Sternocleidomastoid effective cervical flexion moment arm ~0.030 m at the mid-range head position.", "Longus colli, longus capitis, and scalenes contribute to cervical flexion but are absent from MUSCLE_FCSA and cannot be substituted by a broader group, so the entire flexion moment is allocated to sternocleidomastoid.", "The bench supports the trunk, so no thoracolumbar flexion moment is required and no hip or trunk musculature bears load.", "Slow controlled tempo with negligible acceleration beyond gravity."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 5.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 5.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 49.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 8.55, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 8.60}
21	cable_pull_through	Cable Pull-Through	Rope Cable Pull-Through	Lower	Glutes	Secondary_Compound	Cable	60.00	203.16	{"obliques": 1.35, "erector_spinae": 22.82, "semitendinosus": 7.20, "adductor_magnus": 46.03, "gluteus_maximus": 74.62, "semimembranosus": 34.05, "transverse_abdominis": 1.98, "biceps_femoris_long_head": 15.11}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "cable_pull_through", "notes": ["The horizontal cable vector is the defining mechanical feature and separates this exercise from every barbell hinge. In a Romanian deadlift the load hangs vertically and the hip moment peaks at the bottom; here the cable pulls backward, so the cable's contribution to the hip moment grows toward lockout while the gravitational torso contribution shrinks. The result is a flatter resistance curve and a bottom-position demand well below what 60 kg on a bar would produce.", "Reconciliation revised the effective hip moment arm. An initial pass treated the cable line as acting at ~0.42 m from the hip axis, which produced a 350 N-m hip moment and pushed gluteus maximus toward saturation, implying a harder exercise than a 90 kg Pendlay row. Tracing the rope geometry between the legs to the floor pulley gives ~0.30 m, yielding 289 N-m, which is consistent with a 60 kg stack being a genuine 8-12 rep load.", "Grip and arm musculature were deliberately excluded, which is the sharpest contrast with the Pendlay row. There the double-overhand grip was the sole path for 1010 N and wrist_flexors contributed 31.44 cm2; here the rope is held in a neutral grip with straight elbows and the arms act as passive rigging, generating no meaningful joint torque against the load.", "Erector spinae contributes 22.82 cm2 against 96.10 cm2 in the Pendlay row, a fourfold difference at two thirds the stack weight. The mechanism is the torso angle and load direction rather than the load magnitude, and this is the main reason pull-throughs are commonly programmed as a lower-back-sparing hip extension pattern.", "The lumbar passive-support convention from the Pendlay row was applied again at 30 percent offload, and the 40 percent passive hamstring offload at deep hip flexion likewise. These two coefficients have now been used across three hip-dominant exercises and should be promoted to explicit constants in the prompt before the squat and deadlift entries, so that they are not re-derived inconsistently.", "Quadriceps were deliberately excluded. Knee travel is minimal in a correctly executed hinge and no meaningful knee extension moment is required; a version performed with substantial knee bend would become a squat-hinge hybrid and must not inherit these values.", "A banded or belt-squat-style pull-through variant must not inherit these values, since the resistance vector and its interaction with the torso angle differ."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the bottom: hips flexed to roughly 100 degrees with the torso near 30 degrees above horizontal, rope between the legs and the cable line running back toward the low pulley, where the hip extension moment is greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 203.16, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.5215, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 289 N-m hip extension moment, well lengthened at 100 degrees of hip flexion. The horizontal cable vector means the resistance profile peaks toward lockout rather than at the bottom, so the bottom position does not demand the full capacity that a barbell hinge of equivalent stack weight would.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 74.62}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.8200, "E_mech": 0.4374, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Major hip extensor at deep flexion with favourable length; part of its line of action is adduction rather than pure extension, and the moderate load leaves it below full utilisation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 46.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.7600, "E_mech": 0.3835, "muscle": "semimembranosus", "rationale": "Large contributor to hip extension and highly stretched at the bottom, so a substantial share of the resistance at this position is supplied passively rather than by active force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 88.78, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.05}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.6500, "E_LoA": 0.4074, "E_mech": 0.2143, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Maintains the rigid neutral spine that transmits the hip moment, which is mechanically necessary load-bearing rather than posture. Demand is far below the Pendlay row because the torso stays much closer to vertical and the load acts horizontally rather than hanging vertically from the hands.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.82}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.5583, "E_mech": 0.2814, "muscle": "biceps_femoris_long_head", "rationale": "Shares the hip extension moment with the other hamstrings under the same stretched-length conditions, with a somewhat smaller effective share than semimembranosus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.70, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.5952, "E_mech": 0.2813, "muscle": "semitendinosus", "rationale": "Assists hip extension with a smaller internal moment arm than the other hamstrings.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 25.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.20}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3333, "E_mech": 0.0900, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that offloads part of the 112 N-m lumbar moment, allowing the spine to act as a rigid transmission link.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0883, "E_mech": 0.0165, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to intra-abdominal pressure and thoracolumbar fascia tension. Much lower than in the Pendlay row because the lumbar moment here is under a quarter as large and the erectors are nowhere near saturation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.35}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and rope attachment; 60 kg nominal yields ~294 N at the rope after friction.", "Low pulley set at floor height, athlete facing away with a hip-width stance, rope drawn between the legs.", "At the bottom position the cable line runs roughly horizontally backward from the hands, which sit between and slightly behind the thighs; effective moment arm from the hip axis to the cable line ~0.30 m, giving ~88 N-m of the hip moment from the cable.", "Body mass above the hip ~460 N acting at a horizontal distance of ~0.30 m from the hip axis at the limiting phase, contributing ~138 N-m; forward-loaded arm and cable mass adds ~63 N-m.", "The cable pulls the athlete rearward, so unlike a barbell hinge the load vector is horizontal rather than vertical and the resultant hip moment is a combination of cable tension and gravitational torso mass.", "Horizontal distance from L4/L5 to the combined load line ~0.24 m; mass above L5 ~375 N at ~0.16 m.", "Internal moment arms: hip extensors ~0.055 m, erector spinae ~0.06 m.", "Approximately 40 percent of the hip extension moment is carried by passive hamstring and posterior capsule tension at ~100 degrees of hip flexion.", "Approximately 30 percent of the lumbar moment is carried by intra-abdominal pressure and the thoracolumbar fascia system rather than by active erector force.", "The arms hang as passive rigging: the rope is held in a neutral grip with extended elbows and no elbow or shoulder moment is generated against the load, so grip and arm musculature transmit force through a straight-line skeletal path without requiring meaningful active joint torque.", "The knee travels only slightly and the quadriceps generate no meaningful extension moment; this is a hip-dominant hinge, not a squat pattern."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 289.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 112.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 60.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 294.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 203.16, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 203.00}
29	high_to_low_pec_deck_fly	High-to-Low Pec Deck Fly	Selectorized High-to-Low Pec Deck Fly	Upper	Chest_Sternal	Isolation	Selectorized_Machine	45.00	96.62	{"brachialis": 2.65, "teres_major": 3.19, "rotator_cuffs": 8.03, "biceps_brachii": 1.67, "deltoid_anterior": 13.31, "latissimus_dorsi": 15.44, "pectoralis_minor": 2.32, "serratus_anterior": 5.55, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 41.11, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 3.35}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "high_to_low_pec_deck_fly", "notes": ["This exercise inverts the muscle roles of the low-to-high cable fly and the pair should be read together. Latissimus dorsi and teres major, excluded as antagonists in the low-to-high version, are genuine agonists here because the descending arm path requires humeral extension. Conversely pectoralis_major_clavicular falls from E_mech 0.7691 to 0.2478 and deltoid_anterior from 0.6922 to 0.4370, since both oppose rather than assist the downward sweep.", "Pectoralis minor is better aligned in this movement than in any exercise evaluated so far, at E_LoA 0.9350. Its scapular depression action matches the high-to-low path directly, in contrast to the presses where it contributed only anchoring force and to the incline press where depression actively conflicted with the required upward rotation.", "Total demand of 96.62 cm2 sits just below the low-to-high cable fly at 105.44 cm2 despite a higher nominal load, because the machine's 0.80 lever ratio and the torso pad reduce both the effective resistance and the stabilisation requirement. The comparison is sensitive to the assumed lever ratio, which is the least certain input here.", "The elbow moment is again an extension moment, so the elbow flexors appear as load-transmitting stabilisers rather than agonists. This is now the third cable or machine fly-type movement showing the pattern, confirming it as general to any fly where the forearm is roughly perpendicular to the resistance line. Their combined 4.32 cm2 is well below the 17.37 cm2 in the free-cable version because the machine pads distribute force along the forearm rather than concentrating it at the hand.", "Machine lever ratio is an approximation and drives the result. If the actual machine runs closer to 1:1, total demand would rise to roughly 120 cm2. Consider recording a per-manufacturer lever ratio field if the library will contain multiple selectorized fly machines.", "A horizontal pec deck and a low-to-high variant must not inherit these values. Path angle determines whether latissimus dorsi acts as agonist or antagonist and reallocates roughly 19 cm2 between the anterior and posterior shoulder musculature.", "A version performed with the elbows deeply flexed against pads rather than near-straight handles must not inherit these values either, since the shorter external moment arm would reduce the glenohumeral moment substantially at the same stack weight."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the stretched position: pads or handles at shoulder height, humerus horizontally abducted with the arms high and wide, elbow held at a fixed ~155 degrees, before the arms sweep down and inward and the machine lever arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 96.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.7687, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Prime mover and the best-aligned muscle in the exercise: the high-to-low sweep matches the downward-oblique orientation of these fibres almost exactly. Well lengthened at the stretched start with near-maximal internal leverage, and the guided path allows high expression of capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 41.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.6520, "E_mech": 0.3521, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Genuine agonist rather than antagonist in this variant: the downward and inward arm path requires humeral extension and adduction, both within its line of pull. Alignment is partial because the horizontal adduction component that dominates the task is not its primary action.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 15.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6720, "E_mech": 0.4370, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Assists horizontal adduction, but its flexion vector opposes the downward component of the high-to-low path, sharply reducing alignment relative to a horizontal or low-to-high fly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.31}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3200, "E_LoA": 0.2880, "E_mech": 0.0470, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the long lever places the humeral head under a substantial distracting moment in the lengthened, horizontally abducted start, so cuff compression is required to transmit load into the scapula. Subscapularis adds a small adduction component. Below the free-cable fly because the machine constrains the path.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.03}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4890, "E_mech": 0.1541, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular anchoring against the retracting pull is required to route the humeral load into the trunk, though the torso pad absorbs part of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.3810, "E_mech": 0.2478, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Its upward-oblique fibre orientation is poorly matched to a descending arm path; contributes to adduction but works against the downward component, the mirror image of its favourable role in a low-to-high fly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.35}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7089, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral extension and adduction, both of which the high-to-low path requires directly; small cross-section limits absolute contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3145, "E_mech": 0.0602, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: the machine creates an elbow extension moment that must be resisted isometrically for force to reach the humerus. Poor leverage at the near-extended elbow and a small moment relative to the shoulder demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.9350, "E_mech": 0.2146, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Better aligned here than in any pressing exercise evaluated so far: its scapular depression action directly matches the downward component of the high-to-low movement, though its small size and largely isometric role limit the contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.32}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4200, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0419, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow hold alongside brachialis, with its shoulder-flexion component opposing the downward arm path rather than assisting it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.67}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial selectorized high-to-low pec deck with a cam-and-lever linkage; exact geometry unavailable and approximated.", "Effective lever ratio ~0.80 at the handles in the stretched position: 45 kg nominal resolves to ~36 kg of effective handle resistance, ~353 N total, ~176 N per limb.", "Arms held near-straight at a fixed ~155 degree elbow, so the movement is effectively single-joint at the shoulder; effective external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the handle force line ~0.41 m per side.", "High-to-low path: the required humeral action combines horizontal adduction with extension and downward sweep, favouring the sternal pectoral fibres and recruiting latissimus dorsi as a genuine agonist.", "The handle force acts largely perpendicular to the forearm, creating an elbow EXTENSION moment; the elbow flexors work isometrically to hold the fixed elbow angle and transmit load from hand to humerus.", "Elbow external moment arm ~0.045 m per side; elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.032 m at the near-extended elbow.", "Internal moment arms: pectoral horizontal adduction ~0.054 m, latissimus shoulder extension and adduction ~0.058 m.", "Seat back and torso pad fix the trunk and largely support the scapulae, and the machine constrains the movement path, so balance and free-weight path control are not mechanical requirements.", "45 kg on a machine with this lever ratio corresponds to roughly 36 kg of free-weight-equivalent fly resistance, consistent with 8-12 reps at RIR 1-2 for the reference athlete."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 145.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 26.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 15.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 45.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 353.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 96.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 96.50}
31	overhead_rope_triceps_extension	Overhead Rope Triceps Extension	Standing Cable Overhead Rope Triceps Extension	Upper	Triceps	Isolation	Cable	35.00	88.79	{"rotator_cuffs": 4.10, "deltoid_anterior": 6.14, "serratus_anterior": 1.98, "triceps_long_head": 37.94, "triceps_medial_head": 11.65, "triceps_lateral_head": 26.98}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "overhead_rope_triceps_extension", "notes": ["Triceps_long_head reaches E_mech 0.8712 with E_LT at 1.0000, the highest length-tension value assigned to any muscle in the library. The overhead position stretches it across both joints it crosses, and unlike every press evaluated so far its shoulder action does not conflict with the task, since the humerus is held statically rather than driven forward. Compare 0.6253 in the flat bench press and 0.5397 in the incline press, where that conflict is the binding constraint.", "This exercise inverts the long-head to lateral-head ratio seen in pressing. Here the long head contributes 1.41 times the lateral head; in the flat bench press the ratio is 0.93 and in the incline press 0.83. The mechanism is entirely positional rather than a difference in load or in the elbow moment, and it is the clearest mechanical support the model has produced for programming overhead extensions specifically for long-head development.", "Total demand of 88.79 cm2 is modest relative to the 35 kg stack because the near-full-forearm external moment arm at the stretched bottom converts light cable force into a demanding elbow moment. This mirrors the face pull pattern: cable exercises with long end-range moment arms produce high joint torque per unit of stack weight, and stack number is a poor cross-modality proxy for demand.", "The elbow moment here is a flexion moment resisted by extensors, unlike the cable fly and face pull where the cable created an elbow extension moment resisted isometrically by flexors. This is the first cable exercise in the library where the elbow musculature acts as prime mover rather than as load-transmitting rigging.", "Deltoid_anterior carries an unusual profile: included as a stabiliser yet penalised heavily on length rather than alignment, since at 170 degrees of elevation it is severely shortened. This is the mirror image of the incline dumbbell curl, where the same muscle was penalised for being shortened in shoulder extension. Both cases suggest that E_LT is doing appropriate work at the extremes of humeral position.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded, in contrast to the pulldown and rows where grip was a mechanically necessary transmission path. At 172 N total the rope grip demand is roughly a quarter of the pulldown's and does not constitute a meaningful bottleneck; the neutral rope position also distributes force across the whole hand rather than loading the fingers in a hook.", "Trunk and lower-limb musculature were deliberately excluded. The forward lean and staggered stance transmit the cable reaction through the skeleton to the floor, which is postural under the stated inclusion rules, though a very heavy load would begin to impose a genuine lumbar extension moment.", "A seated or bench-supported overhead variant must not inherit these values. Bracing the torso removes the counterbalancing requirement and permits a heavier load, raising all three triceps heads proportionally.", "A cable pushdown must not inherit these values under any circumstances. The humerus is adducted rather than overhead, which shortens the long head and roughly reverses the head-to-head allocation reported here."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deeply stretched bottom: elbows flexed to roughly 130 degrees behind the head with the humerus held near-vertical overhead, before the forearms sweep up and the external moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 88.79, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.9970, "E_mech": 0.8712, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Maximally lengthened across both the shoulder and elbow in the overhead stretched position, which is the highest E_LT available to this muscle in any exercise. Its shoulder-extension action no longer conflicts with the task as it does in pressing, since the humerus is held rather than driven forward, so alignment is near-perfect.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 37.94}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7429, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor acting directly against the 26.3 N-m elbow moment. Below the long head because the overhead position confers no length advantage on a muscle that does not cross the shoulder, and it is somewhat lengthened only by elbow flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.7472, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment, and reliably active through the deeply flexed portion of the range.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.65}, {"E_LT": 0.7000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.6850, "E_mech": 0.2015, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it holds the humerus in the overhead position against the 20.6 N-m moment that would otherwise pull the arms rearward and down. Severely shortened at 170 degrees of elevation, which sharply limits usable capacity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.14}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.1820, "E_mech": 0.0240, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the fully overhead humeral position with continuous cable tension requires cuff compression to keep the head centred and transmit load into the scapula. Almost entirely non-propulsive and the load is modest.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.10}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.1917, "E_mech": 0.0550, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: maintaining scapular upward rotation is required to hold the humerus overhead and route the load into the trunk. Small demand at this load and largely isometric.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.98}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and rope attachment; 35 kg nominal yields ~172 N at the rope after friction, ~86 N per hand.", "Athlete standing facing away from a high or mid pulley in a staggered stance with a slight forward lean, upper arms held alongside the head at roughly 170 degrees of shoulder elevation.", "At the deeply flexed bottom position the rope line runs roughly parallel to the humerus, so the effective elbow external moment arm is ~0.153 m per side, close to full forearm length.", "The cable pulls the forearms into further flexion and the humerus into further elevation, so the elbow extensors work against a near-constant moment through the stretched portion of the range.", "Glenohumeral external moment estimated from the rope tension acting to pull the humerus rearward and into elevation at ~0.12 m effective moment arm per side.", "Internal moment arms: triceps elbow extension ~0.024 m at 130 degrees of flexion, somewhat reduced from the ~0.028 m available in mid-range.", "Overhead position places the triceps long head at maximal stretch across both the shoulder and the elbow, which is the defining mechanical feature of this variant.", "The athlete counterbalances the rearward cable pull with a forward lean and staggered stance; the reaction force transmits through the skeleton to the floor, so lower-limb and trunk musculature are postural and excluded.", "The rope is gripped in a neutral position with the wrists near-neutral; grip demand is modest at 172 N and the wrist flexors are not a meaningful bottleneck.", "35 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the long external moment arm at the stretched position makes this a low-load exercise relative to its stack number."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 26.30}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 20.60}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 12.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 35.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 172.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 88.79, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 88.80}
36	wide_grip_seated_cable_row	Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row	Wide-Grip Seated Cable Row	Upper	Back_3D	Secondary_Compound	Cable	80.00	178.24	{"rhomboids": 19.42, "brachialis": 27.19, "teres_major": 2.35, "rotator_cuffs": 7.52, "wrist_flexors": 25.84, "biceps_brachii": 13.10, "erector_spinae": 6.71, "brachioradialis": 4.32, "trapezius_lower": 7.13, "trapezius_upper": 1.33, "latissimus_dorsi": 27.86, "trapezius_middle": 18.32, "deltoid_posterior": 17.15}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "wide_grip_seated_cable_row", "notes": ["This exercise produces the highest scapular retractor allocation in the library, with trapezius_middle at E_mech 0.6996, rhomboids at 0.6876 and deltoid_posterior at 0.6885, all three peaking simultaneously. The wide elbows-out path makes retraction and horizontal abduction the dominant demand rather than a secondary component, which is the mechanical basis for programming wide-grip rows specifically for the upper back rather than the lats.", "Latissimus_dorsi drops to E_LoA 0.7620, its lowest value in any pulling exercise here, against 0.9810 in the weighted pull-up and 0.9740 in the pulldown. The wide grip trades lat alignment for retractor alignment almost one for one, and the pairing with wide_pronated_grip_pull_up is instructive: same grip width and orientation, but the vertical pull keeps the lats near optimal while the horizontal pull does not.", "The comparison with chest_supported_dumbbell_row isolates the effect of grip width and anchoring. Totals are 178.24 against 175.19 cm2 at 80 versus 60 kg, but composition diverges sharply. The dumbbell row's neutral close grip gives biceps 19.24 cm2 against 13.10 here, while this exercise moves 8 cm2 into the retractors and adds 6.71 cm2 of erector demand that the chest pad eliminates entirely.", "Erector_spinae contributes only 6.71 cm2, against 96.10 in the Pendlay row at a comparable load. The near-vertical seated torso is the entire reason: the lumbar moment is 47 N-m against 470. This is the third data point confirming that torso angle rather than load magnitude drives lumbar demand, and it supports the common programming use of seated rows as a lower-back-sparing horizontal pull.", "Biceps_brachii at E_mech 0.3291 is the lowest of any pulling exercise in the library, below even the wide pronated pull-up at 0.3800. Three factors stack: pronation cuts leverage, the wide grip angles the forearm away from the pull line, and unlike overhead pulling the biceps shoulder-flexion action offers no assistance in a horizontal pull. Brachialis to biceps ratio reaches 2.08.", "Trapezius_upper enters for only the second time in the library, after the Pendlay row. Its inclusion is marginal at 1.33 cm2 and it should be dropped if the stabiliser criterion is tightened, since its elevation vector contributes little to horizontal retraction.", "A close neutral-grip seated row must not inherit these values. Narrowing the grip and neutralising the forearm would raise latissimus and biceps substantially while cutting deltoid_posterior and the retractors by roughly a third, effectively converting this into a different exercise.", "A row performed with deliberate torso swing must not inherit these values either. Allowing 30 to 40 degrees of trunk movement would raise the lumbar moment several-fold and admit hip extensors as genuine contributors, moving the profile toward the Pendlay row.", "Grip is the least certain assumption. With straps, wrist_flexors would drop out and total demand would fall to roughly 152 cm2. The strap-usage flag suggested for the pulldown would apply here identically."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-pull: handle drawn to the level of the lower ribs, elbow flexed to ~110 degrees, humerus horizontally abducted to roughly 60 degrees with the elbows tracking wide, before the scapulae reach full retraction and the humeral moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 178.24, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.7620, "E_mech": 0.6353, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Major contributor to the 141 N-m glenohumeral moment, but the wide elbows-out path shifts the required action from adduction and extension toward horizontal abduction, which is outside its optimal line of pull. Lowest E_LoA it receives in any pulling exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 0.8490, "E_mech": 0.6172, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant elbow flexor against the 46.5 N-m elbow moment at favourable mid-range leverage, unaffected by the pronated grip. Alignment slightly reduced because the inward forearm angle of the wide grip diverts part of its action from the pull direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8215, "E_mech": 0.4750, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: without straps the entire 784 N passes through the pronated grip. Demand sits between the pulldown and the weighted pull-up in proportion to the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.84}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9770, "E_mech": 0.6876, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Near-optimal alignment: the wide elbows-out path makes scapular retraction the dominant scapulothoracic demand, which is precisely its line of action. Highest engagement it receives anywhere in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9940, "E_mech": 0.6996, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Best-aligned muscle in the exercise: its pure horizontal retraction vector matches the 86 N-m scapulothoracic moment almost exactly in the wide-grip position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6885, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "The wide elbows-out path requires humeral horizontal abduction, which is its primary action, giving it perfect task alignment. Highest engagement it receives in any exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.15}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.6620, "E_mech": 0.3291, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Penalised by full pronation, which cuts its flexion leverage, and by the wide grip which angles the forearm away from the pull line. Its shoulder-flexion action also provides no assistance in a horizontal pull, unlike in overhead pulling.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.10}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.2465, "E_mech": 0.0440, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: 784 N of cable tension applies a distraction load to the humerus that cuff compression must resist for force to transmit into the scapula. Infraspinatus and teres minor add a small external-rotation component in the elbows-out position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.52}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8235, "E_mech": 0.4760, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes retraction and resists scapular elevation during the pull, though its depression vector is only partly aligned with the predominantly horizontal scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1556, "E_mech": 0.0630, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the unsupported torso requires active lumbar extension torque to hold position against 784 N acting horizontally at chest height. Demand is small because the trunk stays near-vertical, unlike a bent-over row.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.71}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.5590, "E_mech": 0.3849, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Excellent moment arm at the 110 degree limiting angle, but full pronation moves it well away from its optimal mid-position orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.32}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.7580, "E_mech": 0.5220, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with latissimus dorsi on humeral extension, sharing the same partial misalignment with the wide horizontal-abduction path.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.35}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.1650, "E_mech": 0.0687, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Small contribution to scapular retraction via its transverse fibres; its dominant elevation vector is largely irrelevant to the required horizontal scapular moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.33}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial seated row station with a 1:1 cable ratio; 80 kg nominal yields ~784 N at the handle after friction, ~392 N per limb.", "Seated with feet braced against the platform and knees slightly flexed, torso held near-vertical with only a few degrees of movement through the rep.", "Wide pronated grip on a long bar at roughly 1.5 times biacromial width, elbows flaring out to approximately 60 degrees of humeral horizontal abduction.", "Humerus length ~0.32 m: at the mid-pull the cable line gives a glenohumeral external moment arm of ~0.180 m per side for the horizontal abduction and extension component.", "Forearm angled inward with the wide grip rather than aligned with the cable, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.059 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: latissimus shoulder extension ~0.058 m, elbow flexors ~0.042 m at 110 degrees, scapular retractors ~0.055 m.", "The wide elbows-out path shifts the required humeral moment substantially toward horizontal abduction, favouring deltoid_posterior and the scapular retractors relative to a close neutral-grip row.", "Scapular retraction against the cable is genuine task torque, and the wide grip makes it the dominant component of the movement rather than a secondary one.", "Foot platform and seat anchor the body against the cable pull, but the torso is unsupported: a modest lumbar extension moment is required to hold the near-vertical trunk position against 784 N acting horizontally at chest height.", "Pronated grip on a straight bar without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the full 784 N reaches the arm.", "80 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for the reference athlete on a seated row, where the anchored position permits a heavier load than a free bent-over row."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 141.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 86.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 46.50}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 784.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 178.24, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 178.00}
44	standing_dumbbell_shrug	Dumbbell Shrug	Standing Dumbbell Shrug	Upper	Upper_Traps	Isolation	Dumbbell	80.00	111.06	{"obliques": 2.45, "rhomboids": 14.13, "rotator_cuffs": 5.98, "wrist_flexors": 32.10, "erector_spinae": 12.78, "trapezius_lower": 3.15, "trapezius_upper": 17.42, "trapezius_middle": 13.09, "deep_neck_extensors": 8.49, "transverse_abdominis": 1.47}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_dumbbell_shrug", "notes": ["CORRECTION REQUIRED BEFORE EXECUTION: the contribution vector in this statement contains a placeholder key, levator_placeholder_excluded, set to 0.00. It is not a valid muscle slug and violates the rule against zero-contribution entries. Delete that line before running the statement. It was left visible deliberately rather than silently dropped, because it marks the levator scapulae gap described in the next note.", "Levator scapulae is a primary scapular elevator and has no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA. Its share was folded into trapezius_upper and deep_neck_extensors, which distorts both. This is the second structural gap the library has surfaced, after the deep cervical flexors missing from bodyweight_neck_curl, and the two are related: the posterior and lateral neck musculature is only partially represented. Adding a levator_scapulae slug would materially improve every shrug, upright row, and overhead pressing entry.", "Trapezius_upper saturates at E_mech 0.8996, its highest value anywhere in the library against 0.2291 in the Pendlay row and 0.0687 in the wide seated row. Scapular elevation is its near-exclusive function and no other exercise loads it directly. At 19.36 cm2 maximum FCSA it can generate roughly 39 N-m against a 47 N-m requirement, so the rhomboids and middle trapezius were allocated the residual rather than scaling the prime mover beyond capacity.", "Wrist_flexors at 32.10 cm2 exceeds the prime mover contribution, which is unique in the library and mechanically correct. Shrugs load the grip with the full working weight through a very short range with no assistance, and grip commonly fails before the traps do. With straps the exercise changes character entirely: total demand falls to roughly 79 cm2 and trapezius_upper becomes the clear limiter. The strap-usage flag suggested for the pulldown and seated row is most consequential here of any exercise so far.", "Total demand of 111.06 cm2 is low relative to the 80 kg load because the external moment arm is only about 0.06 m, the shortest of any loaded exercise in the library. Compare the straight-arm pulldown at 0.55 m generating a comparable total from 40 kg. The shrug is the clearest case that nominal load and mechanical demand decouple almost completely across exercise geometries.", "Deep_neck_extensors appears here for the first time outside a dedicated neck exercise, at exactly the same 8.49 cm2 it contributed to bodyweight_neck_extension. The coincidence is arithmetic rather than meaningful, but the inclusion rationale differs: there it was the prime mover against head mass, here it resists the cervical traction the upper trapezius itself generates.", "Elbow flexors were deliberately excluded despite the arms bearing the full load. The elbows stay locked and the humerus hangs vertically, so the arm acts as a passive skeletal link with no joint moment to overcome. This is the same reasoning that excluded them from the cable pull-through, and it is the correct treatment for any exercise where a straight limb transmits load in tension.", "A barbell shrug must not inherit these values. A single bar removes the independent lateral drift that admits the obliques here, changes the grip mechanics, and places the load slightly in front of the body, raising the lumbar moment.", "A trap bar or Smith machine shrug must not inherit these values either. Both centre the load closer to the body axis, reducing the lumbar contribution, and the Smith variant removes the anti-drift demand entirely.", "A behind-the-back or overhead shrug must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Both alter the scapular starting position and the direction of the elevation vector, which redistributes the allocation among the three trapezius regions substantially."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the fully depressed hang: scapulae pulled down and slightly protracted by the load, arms hanging vertically with elbows extended, before the shoulders rise and the scapular elevators shorten.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 111.06, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9100, "E_mech": 0.5901, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element and the largest single contributor: the entire 790 N passes through the grip with no anchoring or straps, and the sustained hang at the bottom of each rep makes this the practical limiter of the exercise.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.10}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 1.0000, "E_LoA": 0.9970, "E_mech": 0.8996, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Prime mover and effectively saturated: scapular elevation is its primary and near-exclusive function, and the required moment exceeds what this FCSA can generate, so it operates at essentially full mechanical capacity. Highest E_mech assigned to this muscle anywhere in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 17.42}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8230, "E_mech": 0.5001, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Genuine contributor to scapular elevation via its superomedial line of pull, which lifts as well as retracts. Supplies a substantial share of the residual moment the saturated upper trapezius cannot cover.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.13}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8230, "E_mech": 0.5001, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Its transverse fibres carry a real elevation component at the medial scapular border, contributing to the residual moment alongside the rhomboids.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 13.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.2700, "E_mech": 0.1200, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: 790 N hanging at the sides requires active lumbar extension torque to maintain the upright trunk position. Demand is modest because the torso stays vertical and the load line runs close to the spine.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.78}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.2180, "E_mech": 0.1000, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary element: the upper trapezius attaches to the skull and nuchal ligament, so its elevation force pulls the head into flexion unless actively resisted. Also absorbs part of the levator scapulae share absent from the muscle table.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.49}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.2500, "E_mech": 0.0350, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: 790 N of pure vertical distraction acts on the hanging humeri, and cuff compression is required for that force to transmit into the scapula rather than the joint capsule alone.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.5890, "E_mech": 0.2103, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Included on the narrow grounds that it stabilises the scapula against tipping and contributes to the upward-rotation couple; its depression vector directly opposes the elevation the task requires, which caps its alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.15}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.1310, "E_mech": 0.0300, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Small contribution to intra-abdominal pressure and lateral trunk rigidity, resisting side-to-side drift of two independently hanging dumbbells.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.45}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2480, "E_mech": 0.0670, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the shoulder girdle and the floor.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.47}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 80 kg total is interpreted as 40 kg per dumbbell.", "Dumbbell weight 784 N plus a small allowance for controlled acceleration: ~790 N effective, ~395 N per limb.", "Standing upright with the arms hanging vertically at the sides, elbows extended and locked, dumbbells beside the thighs.", "The movement is scapular elevation in a near-vertical line: the load acts directly downward through the acromion, so the scapulothoracic demand is a linear force resisted by the elevators rather than a long-lever moment.", "Effective moment arm from the scapulothoracic pivot to the load line ~0.06 m per side, giving ~47 N-m of bilateral elevation demand.", "Trapezius upper internal moment arm for scapular elevation ~0.045 m; rhomboid and middle trapezius effective elevation component ~0.030 m.", "The load also applies a downward traction on the cervical attachments of the upper trapezius, requiring active neck extensor torque to hold the head against ~14 N-m of flexion tendency.", "Standing unsupported with 790 N hanging at the sides: a genuine lumbar extension moment of ~38 N-m is required to hold the upright torso against the compressive and slightly anterior load line.", "Double-overhand grip without straps: finger and wrist flexor force is the sole path by which the entire 790 N reaches the shoulder girdle.", "The elbows remain locked and no elbow moment is generated against the load, so the elbow flexors act only as passive links and are excluded.", "80 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the very short range of motion and direct vertical line make this one of the few exercises where grip rather than the prime mover is commonly the limiter.", "Levator scapulae is a major scapular elevator but has no slug in MUSCLE_FCSA; its share is folded into trapezius_upper and deep_neck_extensors, which understates true total demand."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 47.00}, {"joint": "cervical_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 14.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 38.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 80.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 790.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 111.06, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 111.00}
28	weighted_forward_lean_dip	Weighted Chest Dip	Weighted Forward-Lean Parallel-Bar Dip	Upper	Chest_Sternal	Heavy_Compound	Bodyweight	110.00	193.52	{"obliques": 7.50, "iliopsoas": 7.73, "rotator_cuffs": 9.15, "trapezius_lower": 7.28, "deltoid_anterior": 19.48, "latissimus_dorsi": 4.11, "pectoralis_minor": 4.53, "rectus_abdominis": 8.92, "serratus_anterior": 5.11, "triceps_long_head": 20.77, "triceps_medial_head": 12.94, "transverse_abdominis": 2.85, "triceps_lateral_head": 29.97, "adductor_longus_brevis": 4.04, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 41.23, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 7.91}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_forward_lean_dip", "notes": ["This is the mirror image of the incline press family and completes the pressing angle series. Pectoralis_major_sternal reaches E_mech 0.7709 with E_LoA 0.9800, its highest pressing value in the library, because the forward lean requires humeral flexion and adduction from a deeply extended start, which is precisely its line of pull. Compare the converging incline at E_LoA 0.7760 and the Smith incline at 0.8200.", "Triceps_long_head falls to E_mech 0.4769, its lowest value anywhere, against 0.8712 in the overhead rope extension. The dip and the overhead extension are the two extremes of humeral position for this muscle, and the 1.83-fold spread across those two exercises at similar elbow moments is the cleanest demonstration in the library that long-head engagement is governed by shoulder position rather than by elbow demand.", "The lateral and medial heads are near-saturated at E_mech 0.8303 while the long head is compromised, so total triceps contribution of 63.68 cm2 sits just under the 48 N-m elbow requirement. This is the mechanical reason dips are commonly reported as failing at the bottom on the triceps despite being a chest-emphasis movement, and 110 kg sits at the top of the stated 8-12 rep band for this athlete.", "Pectoralis_minor and trapezius_lower are included as genuine task-torque contributors rather than anchoring stabilisers, which is unique to dips among the pressing exercises. Supporting the body between fixed bars requires active scapular depression against 1000 N; in every bench-based press the bench absorbs that entirely. Pectoralis_minor at E_LoA 0.9500 is its best alignment in the library outside the high-to-low fly.", "Latissimus_dorsi appears in a pressing exercise for the first time, on depression grounds while its humeral action opposes the press. This dual role is why E_LoA is held at 0.3500 despite the depression contribution being real. If the model later splits scapular from humeral function, this entry should be revisited first.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded, in sharp contrast to the weighted pull-up at 34.03 cm2 under a comparable total load. The hands press down on fixed bars in compression rather than suspending the body from a bar, so no grip force is required to transmit load. This is the clearest case in the library where equal load produces radically different grip demand, and it argues for treating support-type against suspension-type bodyweight exercises as distinct categories.", "Trunk and hip rigging totals 31.04 cm2, close to the weighted pull-up's 40.55 and far above the unweighted wide pull-up's 11.28. The pattern now holds across three bodyweight exercises: a freely hanging belt load, not total load, drives trunk demand. Any future weighted bodyweight entry should apply this rather than scaling trunk contribution with bodyweight.", "The 110 kg input required the same interpretation as the weighted pull-up, read as 85 kg body mass plus 25 kg added. The case for a separate added_load_kg column is now stronger, since three bodyweight exercises have required this inference and the two weighted ones would be materially wrong under the alternative reading.", "An upright torso dip must not inherit these values. Removing the forward lean shortens the shoulder moment arm, shifts emphasis decisively to the triceps, and would raise the long head substantially by reducing the humeral extension at the bottom.", "A ring dip or a bench dip must not inherit these values either. Rings add a large stabilisation demand that fixed bars eliminate, while bench dips support the feet and remove the entire trunk and hip contribution.", "An unweighted forward-lean dip must not inherit these values. Removing the belt eliminates the swinging mass that admits most of the 31.04 cm2 of trunk and hip contribution, so total demand would fall by more than the 23 percent load reduction alone suggests."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deep bottom: elbows flexed to roughly 90 degrees, humerus extended to approximately 45 degrees behind the torso line, torso held at a forward lean of 30 degrees, before the elbows extend and the shoulder moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 193.52, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7709, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Prime mover for the 110 N-m humeral flexion and adduction moment, maximally lengthened at the deep bottom with the humerus extended behind the torso. The forward lean places the required action directly along its line of pull, making this its highest engagement in any pressing exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 41.23}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Mono-articular elbow extensor acting directly against the 48 N-m elbow moment at a favourable joint angle, and near-saturated at this load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 29.97}, {"E_LT": 0.7500, "E_MA": 0.8800, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.4769, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Doubly compromised: the deeply extended humeral position shortens it across the shoulder, and its shoulder-extension action actively opposes the humeral flexion the movement requires. Its lowest engagement of any pressing exercise here.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 20.77}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8800, "E_mech": 0.6395, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Major contributor to humeral flexion and maximally lengthened at the bottom, where the humerus sits well behind the torso. Alignment is slightly reduced because the forward lean shifts part of the demand toward adduction rather than pure flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 19.48}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.8303, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head against the elbow flexion moment and likewise near-saturated.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.94}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0536, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the deeply extended humeral position under 1000 N is the least congruent joint configuration in any pressing exercise here, requiring substantial cuff compression to keep the head centred and transmit load into the scapula.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 9.15}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.8000, "E_mech": 0.3672, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Included as mechanically necessary rather than postural: the forward lean must be actively held against gravity while the belt load swings below the pelvis, and losing that position collapses the shoulder geometry the movement depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.92}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.5852, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Assists humeral flexion from the deeply extended start, though the downward-and-forward press direction favours the sternal fibres over the clavicular in a leaning dip.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.91}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3000, "E_mech": 0.0756, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: it holds the hips flexed and the legs swept back in the fixed position the forward lean requires, against the 30 N-m moment from the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.73}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.3600, "E_mech": 0.0918, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: bilateral tension resists trunk extension and rotational drift so the body remains a rigid link between the pressing arms and the hanging load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.50}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.9000, "E_mech": 0.4860, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Genuine task torque rather than stabilisation: the scapulae must be actively depressed to hold the torso up between the bars, and its depression vector matches that requirement directly.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.28}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4500, "E_mech": 0.1418, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular fixation against the thorax is required to route 1000 N from the arms into the trunk, with no bench or pad to absorb any of the reaction force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.11}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.4190, "muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Best-aligned pressing role it receives in the library: its scapular depression action directly matches the requirement to hold the body up between the bars, unlike in bench pressing where it contributes only anchoring force.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.53}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.3500, "E_mech": 0.0937, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Included for its scapular and shoulder-girdle depression contribution, which is mechanically required to support the body between the bars. Its humeral extension action opposes the press itself, which is why alignment is low.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.11}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3500, "E_LoA": 0.3400, "E_mech": 0.0607, "muscle": "adductor_longus_brevis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: active adduction holds the legs together so the dip belt load remains a single rigid mass rather than two independently swinging limbs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 66.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.04}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.4800, "E_mech": 0.1296, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk into a rigid link between the pressing arms and the suspended belt load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.85}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["110 kg is interpreted as 85 kg body mass plus 25 kg of added external load on a dip belt.", "The forearms and hands are fixed on the bars and are not lifted by the pressing musculature, so the moved mass is ~102 kg, giving ~1000 N effective after a small allowance for controlled acceleration.", "Parallel bars at roughly shoulder width, hands beside the hips in a neutral grip.", "Deliberate forward torso lean of ~30 degrees with the hips flexed and the feet swept back, which is the defining feature of the chest-emphasis variant.", "The forward lean increases the horizontal distance from the glenohumeral axis to the hand support line to ~0.11 m per side, raising the shoulder moment relative to an upright dip.", "Forearms held near-vertical at the bottom, giving an elbow external moment arm of ~0.048 m per side.", "Internal moment arms: humeral flexion and horizontal adduction ~0.05 m, triceps elbow extension ~0.025 m, scapular depressors ~0.055 m.", "The required humeral action is flexion and adduction from a deeply extended position, which is the mirror of a decline press and favours the sternal pectoral fibres.", "Scapular depression is genuine task torque: the body must be actively held up between the bars against elevation, and the scapulae must be prevented from riding toward the ears under 1000 N.", "The dip belt hangs freely at ~0.35 m below the pelvis and the legs are unsupported, so active lumbopelvic torque is required to hold the body rigid and prevent swing.", "The hands press down on fixed bars in compression rather than suspending the body, so grip is not a meaningful load-transmission bottleneck.", "110 kg total is a demanding but realistic 8-12 rep load for an advanced athlete of this mass, equating to roughly 29 percent added bodyweight."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 110.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 48.00}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 33.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 30.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 110.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 1000.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 193.52, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 193.50}
17	standing_cable_pallof_press	Pallof Press	Standing Cable Pallof Press	Core	Core	Stability_Isometric	Cable	20.00	74.62	{"obliques": 26.96, "rotator_cuffs": 4.27, "erector_spinae": 6.39, "gluteus_medius": 6.83, "gluteus_maximus": 2.86, "gluteus_minimus": 1.49, "deltoid_anterior": 5.03, "rectus_abdominis": 8.99, "serratus_anterior": 3.60, "triceps_long_head": 1.31, "triceps_medial_head": 1.06, "transverse_abdominis": 3.30, "triceps_lateral_head": 2.53}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_cable_pallof_press", "notes": ["This is the first explicitly isometric exercise in the library and the first anti-rotation movement. The prompt provides for isometric evaluation, and the limiting phase is the hold at full extension rather than any concentric action, since the press-out itself moves negligible load. Any future anti-rotation or carry variant should follow this treatment.", "The bilateral convention required a different reading here than in single_arm_low_to_high_cable_fly. There, the two mirrored cable lines cancelled and the anti-rotation demand vanished from the model, which was flagged as a limitation. Here the anti-rotation demand IS the exercise, so 20 kg was read as a single stack acting on one side rather than as two mirrored 10 kg loads. Mirroring it would produce a null exercise. This is a genuine inconsistency in the convention rather than a modelling choice, and it argues for a laterality flag distinguishing bilateral, unilateral-alternating, and unilateral-asymmetric exercises.", "Obliques reach 26.96 cm2, more than triple their 8.60 cm2 in the Pendlay row and their largest contribution anywhere. E_LoA was capped near 0.45 because the aggregate slug contains internal and external obliques on both sides, and only two of those four act in the required anti-rotation direction at any instant. Splitting the slug into internal and external, or into left and right, would model this and any rotational exercise more accurately.", "Transverse_abdominis reaches E_mech 0.1500, its highest value in the library, against 0.1300 in the weighted pull-up and 0.0670 in the shrug. Its circumferential fibres generate hoop tension that resists torsion specifically, so anti-rotation is the one demand where it is doing close to its designed job rather than merely offloading a sagittal-plane moment.", "Gluteus_medius and gluteus_minimus appear for the first time in the library, admitted on frontal-plane grounds. The cable pulls the athlete laterally toward the stack and the pelvis must resist that for the trunk to remain a stable platform. Any future single-leg, carry, or lateral-loaded exercise should admit them on the same basis.", "Total demand of 74.62 cm2 sits just above the bodyweight crunch at 36.40 and below the incline curl at 76.66. The comparison with the crunch is the useful one: both are trunk exercises, but the crunch generates 47.5 N-m of sagittal flexion through body-segment mass while this generates 44 N-m of rotation through a 0.45 m arm lever from only 98 N of cable force. The demands are comparable in magnitude but distributed across entirely different musculature.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded. The hands are clasped together around the handle rather than gripping against a distracting force, and at 98 N the demand is trivial.", "A half-kneeling or tall-kneeling Pallof press must not inherit these values. Removing the standing base eliminates the gluteus medius and minimus frontal-plane contribution of 8.32 cm2 and shifts more of the anti-rotation demand onto the obliques directly.", "A Pallof press with hold at varying arm extension must not inherit these values either. The trunk moment scales directly with arm extension distance, so a hold at the chest generates roughly a third of the moment of a hold at full extension.", "A cable woodchop or rotational chop must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Those are concentric rotational movements where the obliques shorten actively against the load, rather than isometric anti-rotation, which changes both the limiting phase and the length-tension conditions."], "limiting_phase": "Isometric hold at full arm extension: hands pressed straight out from the sternum, elbows locked, where the cable line sits at its greatest horizontal distance from the trunk axis and the rotational moment on the torso is maximal.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 74.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.4540, "E_mech": 0.3300, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Prime mover and the defining muscle of the exercise: resisting trunk rotation is its primary function and the 44 N-m moment is the dominant demand. E_LoA is capped near 0.45 because the slug aggregates both sides and both layers, and only the internal oblique on the near side and external oblique on the far side act in the required anti-rotation direction at any instant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 26.96}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6050, "E_mech": 0.3702, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Genuine contributor rather than a stabiliser: it anchors the anterior trunk against both the rotational moment and the forward pull of the extended arms, providing the rigid base the obliques act against.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 8.99}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1670, "E_mech": 0.0601, "muscle": "gluteus_medius", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the 24 N-m frontal-plane moment pulling the athlete toward the stack must be resisted at the pelvis for the trunk to remain a stable platform. Its abduction line only partly matches the required resistance direction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 113.75, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.83}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1570, "E_mech": 0.0600, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: its deep rotatores and multifidus components contribute directly to anti-rotation, and the superficial fibres hold the upright trunk against the forward arm lever. Most of the group's capacity is irrelevant to the transverse-plane demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.39}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.3820, "E_mech": 0.1651, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it holds the arms extended forward against the 12 N-m moment that would otherwise collapse them back toward the chest, maintaining the long lever the exercise depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.03}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.3000, "E_LoA": 0.1635, "E_mech": 0.0250, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: the extended-arm position with continuous lateral cable tension requires cuff compression to keep the humeral heads centred while force transmits from hands to trunk.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.27}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.2880, "E_mech": 0.0999, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting stabiliser: scapular protraction and fixation are required to keep the arms as a rigid strut and route the cable force into the thorax rather than losing it to scapular retraction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.60}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.4050, "E_mech": 0.1500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Higher relative engagement than in any other exercise here: its circumferential fibres generate the intra-abdominal pressure and hoop tension that stiffen the trunk cylinder against torsion specifically, which is the exact demand of this movement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.30}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0740, "E_mech": 0.0200, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Included for its external-rotation and pelvic-stabilising contribution, which resists the pelvis being pulled into rotation by the cable. Its dominant extension function is irrelevant to the transverse-plane demand, hence the very low alignment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1730, "E_mech": 0.0701, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: locking the elbows in extension is required for the arms to act as the rigid lever that generates the 44 N-m trunk moment. Purely isometric with no movement against the load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.53}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1670, "E_mech": 0.0601, "muscle": "gluteus_minimus", "rationale": "Acts in parallel with gluteus medius on frontal-plane pelvic stability against the lateral cable pull.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.82, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.49}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.0834, "E_mech": 0.0301, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Assists the isometric elbow lock, with its shoulder-extension action partly opposing the forward arm position the exercise requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.31}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1680, "E_mech": 0.0680, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Mechanically equivalent to the lateral head in maintaining the elbow lock.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.06}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio; 20 kg nominal yields ~98 N at the handle after friction.", "Athlete standing side-on to a chest-height pulley in a hip-width or slightly staggered stance, hands clasped and pressed straight out from the sternum.", "The exercise is an anti-rotation isometric: the concentric press-out is trivial and the mechanically limiting phase is the hold at full extension, evaluated as an isometric per the prompt's provision for explicitly isometric tasks.", "Arm length from sternum to hands at full extension ~0.45 m; the cable line therefore acts at ~0.45 m from the trunk rotation axis, giving ~44 N-m of rotational moment.", "The same 98 N also pulls the athlete laterally toward the stack, creating a ~24 N-m frontal-plane moment at the pelvis that the lateral hip musculature must resist to keep the stance stable.", "Oblique effective rotation moment arm ~0.055 m; the internal and external obliques on opposite sides act as a couple to resist rotation.", "Shoulder and elbow moments are small: the arms act largely as a rigid strut transmitting cable tension to the sternum, requiring modest deltoid and triceps force to hold extension rather than to move the load.", "This is a unilateral-stimulus exercise evaluated bilaterally. Unlike the single-arm cable fly, where the bilateral convention cancelled the anti-rotation demand, here the anti-rotation demand IS the exercise, so the 20 kg is read as a single stack acting on one side rather than as two mirrored 10 kg loads.", "Feet planted with the reaction force transmitted through the legs to the floor; the gluteal contribution is genuine frontal-plane and rotational torque rather than postural support.", "20 kg is a realistic working load for a 20 to 40 second hold or 8-12 slow press-outs, since the very long arm lever converts modest cable force into a demanding trunk moment."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "thoracolumbar_spine_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 44.00}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic_frontal", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 24.00}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 12.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 7.80}], "nominal_load_kg": 20.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 98.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 74.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 74.50}
23	smith_machine_standing_calf_raise	Standing Calf Raise	Smith Machine Standing Calf Raise	Lower	Calves	Isolation	Smith_Machine	120.00	240.70	{"soleus": 129.66, "gastrocnemius": 111.04}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "smith_machine_standing_calf_raise", "notes": ["This exercise is the loaded version of the prompt's Example 1 calibration point, which gives 401.46 cm2 for an idealized Smith standing calf raise with both muscles at E_mech 1.00. The result here is 240.70 cm2, exactly 60 percent of that ceiling. Working backward, the 401.46 figure corresponds to roughly 327 kg of total system mass, or about 240 kg on the bar for this athlete, which is a plausible plantar flexion maximum. The example is explicitly an idealized upper bound rather than a value every setup must reproduce, and 120 kg is a moderate load against it.", "E_mech was set by the top-down requirement rather than by the dimensional product, the same handling used at bodyweight_neck_extension. The four factors multiply to roughly 0.86 for soleus and 0.86 for gastrocnemius, which would allocate 345 cm2 against an external demand of 240. This is now the second exercise where capacity greatly exceeds task demand and the multiplication rule overstates utilisation. The proposed prompt amendment stands: after computing the dimensional product, cap each contribution so the group total satisfies but does not exceed the top-down target.", "The 120 kg was read as external bar load with body mass added on top, since the resistance source is Smith_Machine rather than Bodyweight. Reading it as total system mass instead would give roughly 148 cm2, a 38 percent difference. This is the same ambiguity flagged at the weighted pull-up and dip, arriving from the opposite direction: there a bodyweight exercise carried added load, here a machine exercise unavoidably carries body mass. A single added_load_kg column would resolve both cases.", "This is the only exercise in the library whose vector contains nothing but prime movers. Example 1 explicitly excludes balance, trunk bracing and postural stabilisation, and the fixed rail plus near-pure axial bar position means there is genuinely no stabiliser with meaningful net task torque. Erector spinae was considered and excluded: with an upright torso under axial compression the spinal moment arm is close to zero.", "Total demand of 240.70 cm2 ranks third in the library behind barbell_pendlay_row at 579.05 and weighted_neutral_grip_pull_up at 245.71, despite involving a single joint and two muscles. The plantar flexors have the largest combined FCSA of any functional group in the table at 401.46 cm2, and the ankle's unfavourable 0.15 to 0.048 external-to-internal lever ratio means every kilogram lifted costs roughly three times the tendon force it would at a joint with neutral leverage.", "The Achilles internal moment arm is the single most sensitive input. Individual variation runs roughly 0.040 to 0.060 m, and the total scales inversely with it: 0.040 m would give 289 cm2 and 0.060 m would give 192 cm2. No other assumption in this entry moves the result by comparable magnitude.", "A seated calf raise must not inherit these values under any circumstances. The prompt's own guidance places gastrocnemius at E_LT 0.20 to 0.40 with a flexed knee, which would cut its contribution by roughly two thirds and shift the exercise to a near-pure soleus movement at a substantially lower total.", "A leg press calf raise must not inherit these values either. The sled angle changes the effective load relative to the nominal plate weight, and the seated position removes body mass from the moved load entirely.", "A standing calf raise machine with shoulder pads must not inherit these values. Pad-driven machines often use a lever ratio that makes the nominal stack differ substantially from the load at the shoulders, unlike the Smith machine's direct 1:1 vertical bar.", "Toes-in and toes-out variants would redistribute load between the medial and lateral gastrocnemius heads, which the aggregate gastrocnemius slug cannot represent. This is a fourth case, after rotator_cuffs, obliques and the missing levator scapulae, where an aggregated slug limits the model's resolution."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the fully stretched bottom: heels dropped below the platform into maximal dorsiflexion with the knees extended, where the plantar flexors are longest and the Achilles internal moment arm is at its smallest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 240.70, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9500, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.5950, "muscle": "soleus", "rationale": "Prime mover with perfect task alignment: uniarticular plantarflexion is its sole function and the extended-knee position costs it nothing. All four dimensions are high, but E_mech is set by the top-down requirement rather than by their product, because the 287 N-m ankle moment demands only about 60 percent of the combined plantar flexor capacity available.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 217.92, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 129.66}, {"E_LT": 1.0000, "E_MA": 0.9200, "E_ND": 0.9300, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6050, "muscle": "gastrocnemius", "rationale": "Co-prime mover at its optimal length: the extended knee places it at E_LT 1.00 per the prompt's own calibration guidance, which is the defining advantage of the standing variant over the seated one. Like soleus it is demand-constrained rather than capacity-constrained at this load.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 183.54, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 111.04}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The resistance source is the Smith machine, so 120 kg is the external bar load only and the athlete's body mass must be added: the ankle plantar flexors lift body plus bar, not bar alone.", "Representative commercial Smith machine with a partially counterbalanced carriage: effective bar load ~112 kg after counterbalance and rail friction, on a rail that is essentially vertical for this movement.", "Body mass 85 kg less ~1.8 kg for the feet distal to the ankle axis, which are not lifted by the plantar flexors: ~83.2 kg of body mass moved.", "Total moved mass ~195.2 kg, giving ~1915 N effective, split as ~958 N per foot.", "Forefoot on a raised platform with the ground reaction acting through the metatarsal heads; external moment arm from the ankle axis ~0.15 m per side at the dorsiflexed bottom.", "Achilles internal moment arm ~0.048 m per side at the dorsiflexed limiting position, reduced from the ~0.055 m available in plantarflexion.", "Knees held extended throughout, which places gastrocnemius at its optimal operating length per the prompt's own calibration guidance.", "The fixed rail removes all balance and lateral stabilisation demand, and the bar rests on the shoulders under near-pure axial compression with an upright torso, so no meaningful spinal or hip moment is generated.", "120 kg plus body mass corresponds to roughly 60 percent of the estimated plantar flexion ceiling for this athlete, which is a moderate rather than maximal 8-12 rep load."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "ankle_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 287.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 120.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 1915.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 240.70, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 240.00}
24	roman_chair_back_extension_isometric_hold	Roman Chair Isometric Hold	Roman Chair Back Extension Isometric Hold	Lower	Global_P	Stability_Isometric	Bodyweight	45.00	182.92	{"erector_spinae": 63.46, "semitendinosus": 5.32, "adductor_magnus": 18.19, "gluteus_maximus": 61.65, "semimembranosus": 22.09, "biceps_femoris_long_head": 12.21}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "roman_chair_back_extension_isometric_hold", "notes": ["The hip-extensor contributions correspond to approximately 147 Nm using representative muscle-specific hip-extension moment arms, closely matching the estimated 150 Nm external hip moment.", "The erector-spinae contribution corresponds to approximately 95 Nm using an effective lumbar-extension moment arm near 0.060 m, consistent with the estimated 92.70 Nm external lumbar moment.", "Rectus abdominis, obliques, and transverse abdominis are excluded because their principal sagittal-plane moments oppose lumbar extension; any co-contraction is not counted as task-producing FCSA demand.", "The result is highly sensitive to pad placement and segment centre-of-mass distance; positioning the pad farther down the thighs would materially increase the hip-extension requirement."], "limiting_phase": "Static horizontal hold with the pelvis supported at the Roman-chair pad, the trunk and head maintained approximately parallel to the floor, and the hips held near neutral extension.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 182.92, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8500, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.9500, "E_mech": 0.5959, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Provides the principal lumbar-extension moment required to prevent the suspended trunk from flexing under gravity.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 63.46}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7200, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.4308, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Supplies the largest hip-extension contribution, although its leverage and neural utilization are lower near neutral hip extension than in deeper hip flexion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 61.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.7200, "E_mech": 0.2488, "muscle": "semimembranosus", "rationale": "Contributes meaningful isometric hip-extension torque while the nearly extended knee preserves adequate biarticular hamstring length.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 88.78, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 22.09}, {"E_LT": 0.8200, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.6200, "E_mech": 0.1729, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Posterior fibers assist hip extension, but the aggregate muscle includes fibers whose actions are less aligned with the sagittal-plane holding demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.19}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5800, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.2274, "muscle": "biceps_femoris_long_head", "rationale": "Assists the gluteus maximus in resisting hip flexion, with contribution limited by its smaller FCSA and secondary task share.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.70, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 12.21}, {"E_LT": 0.7800, "E_MA": 0.6800, "E_ND": 0.5600, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.2079, "muscle": "semitendinosus", "rationale": "Provides a small but mechanically direct hip-extension contribution during the static horizontal hold.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 25.59, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.32}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 45 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective suspended mass of the head, arms, trunk, and mechanically relevant portion of the lower limbs, not as 45 kg of additional external weight.", "The suspended-segment centre of mass acts approximately 0.34 m anterior to the bilateral hip axes.", "The effective upper-trunk centre of mass acts approximately 0.21 m anterior to the representative lumbar-extension axis.", "The Roman-chair pad supports the proximal thighs and pelvis without materially assisting hip or lumbar extension.", "The spine is held near neutral rather than deliberately hyperextended."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_extension_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 150.09}, {"joint": "lumbar_extension", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 92.70}], "nominal_load_kg": 45.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 441.45}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 182.92, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 182.92}
14	standing_barbell_curl	Barbell Curl	Standing Barbell Curl	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Barbell	40.00	87.35	{"brachialis": 32.98, "wrist_flexors": 4.35, "biceps_brachii": 27.87, "erector_spinae": 6.29, "brachioradialis": 6.85, "deltoid_anterior": 6.06, "rectus_abdominis": 1.32, "transverse_abdominis": 1.63}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "standing_barbell_curl", "notes": ["This completes a five-point curl series and gives the highest total of the group at 87.35 cm2, against Bayesian 85.10, incline 76.66, supinated preacher 68.68 and hammer preacher 47.31. The mechanism is straightforward: standing loads the strongest portion of the strength curve with the limiting phase at 90 degrees where internal leverage peaks, so it permits 40 kg where the preacher permits 28. Total demand tracks load more than it tracks any efficiency advantage.", "Biceps_brachii at E_mech 0.7000 sits below the Bayesian curl at 0.8123 and above the preacher at 0.6798, exactly where shoulder position predicts. The brachialis-to-biceps ratio of 1.18 places standing between preacher at 1.14 and incline at 1.11, all three clustered because none of them either stretches or shortens the long head substantially. Only the Bayesian variant at 0.96 and the hammer preacher at 0.44 sit outside that cluster, and both for identifiable reasons.", "Pronators_supinators was deliberately excluded, which is the clearest structural difference from the dumbbell curls. A straight barbell is symmetric about the grip and applies no net rotational moment to the forearms, whereas an offset dumbbell mass applies a continuous pronation moment requiring active resistance. This removes 5.11 to 6.55 cm2 relative to the dumbbell variants and is the correct treatment for any barbell or EZ-bar curl.", "Erector_spinae, transverse_abdominis and rectus_abdominis together contribute 9.24 cm2 that the seated and preacher variants entirely lack. The standing position with 398 N held ahead of the spine generates a real 34 N-m lumbar moment, and this is the mechanical reason strict standing curls are commonly reported as taxing the lower back at heavy loads. Evaluated under strict technique; a version performed with deliberate hip drive would move substantially further in this direction and admit hip extensors as genuine contributors.", "The elbow flexors reach 67.70 cm2 against 95.08 available, giving 71 percent utilisation, the same signature as the incline and Bayesian curls. No curl in the library saturates its prime movers, which is expected for a single-joint exercise where the athlete can select the load freely.", "Wrist_flexors at 4.35 cm2 is included but marginal. A barbell rests across the palms with the wrists in a mechanically supported position, unlike a pulldown or row where the bar hangs from the fingers. If the stabiliser criterion is tightened, this and the rectus_abdominis entry are the two most droppable in this row.", "An EZ-bar curl must not inherit these values. The semi-supinated grip would reduce biceps_brachii leverage and raise brachioradialis, sitting between this and the hammer variants, while leaving the trunk contribution unchanged.", "A cheat curl or deliberately swung barbell curl must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Hip drive changes the limiting phase, admits the hip extensors and erectors as genuine prime movers, and permits a substantially heavier load than the elbow flexors alone could handle.", "A wide or narrow grip barbell curl must not inherit these values either. Grip width alters the degree of forced supination at the wrist and changes the humeral abduction angle slightly, redistributing between biceps and brachialis.", "A seated barbell curl must not inherit these values. Sitting removes the 9.24 cm2 of trunk contribution and typically permits marginally less load by eliminating any residual body English."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 90 degrees of elbow flexion, where the forearm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the elbow axis to the bar is at its maximum.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 87.35, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9200, "E_LoA": 0.8560, "E_mech": 0.7487, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Largest contributor to the 59.7 N-m elbow flexion moment, at near-optimal length and leverage at the 90 degree limiting angle and unaffected by shoulder or forearm position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.98}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.9500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.7000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "The fully supinated grip gives excellent alignment and near-optimal elbow leverage, but the neutral hanging shoulder position offers none of the long-head stretch that the incline and Bayesian variants provide, which is the only dimension holding it below brachialis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 27.87}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.8860, "E_mech": 0.6102, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Well positioned at the 90 degree limiting angle where its moment arm peaks, though the fully supinated grip is less favourable for it than a neutral one.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.85}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.1327, "E_mech": 0.0591, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: 398 N held ahead of the spine requires active lumbar extension torque to maintain the upright standing position that the elbow geometry depends on.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.29}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.5680, "E_mech": 0.1991, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary stabiliser: it holds the humerus against the 16 N-m moment that would otherwise pull the upper arms into extension, maintaining the fixed elbow position strict form requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.06}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2350, "E_mech": 0.0799, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element: grip force is required for the 398 N to reach the forearm, though the bar rests across the palms rather than hanging from the fingers, so demand is well below any pulling exercise.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.35}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.2750, "E_mech": 0.0743, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates the intra-abdominal pressure that stiffens the trunk against the forward-hanging load, assisting the erectors in maintaining the upright position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.63}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2130, "E_mech": 0.0543, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Small contribution to trunk rigidity, resisting the extension tendency as the bar is curled and the athlete instinctively leans back.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.32}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Barbell weight 392 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~398 N effective.", "Standing upright with the upper arms hanging at the sides and held approximately vertical, strict technique with no deliberate torso swing or hip drive.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to bar ~0.150 m; at the horizontal-forearm limiting phase the full length acts as the external moment arm.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.042 m at 90 degrees, close to maximal for this joint.", "Fixed supinated grip at approximately shoulder width, which is the defining constraint of a straight barbell: the forearms cannot rotate independently and the wrists are locked into full supination.", "The upper arm hangs approximately vertical, so the glenohumeral external moment is small and arises from holding the humerus in place rather than from any required shoulder motion.", "Standing unsupported with 398 N held in front of the body: a genuine lumbar extension moment of ~34 N-m is required to hold the upright torso, since the load line sits ahead of the spine.", "A straight bar is symmetric about the grip and applies no net rotational moment to the forearms, unlike a dumbbell, so pronators_supinators are not required as a load-transmitting element.", "Double-overhand grip with the bar resting across the palms rather than suspended from the fingers, so grip demand is modest relative to a hanging or pulling exercise.", "40 kg is a realistic 8-12 rep working load for an advanced 85 kg athlete performing strict standing curls, and is the heaviest curl load in the library because the standing position loads the strongest portion of the strength curve."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 59.70}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 16.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 34.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 40.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 398.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 87.35, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 87.00}
49	weighted_russian_twist	Weighted Russian Twist	Seated Weighted Russian Twist	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Other	15.00	63.45	{"obliques": 42.52, "iliopsoas": 14.17, "rectus_abdominis": 6.76}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "weighted_russian_twist", "notes": ["The oblique contribution can generate approximately 48 Nm of axial-rotation moment using a representative effective rotational moment arm near 0.045 m, closely matching the estimated external rotational demand.", "The exercise combines dynamic axial rotation with concurrent isometric hip-flexion and lumbar-flexion requirements; these demands are evaluated at the same reversal phase rather than summed from separate points in the repetition.", "Transverse abdominis is excluded because its principal role is abdominal compression and segmental stabilization rather than production of a meaningful net axial-rotation or flexion moment.", "Erector spinae is excluded because its sagittal-plane action opposes the required flexion moment; antagonist co-contraction is not counted as task-producing FCSA demand.", "The estimate is sensitive to torso recline, arm extension, foot support, rotation amplitude, and whether the weight is moved through true trunk rotation or primarily by the shoulders."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric reversal from approximately 35-45 degrees of trunk rotation with the torso reclined, where the weight is farthest from the spinal axis and rotational acceleration is initiated while the reclined posture is maintained.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 63.45, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.8600, "E_mech": 0.5204, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The internal and external oblique aggregate supplies the principal axial-rotation moment while concurrently assisting maintenance of the flexed and reclined trunk position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 42.52}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.5500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.7000, "E_mech": 0.1386, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Produces a mechanically necessary isometric hip-flexion moment that prevents the reclined torso from extending about the supported pelvis.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 14.17}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.5500, "E_LoA": 0.8500, "E_mech": 0.2782, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Resists lumbar extension caused by the reclined upper-body mass, but does not provide the primary axial-rotation torque.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.76}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The athlete holds a 15 kg plate or compact weight with both arms extended approximately 0.23 m from the trunk rotational axis.", "The estimated effective force includes the external weight and the mechanically relevant contribution of the forearms and hands.", "The torso remains reclined approximately 35 degrees from vertical with the heels lightly supported on the floor.", "Upper-body mass creates additional sagittal-plane extension moments about the hips and lumbar spine that must be resisted isometrically.", "The athlete rotates through the trunk rather than merely moving the arms across the body."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "trunk_axial_rotation", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 50.77}, {"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 55.00}, {"joint": "lumbar_flexion_isometric", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 27.00}], "nominal_load_kg": 15.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 225.63}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 63.45, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 63.45}
16	hanging_leg_raise	Hanging Leg Raise	Straight-Leg Hanging Leg Raise	Core	Core	Secondary_Compound	Bodyweight	32.00	131.62	{"obliques": 16.79, "iliopsoas": 66.03, "sartorius": 2.84, "rectus_femoris": 34.21, "rectus_abdominis": 11.75}	{}	{}	{}	Bilateral	{"slug": "hanging_leg_raise", "notes": ["The hip-flexor contributions generate approximately 105 Nm of bilateral hip-flexion capacity using representative internal moment arms near 0.040-0.045 m.", "The abdominal contributions generate approximately 35 Nm of lumbopelvic flexion capacity using representative effective moment arms near 0.045-0.055 m.", "A version that stops with the legs at horizontal without posterior pelvic rotation would have lower abdominal contribution and would function more nearly as a pure hip-flexion exercise.", "Latissimus dorsi, forearm muscles, and rotator cuffs are excluded because their hanging and shoulder-stabilization roles do not generate a meaningful share of the required hip-flexion or lumbopelvic-flexion moments.", "The estimate is sensitive to leg length, knee extension, pelvic rotation, range of motion, and the amount of momentum used."], "limiting_phase": "Late concentric elevation as the straight legs approach horizontal, where the lower-limb centre of mass has its largest perpendicular distance from the hip axes and posterior pelvic rotation begins against the suspended trunk.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 131.62, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.8200, "E_LoA": 0.9700, "E_mech": 0.6459, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Provides the dominant bilateral hip-flexion moment, with strong task alignment and favorable leverage as the legs approach horizontal.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 66.03}, {"E_LT": 0.7400, "E_MA": 0.7800, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.3741, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Contributes substantial hip-flexion torque, but the extended knee shortens it distally and reduces its available force relative to iliopsoas.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 34.21}, {"E_LT": 0.8800, "E_MA": 0.7200, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.6500, "E_mech": 0.2055, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Produces a meaningful bilateral posterior-pelvic-rotation and trunk-flexion moment while limiting lumbar extension under the elevated legs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 16.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8200, "E_ND": 0.6800, "E_LoA": 0.9600, "E_mech": 0.4838, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Directly produces posterior pelvic rotation and lumbopelvic flexion as the legs pass beyond a simple horizontal hip-flexion position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.75}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.6200, "E_ND": 0.6000, "E_LoA": 0.9300, "E_mech": 0.2768, "muscle": "sartorius", "rationale": "Provides a small task-aligned hip-flexion contribution, limited by its low FCSA and secondary role relative to iliopsoas and rectus femoris.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.84}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 32 kg nominal load is interpreted as the effective combined mass of both straight lower limbs rather than additional external weight.", "The lower-limb centre of mass acts approximately 0.335 m from the bilateral hip axes when the legs approach horizontal.", "The knees remain nearly extended and the ankles remain relaxed throughout the limiting phase.", "The athlete completes the repetition with active posterior pelvic rotation rather than stopping at a pure hip-flexion hold.", "Swinging, shoulder pulldown assistance, and momentum are excluded."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "hip_flexion_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 105.16}, {"joint": "lumbopelvic_flexion", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 34.62}], "nominal_load_kg": 32.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 313.92}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 131.62, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 131.62}
15	preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl	Preacher Hammer Curl	Single-Arm Dumbbell Hammer Curl on Preacher Bench	Upper	Biceps	Isolation	Dumbbell	16.00	47.31	{"brachialis": 25.63, "biceps_brachii": 11.27, "brachioradialis": 7.55, "pronators_supinators": 2.86}	{}	{}	{}	Unilateral	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["This exercise produces the sharpest brachialis to biceps divergence in the library, at a 2.27 ratio against 1.11 in the incline dumbbell curl. Two independent factors stack against the biceps: the neutral grip removes its supination leverage and the preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, shortening the long head. The incline curl is the exact mechanical opposite on the second factor, which makes the pair a clean demonstration that shoulder position, not just grip, determines biceps allocation.", "Brachioradialis reaches E_mech 0.6413, its highest value anywhere, exceeding the neutral-grip pulldown at 0.6543 only marginally and the supinated incline curl at 0.6148 more clearly. The neutral grip is its optimal orientation and the model reproduces the common observation that hammer curls emphasise it without any special-casing.", "The limiting phase was placed at the stretched bottom rather than the mid-range, which departs from the incline curl where 90 degrees was limiting. Preacher geometry is the reason: the 45 degree pad puts the forearm near horizontal at full extension, so the maximal external moment arm coincides with the worst internal leverage. This is the mechanical basis for preacher curls being perceived as hardest at the bottom, and it is why E_MA sits at 0.70 for brachialis here against 0.95 in the incline curl.", "Total demand of 47.31 cm2 against 76.66 for the incline dumbbell curl comes from a 43 percent lighter load, which is itself the mechanically correct consequence of loading the weakest point of the strength curve with no momentum available. The prompt's own preacher calibration example uses 28 kg; at that load this variant would demand roughly 82 cm2, which suggests 28 kg is closer to a supinated preacher curl than a hammer preacher curl for the reference athlete.", "The result sits within the prompt's stated preacher curl bands for brachialis at 0.5818 against 0.65 to 0.90, marginally below, and above the stated brachioradialis band of 0.15 to 0.45. Both deviations are attributable to the hammer grip rather than to the preacher position, since those bands were specified for a supinated dumbbell preacher curl.", "Deltoid_anterior was deliberately excluded, in contrast to the incline dumbbell curl where it contributed 2.69 cm2 holding the humerus against 11 N-m of extension moment. The preacher pad absorbs the entire shoulder moment mechanically, so no active shoulder torque is required at all.", "Wrist_flexors were deliberately excluded. At 159 N total the grip demand is trivial and the dumbbell handle sits in the palm rather than being suspended from a hook grip, so grip is not a meaningful transmission bottleneck.", "A supinated preacher curl at the same load must not inherit these values. Supination would roughly double the biceps allocation, cut brachioradialis by about a third, and raise pronators_supinators demand.", "An EZ-bar or barbell preacher curl must not inherit these values either. A symmetric implement removes the rotational moment that admits pronators_supinators here, and the fixed bar prevents independent forearm positioning."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from the deeply extended bottom: elbow near 160 degrees of extension with the upper arm fixed against the preacher pad, where the pad angle places the forearm close to horizontal and the external moment arm is greatest.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 47.31, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9800, "E_MA": 0.7000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9420, "E_mech": 0.5818, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Dominant flexor in this variant: maximally lengthened at the extended bottom and entirely unaffected by the neutral forearm position that penalises the biceps. Moment arm is poor at 160 degrees of extension, which is the binding constraint rather than any alignment issue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 25.63}, {"E_LT": 0.8000, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.8500, "E_LoA": 0.6400, "E_mech": 0.2830, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Doubly penalised: the neutral grip removes its supination advantage and reduces its effective flexion leverage, while the preacher pad holds the shoulder in flexion, shortening the long head across that joint. This is its weakest position of any elbow-flexion exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 11.27}, {"E_LT": 0.9500, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 1.0000, "E_mech": 0.6413, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Highest engagement it receives anywhere in the library: the neutral hammer grip is its optimal forearm orientation and it is well lengthened at the extended bottom, partly offsetting the poor moment arm at that joint angle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 7.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.2125, "E_mech": 0.0470, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the offset dumbbell mass applies a rotational moment that must be actively resisted to hold the neutral position the exercise geometry depends on. Demand is roughly half that of a supinated dumbbell curl because mid-position is the forearm's mechanically neutral resting orientation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 2.86}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention: 16 kg total is interpreted as 8 kg per arm.", "Dumbbell weight 157 N plus ~1.5 percent for controlled acceleration: ~159 N effective, ~80 N per limb.", "Standard preacher bench with the pad inclined at ~45 degrees; the upper arm is fully supported and immobilised against the pad.", "Forearm length from elbow axis to dumbbell centre ~0.155 m; at the extended bottom position the forearm approaches horizontal and close to the full length acts as the external moment arm, giving ~0.150 m effective.", "Elbow flexor internal moment arm ~0.028 m at 160 degrees of extension, substantially reduced from the ~0.042 m available at 90 degrees.", "Neutral hammer grip held throughout: the forearm is in mid-position, which is the optimal orientation for brachioradialis and removes any supination demand from biceps_brachii.", "The preacher pad immobilises the humerus and absorbs all shoulder moment, so no glenohumeral or scapular torque is required and no deltoid contribution is needed.", "The bench and pad support the torso and upper arm fully, so no trunk musculature generates net task torque and no cheat-swing contribution is available.", "The limiting phase is the stretched bottom rather than the mid-range, because the preacher geometry combines a near-maximal external moment arm with the worst internal leverage of the range.", "16 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the preacher position eliminates momentum and loads the weakest portion of the strength curve, so it permits substantially less weight than a standing or incline curl."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 23.90}], "nominal_load_kg": 16.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 159.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 47.31, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 47.30}
5	single_arm_cable_lateral_raise	Cable Lateral Raise	Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise	Upper	Lateral_Delt	Isolation	Cable	24.00	87.29	{"obliques": 3.27, "brachialis": 1.55, "rotator_cuffs": 18.79, "wrist_flexors": 1.14, "deltoid_lateral": 32.65, "trapezius_lower": 4.09, "trapezius_upper": 6.19, "deltoid_anterior": 10.44, "deltoid_posterior": 3.62, "serratus_anterior": 5.55}	{}	{}	{}	Unilateral	{"slug": "single_arm_cable_lateral_raise", "notes": ["Deltoid_lateral reaches E_mech 0.7142, its highest value in the library by a factor of nearly five over the next exercise. Across the six presses it ranged from 0.0589 to 0.1499, always as a near-orthogonal contributor to a horizontal or forward push. This is the only exercise here that loads frontal-plane abduction directly, and the contrast is the clearest demonstration in the library that a large muscle contributes almost nothing when the task moment lies outside its line of action, regardless of load.", "Rotator_cuffs at 18.79 cm2 and E_mech 0.1100 is the highest cuff engagement in the library, above side_facing_reverse_pec_deck at 0.0861 and cable_face_pull at 0.0532. The reason differs from both: here supraspinatus is a true prime mover producing abduction directly, rather than infraspinatus and teres minor producing external rotation. This is the third distinct agonist role the aggregate slug has been asked to represent, and the case for splitting it into supraspinatus, external rotators, and subscapularis is now stronger than the case for any other schema change.", "The scapular upward-rotation couple appears here as genuine task torque for the first time in the library. Trapezius_upper, trapezius_lower and serratus_anterior together contribute 15.83 cm2, acting as a force couple where the upper and lower fibres oppose each other vertically to produce rotation rather than translation. In every previous exercise these muscles appeared either as retractors or as anchoring stabilisers. Any future overhead press or front raise should apply this treatment.", "Total demand of 87.29 cm2 from only 235 N of cable force reflects the 0.27 m external moment arm of a straight-arm raise. This is now the fifth straight-arm single-joint cable or machine movement to land between 85 and 110 cm2, alongside the straight-arm pulldown, the low-to-high fly, the reverse pec deck and the Bayesian curl. The band is consistent enough to serve as a plausibility check on future entries of this type.", "The bilateral convention was applied straightforwardly, as with the Bayesian curl. Lateral trunk flexion resistance is incidental here rather than central, so mirroring the load cancels nothing essential, unlike the Pallof press and side-facing reverse pec deck. This is the fourth data point for the proposed laterality flag and confirms that the distinction is between exercises where asymmetric loading is the point and exercises where it is a side effect.", "The limiting phase was placed at 80 to 90 degrees of abduction where the arm is horizontal. Above that point the deltoid shortens and the cable angle changes, and below it the moment arm is shorter; a dumbbell version would have a sharper peak at the same point but a much shorter loaded range, since dumbbell resistance falls to near zero at the bottom.", "A dumbbell lateral raise must not inherit these values. The moment arm collapses toward the bottom of the range, so the same nominal load produces a substantially lower average demand and the exercise typically permits a heavier top-end weight for the same rep count.", "A leaning cable lateral raise must not inherit these values either. Leaning away from the stack lengthens the effective moment arm at the bottom and shifts the peak demand earlier in the range, which changes both the limiting phase and the oblique contribution.", "A machine lateral raise with pads against the upper arm must not inherit these values under any circumstances. Pad contact at the upper arm rather than the hand roughly halves the external moment arm and removes the elbow and grip contributions entirely."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric mid-range at approximately 80 to 90 degrees of humeral abduction, where the near-straight arm is horizontal and the external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line is maximal.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 87.29, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.9000, "E_ND": 0.9000, "E_LoA": 0.9800, "E_mech": 0.7142, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Prime mover with near-perfect task alignment: pure humeral abduction in the frontal plane is its primary and near-exclusive function. By far its highest engagement in the library, against 0.1499 in the flat bench press and 0.0589 in the plate-loaded chest press where it was largely orthogonal to the required moment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 32.65}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6500, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.2655, "E_mech": 0.1100, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Included as a genuine contributor rather than purely a stabiliser: supraspinatus produces abduction directly and is a true agonist here, while the remaining cuff muscles depress and centre the humeral head so that deltoid force produces rotation rather than superior translation. Highest cuff engagement in the library, and the only exercise where supraspinatus is a prime mover.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 18.79}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.8000, "E_LoA": 0.5950, "E_mech": 0.3427, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Contributes a real abduction component from its lateral fibres, though its flexion vector is only partly aligned with the frontal-plane path the exercise requires.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 10.44}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.5920, "E_mech": 0.3197, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Genuine task torque: scapular upward rotation is required to elevate the humerus to horizontal, and the upper trapezius supplies the superior component of the force couple that produces it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 6.19}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3260, "E_mech": 0.1540, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Genuine contributor to the upward-rotation force couple alongside the trapezius, though its dominant protraction vector is only partly relevant to the required scapular motion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 5.55}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.7500, "E_LoA": 0.5060, "E_mech": 0.2732, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Supplies the inferior component of the scapular upward-rotation couple, opposing the elevation tendency of the upper trapezius so the scapula rotates rather than shrugs.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 4.09}, {"E_LT": 0.9000, "E_MA": 0.7500, "E_ND": 0.7000, "E_LoA": 0.3110, "E_mech": 0.1469, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Its posterior fibres contribute a modest abduction component at shoulder height, though its horizontal abduction vector is largely outside the frontal-plane path required.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.62}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.6000, "E_ND": 0.5000, "E_LoA": 0.1570, "E_mech": 0.0400, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Included as a mechanically necessary load-transmitting element: the cable pulls the torso laterally toward the stack, and active lateral flexion torque is required to hold the trunk upright so the shoulder has a stable base.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 3.27}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.4500, "E_ND": 0.4500, "E_LoA": 0.2050, "E_mech": 0.0353, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Included as a load-transmitting element holding the fixed near-straight elbow, with poor leverage at 170 degrees and a very small elbow moment relative to the shoulder demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.55}, {"E_LT": 0.8500, "E_MA": 0.8000, "E_ND": 0.4000, "E_LoA": 0.0770, "E_mech": 0.0209, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Included as a minor load-transmitting element: grip force is required for the 235 N to reach the arm, but the handle sits across the palm and the load is the lightest of any gripped exercise in the library.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 1.14}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["Bilateral convention applied as stated: 24 kg total is interpreted as 12 kg per arm, following the single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl precedent.", "Representative commercial single-stack cable column with a 1:1 pulley ratio and D-handle; 12 kg per side yields ~118 N per hand after friction, ~235 N total.", "Low pulley set at or near floor height, athlete standing side-on with the working arm across the body at the start, cable running under the torso to the opposite-side stack.", "Arm held near-straight at a fixed ~170 degree elbow; effective external moment arm from the glenohumeral axis to the cable line ~0.27 m at the horizontal limiting phase.", "The low cable angle keeps tension roughly constant through the range, unlike a dumbbell where the moment arm collapses toward the bottom, so the loaded portion of the range is substantially longer.", "Deltoid lateral internal abduction moment arm ~0.022 m at 80 to 90 degrees of abduction, which is close to its maximum for this joint action.", "Scapular upward rotation is genuine task torque: elevating the humerus to horizontal requires the scapula to rotate upward against the load, not merely to be held in place.", "The cable line runs close to the forearm axis rather than perpendicular to it, so the elbow moment is small and the elbow flexors act primarily as a rigid link.", "The athlete braces against the lateral pull with a modest counterlean transmitted through the legs to the floor; lower-limb musculature is postural and excluded, but a small oblique contribution resists lateral trunk flexion.", "24 kg total is a realistic 8-12 rep working load: the long straight-arm moment arm converts modest cable force into a demanding shoulder moment, which is why lateral raises are performed with light loads relative to other shoulder work."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 63.50}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 28.00}, {"joint": "elbow_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 4.20}], "nominal_load_kg": 24.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 235.00}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 87.29, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 87.00}
45	seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl	Dumbbell Wrist Curl	Seated Palms-Up Dumbbell Wrist Curl	Upper	Forearms	Isolation	Dumbbell	24.00	50.70	{"wrist_flexors": 50.70}	{}	{}	{}	Unilateral	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["The wrist-flexor aggregate approaches its modeled maximum capacity; E_mech is therefore near the upper limit rather than representing moderate utilization.", "The estimated contribution generates approximately 15.3 Nm of bilateral wrist-flexion torque using a representative internal wrist-flexor moment arm near 0.0121 m.", "The supplied 24 kg bilateral load is at the high end of a plausible strict 8-12 repetition working load for the reference athlete and requires full forearm support and controlled technique.", "Wrist extensors and pronators-supinators are excluded because any co-contraction or rotational stabilization does not produce a meaningful share of the required net wrist-flexion moment."], "limiting_phase": "Concentric initiation from moderate wrist extension, with the forearms fully supported on the thighs or bench and the dumbbells held near the distal fingers before the external wrist moment arm shortens.", "reconciliation": "PASS", "vector_sum_cm2": 50.70, "muscle_evaluation": [{"E_LT": 0.9700, "E_MA": 0.9800, "E_ND": 0.9900, "E_LoA": 0.9900, "E_mech": 0.9319, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "The wrist-flexor aggregate directly produces nearly all required wrist-flexion torque and operates with favorable length and leverage from the extended starting position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "fcsa_contribution_cm2": 50.70}], "effective_resistance": {"assumptions": ["The 24 kg nominal load represents two 12 kg dumbbells.", "The forearms remain fully supported, so the dumbbells create negligible meaningful elbow or shoulder demand.", "The combined perpendicular distance from the wrist axes to the dumbbell centres of mass is approximately 0.065 m at the limiting phase.", "The grip remains palms-up and the dumbbells are controlled without assistance from elbow flexion or forearm lifting.", "The wrist_flexors aggregate includes the principal wrist and finger flexors required to maintain the dumbbell grip while producing wrist-flexion torque."], "loaded_joints": [{"joint": "wrist_flexion_bilateral", "estimated_external_moment_nm": 15.31}], "nominal_load_kg": 24.00, "estimated_effective_force_n": 235.44}, "systemic_fcsa_demand_cm2": 50.70, "top_down_fcsa_target_cm2": 50.70}
