contribution is more compressive and anti-rotational and they are less exposed to end-range loaded lengthening.", "Elbow, grip and hip involvement is low-level positional isometry with full inter-repetition unloading, hence a uniform 0.95 rather than any isometric perfusion penalty.", "The intense acute burning typical of loaded abdominal work was excluded as a target variable; it resolves quickly and does not represent persistent restoration of force-producing readiness."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Contributes to trunk flexion through a controlled dynamic range with a resisted eccentric back to the extended position. Its role is partly compressive and anti-rotational rather than the primary spinal flexor, so it experiences less of the loaded lengthening at end-range extension than rectus abdominis, and a moderate tension fraction with no other distinguishing mechanism leaves the neutral reference appropriate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3264, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 26.67, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Unlike bodyweight crunching, the cable maintains meaningful resistance as the trunk returns into extension, so active force persists while the muscle is lengthened at the top of the movement. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.78 that adds a small nonlinear residual cost, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 because the achievable spinal extension range is anatomically limited and the absolute cable force is modest, so this is