 fixed sled path eliminate balance, path-control and deceleration demands, so force-development profile is controlled throughout and contributes nothing.", "No hypertrophy-quality or perceived-difficulty reasoning was applied; the leg press's reputation for systemic and cardiovascular fatigue is explicitly excluded from this local-recovery vector."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "vastus_lateralis", "rationale": "The sled carries substantial force into deep knee flexion, so meaningful active tension coincides with a moderately lengthened quadriceps position rather than resolving in mid-range. This mild high-force lengthening component is the specific reason for elevation. It stays small because the monoarticular vasti are not driven to extreme length, the guided sled path removes deceleration demands, and the back-supported position eliminates the balance-related eccentric spikes of a free-weight squat.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 243.17, "tension_fraction": 0.5852, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 142.30, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_deep_knee_flexion", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "Deep hip flexion at the bottom of the sled stroke places the hip extensors at substantially long length while the sled load remains high, and the eccentric is resisted throughout that range. This interaction of high force with active lengthening at long muscle length is the exercise's principal persistent-recovery mechanism, with a tension fraction of 0.72 adding a small nonlinear residual. The lengthened-position and eccentric factors are treated as one combined mechanism rather than two full penalties.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "tension_fraction": 0.7237, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 103.55, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_deep_hip_flexion", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "vastus_medialis", "rationale": "Loaded through the same deep knee-flexion range as the other vasti, with meaningful force sustained into the lengthened bottom position. The mild lengthened-position loading justifies a small elevation; the fixed sled path and absence of any rapid force reversal prevent anything larger.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 117.38, "tension_fraction": 0.6050, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 71.01, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_deep_knee_flexion", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Acting as a hip extensor, its posterior portion is lengthened by deep hip flexion at the bottom of the stroke while still resisting high sled force, matching the gluteal loading profile. This high-force active lengthening at long length is the identifiable mechanism for elevation, supported by a tension fraction of 0.61.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6105, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 64.24, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_deep_hip_flexion", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "soleus", "rationale": "The foot is fixed on the platform, so plantarflexor tension is essentially isometric force transmission with no meaningful active lengthening and no dynamic ankle excursion. Such tension produces intra-set fatigue that resolves largely on release rather than a persistent force deficit, placing it slightly below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 217.92, "tension_fraction": 0.2480, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 54.04, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["isometric_force_transmission_through_fixed_ankle"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "vastus_intermedius", "rationale": "Follows the vastus group profile: high sled force maintained as the knee flexes deeply gives mild loaded lengthening beyond conventional mid-range dynamic work, warranting a small positive modifier and nothing more.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 70.35, "tension_fraction": 0.6330, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 44.53, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_deep_knee_flexion", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rectus_f