tterns.", "load_exposure": 0.8800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "tibiofemoral_joint", "rationale": "The tibiofemoral joint carries a large knee-extension moment while transmitting high quadriceps, hamstring, and axial leg forces between the sled and pelvis. High compressive loading is combined with meaningful anterior-posterior shear components that vary with knee angle. The machine constrains the movement path and therefore reduces stabilization demand, but the local knee forces remain very high.", "load_exposure": 0.8400, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "shear", "co_contraction", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "The hip experiences a large external flexion moment in the bottom portion of the press and requires substantial gluteal, adductor, and hamstring force to extend the hip against the sled. These large muscle forces generate high hip-joint reaction and compression while the joint is substantially flexed. Back support lowers whole-body stabilization requirements but does not materially remove the high local hip-extension demand.", "load_exposure": 0.7800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "talocrural_joint", "rationale": "The ankle transmits substantial force between the footplate and lower leg and commonly operates in meaningful dorsiflexion near the bottom of the repetition. Plantarflexor activity and joint compression contribute moderate talocrural exposure, although ankle moment demand is lower than in direct calf exercises and the fixed footplate reduces stabilization requirements.", "load_exposure": 0.4200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The seat and backrest externally support the trunk, greatly reducing the lumbar flexion moment compared with squats or free-weight hinging. Nevertheless, substantial lower-body force is transmitted through the pelvis into the supported trunk, and abdominal and spinal musculature must maintain lumbopelvic position under heavy loading. Lumbar exposure therefore remains meaningful but clearly secondary.", "load_exposure": 0.2200, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "patellofemoral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
