etition.", "Rectus femoris and sartorius were held at or below reference because knee flexion offsets hip extension on the descent, preventing loaded lengthening at long length."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "iliopsoas", "rationale": "Free hanging means the limb weight is never set down, so the hip flexors carry meaningful tension continuously through the lowering phase and into the hanging position where they approach full hip extension — mild loaded lengthening rather than a mid-range return against a stop. The alternating pattern also extends the duration over which substantial tension is maintained relative to a conventional supported repetition. Together these give a small elevation; neither mechanism alone is severe enough to warrant more.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 102.23, "tension_fraction": 0.4842, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 49.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["controlled_eccentric_return_toward_full_hip_extension", "prolonged_meaningful_tension_across_the_repetition"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The alternating oblique pattern requires resisted eccentric control of trunk rotation and lateral flexion while hanging elongates the torso, so meaningful active force coincides with a mildly lengthened trunk position rather than resolving in mid-range. Tension also remains substantial across the whole repetition because bodyweight never unloads. These combine into one modest structural mechanism justifying 1.05.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3529, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 28.84, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_control_of_rotation_at_lengthened_trunk_position", "prolonged_meaningful_tension_across_the_repetition"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rectus_femoris", "rationale": "Assists hip flexion while the knee stays flexed, so its hip-driven lengthening on the descent is offset by knee position and it is not exposed to loaded lengthening at long length. Conventional dynamic tension at a low fraction places it at the neutral reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 91.44, "tension_fraction": 0.1934, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 17.68, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Contributes posterior pelvic tilt and anti-extension control at moderate force. In a knee raise the trunk flexion range is small and its role is largely stabilizing against the hang rather than driving a loaded lengthened contraction, so no mechanism distinguishes it from the reference condition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.3759, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.13, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Maintains glenohumeral centring continuously during the hang, but at low force relative to its large available capacity, with no active lengthening under meaningful load. Sustained low-level isometry of this kind produces intra-set fatigue that largely resolves on release rather than a persistent force deficit, so it sits slightly below the dynamic reference despite its continuous duty cycle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0293, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grip tension is genuinely continuous throughout the hang with no unloading phase, and sustained gripping does materially restrict local perfusion. This raises it above the sub-reference value assigned to briefly-gripping exercises, but the fatigue generated by sustained submaximal gripping recovers substantially once tension is released, so the neutral reference rather than an elevated value is appropriate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0735, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_gripping_isometric_tension_with_partial_perfusion_constraint"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Provides intra-abdominal bracing at low force with no contractile lengthening under load; its fatigue is predominantly transient, leaving marginally less persistent local deficit than the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.1593, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position against the bar as an antagonist to grip at very low force, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; such low-level sustained tension leaves little persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0559, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Holds the scapula against downward distraction during the hang at low force with no meaningful lengthening under load; the resulting fatigue resolves largely on release, placing persistent cost just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "tension_fraction": 0.0833, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_scapular_stabili