 a broad ankle ROM create pronounced lengthened-position loading. This supports a strong but non-exceptional modifier without implying eccentric overload.", "normalized_etu": 0.8223, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "broad", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 217.92, "smh_interpretation": "The flexed-knee position reduces gastrocnemius contribution and directs plantar-flexion demand toward the soleus, which remains highly loaded as the ankle moves into deep dorsiflexion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 155.82, "resistance_profile_alignment": "very_good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "gastrocnemius", "etu_cm2": 5.85, "rationale": "The muscle receives meaningful but low dynamic tension under shortened and mechanically disadvantaged conditions, warranting a clearly below-reference modifier.", "normalized_etu": 0.0319, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 183.54, "smh_interpretation": "Knee flexion shortens the gastrocnemius proximally and limits its ability to experience productive high-force lengthened plantar-flexion loading.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.88, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "tibialis_anterior", "etu_cm2": 1.60, "rationale": "Low-level antagonist co-contraction and positional ankle control with little productive dorsiflexion excursion warrant the lower calibration anchor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0384, "contraction_mode": ["isometric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 41.70, "smh_interpretation": "The muscle primarily stabilizes the ankle and regulates movement rather than performing directly resisted dorsiflexion through a productive range.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.1500, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.9333, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.1332}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 1.1332, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 164.70}	{"soleus": 1.0500, "gastrocnemius": 0.9500, "tibialis_anterior": 0.9500}	{"slug": "plate_loaded_seated_calf_raise", "notes": ["The single above-reference mechanism is heavy loading carried into end-range dorsiflexion on an elevated footplate, giving the soleus genuine high-force active lengthening at long muscle length.", "The seated knee-flexed position is the reason gastrocnemius is separated downward rather than sharing the soleus profile: proximal slack means it never carries meaningful force at a lengthened position.", "Sustained isometry was assessed as absent — this is a dynamic repetition, and the acute burning characteristic of calf work resolves rapidly and was excluded as a target variable.", "Fiber-type composition of the soleus was deliberately not used to modify R_m; intrinsic recovery speed belongs to the Muscle Recovery Velocity model, not to exercise-specific recovery cost."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "soleus", "rationale": "The elevated footplate allows the heel to drop below the forefoot, so substantial machine-supplied force is still resisted at end-range dorsiflexion where the soleus is maximally lengthened. This high-force active lengthening at long muscle length is the specific mechanism, supported by a tension fraction of 0.72 contributing a small nonlinear residual. It remains at 1.05 rather than higher because the plantarflexor excursion is short, the eccentric is controlled rather than overloaded, and the knee-flexed seated position removes any biarticular stretch amplification.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 217.92, "tension_fraction": 0.7150, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 155.82, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_dorsiflexion", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "gastrocnemius", "rationale": "The flexed knee holds the biarticular gastrocnemius slackened at its proximal attachment, so despite the ankle moving through dorsiflexion it contributes only low-force tension and is not exposed to meaningful loading at long length. Such tension leaves less persistent local deficit per unit than the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 183.54, "tension_fraction": 0.0375, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.88, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_synergist_tension_at_shortened_knee_position"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "tibialis_anterior", "rationale": "Acts only as a low-force antagonist stabilising foot position on the platform; there is no loaded lengthening and tension releases between repetitions, so its fatigue is largely transient and persistent cost sits just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 41.70, "tension_fraction": 0.0480, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_antagonist_co_contraction"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9833, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0458}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 164.70}	{"talocrural_joint": 0.8600, "tibiofemoral_joint": 0.1200}	{"slug": "plate_loaded_seated_calf_raise", "notes": ["This is among the more mechanically demanding conventional isolated plantarflexion exercises for the talocrural joint because substantial external resistance acts through the forefoot while large soleus and Achilles forces are generated.", "The seated flexed-knee configuration biases plantarflexor demand toward the soleus and substantially limits meaningful knee-joint loading.", "Machine guidance lowers stabilization requirements without materially reducing intrinsic ankle mechanical exposure."], "loaded_joints": ["talocrural_joint", "tibiofemoral_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "talocrural_joint", "rationale": "The talocrural joint is the primary loaded articulation. The 80 kg machine resistance acts through the forefoot to create a large dorsiflexion moment that must be opposed by high plantarflexor force, predominantly from the soleus in the flexed-knee position. Achilles-transmitted force and external footplate resistance generate very high ankle joint reaction and compression. Full intended ROM commonly places substantial force on the ankle in loaded dorsiflexion at the bottom and high plantarflexor demand through the concentric phase. Machine guidance minimizes stabilization demands but does not materially reduce the large local ankle moment.", "load_exposure": 0.8600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "loaded_end_range"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "tibiofemoral_joint", "rationale": "The knee remains substantially flexed and the thigh pad transmits external force through the distal femur and tibia, creating minor compressive exposure. However, the knee performs little dynamic work and the flexed position reduces gastrocnemius contribution, so net knee moment and associated tibiofemoral loading remain low relative to exercises that directly load knee flexion or extension.", "load_exposure": 0.1200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "talocrural_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
