ns full resistance at the bottom rather than unloading as gravitational torque does at full elbow extension.", "This is why the biceps receives 1.10 against the 1.05 assigned to the same muscle in a standing barbell curl — the mechanism is the coincidence of peak force with near-maximal length, not merely the presence of a stretched position.", "Brachialis and brachioradialis were held at the neutral reference precisely because they are monoarticular at the elbow and gain nothing from the shoulder position, which is the discriminating anatomical fact in this exercise.", "Shoulder-girdle and trunk involvement is positional isometry at low force with the relevant muscles near neutral length, hence a uniform 0.95 despite the unilateral anti-rotation demand.", "No stretch-mediated hypertrophy reasoning was applied; the elevation reflects restoration of local force-producing readiness after lengthened-position loading, and the exercise's reputation as a stretch-biased curl variant was excluded."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "This is the defining feature of the Bayesian variant: standing forward of the low pulley holds the humerus behind the torso, so the biceps is lengthened at the shoulder while the elbow also extends, and the cable — unlike a free weight — maintains full resistance precisely in that stretched bottom position rather than unloading there. High active force therefore coincides with near-maximal muscle length during the eccentric, a genuine persistent-deficit mechanism. A tension fraction of 0.81 adds a small nonlinear residual. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.8124, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.34, "eccentric_structural_stress": 