fect on R_m — hence a uniform 0.95 despite the continuous duty cycle.", "Contrast with the Copenhagen plank, where sustained isometry did justify elevation: there the loaded muscles were held at a lengthened position under high force with real perfusion constraint, conditions absent here.", "The exercise's high perceived core demand and its reputation as a stability movement were excluded; only persistent local recovery cost per unit tension was estimated."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Anti-rotation tension is maintained continuously as the arms extend and the resistance moment arm lengthens, with no unloading phase within the repetition — this continuous duty cycle lifts it above the sub-reference value given to intermittent stabilizers. It is not elevated because the trunk is held at essentially neutral length throughout, so there is no active lengthening under load and no eccentric structural component; moderate-force isometric fatigue of this type resolves substantially once tension is released rather than leaving a persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.3427, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 28.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_anti_rotation_isometry_at_neutral_trunk_length"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Contributes anti-extension bracing rather than resisting the primary rotational torque, so its force is lower than that of the obliques and it never lengthens under load. Isometric tension of this magnitude produces predominantly transient fatigue, placing persistent cost slightly below the dynamic reference.", "m