ting fatigue is transient and leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.0354, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9526, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9660}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 87.50}	{"hip_joint": 0.2600, "elbow_joint": 0.0800, "lumbar_spine": 0.5200, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.1000, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.1800, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.2200}	{"slug": "standing_cable_pallof_press", "notes": ["The defining mechanical demand is anti-rotation rather than dynamic spinal rotation.", "Lumbar exposure is substantial because meaningful rotational torque is resisted through high trunk co-contraction, but it remains below heavy externally loaded spinal-flexion, extension, or axial-loading exercises.", "Standing execution adds pelvic and hip stabilization demand that would be lower in a seated or externally braced Pallof-press variant."], "loaded_joints": ["lumbar_spine", "hip_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "glenohumeral_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "elbow_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The cable creates a persistent external axial-rotation moment on the trunk that the abdominal wall and spinal stabilizers must resist isometrically. As the arms press farther from the torso, the effective lever arm of the cable force increases and anti-rotation demand rises. Substantial trunk co-contraction generates meaningful lumbar compression and rotational control demand even though intended lumbar motion is minimal and the spine remains near neutral.", "load_exposure": 0.5200, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["rotation", "co_contraction", "stabilization", "compression"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "The hips form part of the base that prevents the cable from rotating the pelvis and trunk. Gluteal, adductor, and other pelvic stabilizers generate force to maintain frontal and transverse-plane position, producing modest hip-joint reaction and rotational stabilization demand. Because the exercise does not impose a large dynamic hip moment, exposure remains low-to-moderate.", "load_exposure": 0.2600, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "rotation", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae must remain controlled against the thorax while the arms move from the chest toward full reach and cable force attempts to rotate the upper body. Serratus anterior, trapezius, and other scapular stabilizers provide meaningful fixation and force transmission, but the articulation is not exposed to large pressing or overhead moments.", "load_exposure": 0.2200, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The shoulders transmit cable force through the extended arms and require rotator-cuff and deltoid stabilization, particularly as the hands move away from the torso. However, conventional Pallof-press technique keeps shoulder motion limited and the external force is primarily resisted by the trunk rather than by a large glenohumeral moment.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["stabilization", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist mainly transmits handle and grip forces while remaining near neutral. There is little direct flexion, extension, or deviation moment, so mechanical exposure is minor.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbows extend during the press and must maintain arm position against cable force, but the exercise is not intended to create a large elbow-extension moment. Local elbow loading is therefore secondary to trunk anti-rotation demand.", "load_exposure": 0.0800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "lumbar_spine", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl	28.00	68.68	{"brachialis": 30.83, "biceps_brachii": 27.06, "brachioradialis": 4.24, "pronators_supinators": 6.55}	{"obliques": 2.00, "rhomboids": 1.50, "brachialis": 30.83, "rotator_cuffs": 3.50, "wrist_flexors": 5.50, "biceps_brachii": 27.06, "erector_spinae": 2.50, "brachioradialis": 4.24, "deltoid_anterior": 4.50, "trapezius_middle": 1.50, "pronators_supinators": 6.55, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 2.50}	{"slug": "single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl", "notes": ["Supplied vector sums to 68.68, matching systemic_propulsive_fcsa_demand exactly.", "The internal ordering of the supplied vector is notable and mechanically coherent: brachialis exceeds biceps_brachii, which is the reverse of the Bayesian curl row. The preacher pad holds the humerus flexed, shortening the biceps at the shoulder and reducing its capacity to contribute, while the monoarticular brachialis is unaffected. No adjustment was made, since the previous model appears to have captured this correctly.", "Tension expansion ratio of 1.3421 is high for a supported single-joint exercise, but is driven mainly by the number of small additions rather than by any large one. The largest addition is only 5.50.", "The preacher pad substantially reduces shoulder and scapular stabilization demand relative to the Bayesian curl, which is reflected in deltoid_anterior at 4.50 versus 6.22 and rotator_cuffs at 3.50 versus 5.00, despite this exercise using a free implement.", "Triceps were considered as antagonists and excluded. Their activity during a controlled preacher curl is incidental co-contraction with no specific mechanical requirement.", "load_capacity_kg of 28 is read under the bilateral convention as 14 kg in the working hand, matching the convention example given in the specification. It was not used to recalculate any mechanical value."], "added_muscles": ["wrist_flexors", "deltoid_anterior", "rotator_cuffs", "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "erector_spinae", "obliques", "trapezius_middle", "rhomboids"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexor unaffected by the shoulder flexion the preacher pad imposes, and therefore the largest single contributor here. Supplied value retained; no secondary mechanical demand applies to this muscle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.6999, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 30.83, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 30.83, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Contributes elbow flexion torque, but with the humerus resting flexed on the pad the shoulder joint is externally supported and the muscle produces no shoulder-level restraint. Supplied value retained. Its position below brachialis in the vector is consistent with the shortened shoulder configuration reducing its effective contribution.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.6797, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 27.06, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.06, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Maintains forearm supination against both the dumbbell's free rotation about the forearm axis and the biceps supination tendency. The comparatively high supplied value is plausible for an unsupported single-dumbbell implement and is retained as the complete estimate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.1076, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.55, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.55, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Added because the entire load passes from the dumbbell through the hand to the forearm, and the wrist must be held against the extension moment the mass applies at the far end of the arc. Absent from the supplied vector despite being the sole link in the chain.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.1011, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Added because the load acting at the distal forearm applies a shoulder extension moment that would slide the humerus down the inclined pad. Low-level isometric shoulder flexion keeps the arm seated against the pad. Deliberately smaller than in the Bayesian curl, where no pad supported the humerus.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.1477, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Contributes elbow flexion torque, most effectively through the mid-range where its moment arm is largest. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.3779, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 4.24, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.24, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Added for humeral head centring against the distraction the hanging load applies at full elbow extension. Kept very low because the pad supports the humerus directly, removing most of the demand that an unsupported arm would create.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.0205, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Added as synergist to anterior deltoid in holding the humerus forward against the pad. Its tension fraction appears moderate only because its maximum FCSA is small.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.1849, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Added because the forward-leaning preacher position places the trunk in unsupported partial flexion over the bench, requiring extensor tension to maintain that posture. Small, since the chest and arm rest against the pad.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0235, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Added because a single-arm implement loads one side only, producing a modest asymmetric trunk moment. Much smaller than in a standing single-arm exercise, since the pad and bench absorb most of the asymmetry.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0245, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Added to hold the scapula against the protraction tendency created by the load pulling the arm forward over the pad, giving the elbow flexors a stable proximal base.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0573, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Shares the anti-protraction scapular fixation demand with middle trapezius. Purely positional, with no retraction excursion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0531, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.3421, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 68.68, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 68.68, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 92.18}	{"obliques": 1.70, "rhomboids": 1.28, "brachialis": 33.91, "rotator_cuffs": 2.98, "wrist_flexors": 4.68, "biceps_brachii": 28.41, "erector_spinae": 2.13, "brachioradialis": 4.03, "deltoid_anterior": 3.83, "trapezius_middle": 1.28, "pronators_supinators": 5.57, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 2.13}	{"slug": "single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl", "notes": ["The central result of this row is the inversion relative to the Bayesian curl. There, biceps_brachii received 1.20 and brachialis 1.05 because the shoulder was extended; here the ranking reverses, with brachialis at 1.10 and biceps at 1.05, because the preacher pad holds the shoulder flexed and shortens the biarticular muscle proximally.", "Brachialis at 1.10 is the highest modifier assigned to that muscle in this series, and it rests on the preacher pad geometry placing peak gravitational torque at maximum elbow extension.", "Neither elbow flexor reaches 1.15, because the preacher resistance profile collapses near the top of the arc as the forearm approaches vertical. The favourable lengthened loading is real but the high-tension range is truncated at the shortened end.", "brachioradialis at 0.95 is separated from the other two flexors because its peak moment arm falls in the mid-range, where this exercise's resistance is already declining.", "Hypertrophic quality ratio of 0.9973 sits at the global calibration target. Two lengthened-loaded prime movers carrying 63 percent of total tension offset nine low-excursion stabilizers.", "Normalized ETU of 0.7698 for brachialis exceeds that of biceps_brachii at 0.7137, so the preacher variation is estimated to deliver its largest stimulus to the monoarticular flexor.", "No muscle received the 0.80 floor. Every stabilizer here has a specific mechanical requirement and sustained tension."], "etu_sum_cm2": 91.93, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachialis", "etu_cm2": 33.91, "rationale": "The inclined pad orients the humerus so the forearm is near horizontal at the bottom, putting the dumbbell moment arm at maximum exactly where the elbow is most extended and the muscle longest. This is the defining hypertrophic feature of the preacher variation and the muscle cannot escape the load there. Held at 1.10 rather than higher because the top of the arc unloads almost completely as the forearm approaches vertical, truncating the high-tension range at the shortened end.", "normalized_etu": 0.7698, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "smh_interpretation": "No SMH factor supplied. Monoarticular architecture means elbow angle alone sets length, and the preacher geometry places peak gravitational torque at the most extended elbow position the exercise allows.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 30.83, "resistance_profile_alignment": "very_good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "etu_cm2": 28.41, "rationale": "Receives a smaller bonus than brachialis on a specific mechanism: it shares the favourable elbow-angle resistance profile but forfeits it partially to sustained shoulder flexion. Deliberately three steps below the 1.20 assigned to the same muscle in the Bayesian curl, where the shoulder position lengthened rather than shortened it. This contrast is the analytically important result of the row.", "normalized_etu": 0.7137, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "smh_interpretation": "The biarticular case works against it here. Elbow extension lengthens the muscle distally, but the flexed humeral position on the pad shortens it proximally throughout, so the two effects largely cancel and it never reaches the length available in a behind-the-body curl.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.06, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "etu_cm2": 5.57, "rationale": "Meaningful sustained isometric control of forearm orientation against a freely rotating implement, but with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0915, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "smh_interpretation": "Forearm rotation is held constant, so no length variation occurs.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.55, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 4.68, "rationale": "Sustained isometric grip and wrist fixation with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0860, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "Static grip with no length change through the repetition.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "etu_cm2": 4.03, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic flexion tension, but its peak contribution region sits where the preacher resistance profile is already declining, and it gains nothing from the extended bottom position. One step below reference for that mismatch.", "normalized_etu": 0.3588, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "smh_interpretation": "Its greatest mechanical advantage falls in the mid-range, away from the extended bottom position that benefits brachialis.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.24, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "etu_cm2": 3.83, "rationale": "Low-level positional fixation in a shortened position with no excursion. Kept at 0.85 rather than the floor because holding the humerus against the pad is a genuine mechanical requirement, not incidental activity.", "normalized_etu": 0.1256, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "smh_interpretation": "Held actively shortened by the flexed humeral position on the pad for the entire set.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 2.98, "rationale": "Humeral head centring only, with no external rotation excursion. Matches the treatment given in the Bayesian curl rather than the partly dynamic role it played in the reverse fly. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0174, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "Pure centring isometry at a fixed humeral position, with individual cuff muscles at differing lengths and no net advantage.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "etu_cm2": 2.13, "rationale": "Excursion-free fixation in a shortened position, matched to anterior deltoid for consistency. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.1575, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "smh_interpretation": "Shortened by the flexed shoulder position throughout; no lengthened loading.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "etu_cm2": 2.13, "rationale": "Sustained postural isometry without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0200, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "Static trunk position over the bench; no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "obliques", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Low-level sustained isometric trunk control without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0208, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "smh_interpretation": "Anti-asymmetry bracing at a fixed length.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "etu_cm2": 1.28, "rationale": "Positional scapular fixation without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0489, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "smh_interpretation": "Scapular position held constant; no length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "etu_cm2": 1.28, "rationale": "Positional anti-protraction fixation without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0453, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "smh_interpretation": "No scapular excursion, hence no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.8500, "unweighted_mean": 0.8917, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9973}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9973, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 92.18}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "rhomboids": 0.9500, "brachialis": 1.1000, "rotator_cuffs": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "brachioradialis": 1.1000, "deltoid_anterior": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 0.9500}	{"slug": "single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl", "notes": ["The preacher pad's angle is the identifiable mechanism: peak gravitational resistance torque occurs at or near full elbow extension, so unlike a standing curl the load does not fall away where the elbow flexors are longest.", "Monoarticular elbow flexors (brachialis, brachioradialis) receive 1.10 while the biceps receives 1.05, because the pad holds the humerus flexed and shortens the biceps proximally — the inverse of the Bayesian curl, where shoulder extension amplified biceps lengthening.", "This is consistent with the hammer-grip preacher variant: the supinated grip redistributes load toward the biceps, but that redistribution appears in Active Tension Exposure, not in R_m, since the per-unit recovery mechanism is unchanged.", "Eccentric structural stress and lengthened-position loading were combined into one mechanism per muscle rather than counted as two full penalties.", "The pronounced acute discomfort at the stretched bottom position of preacher work was excluded as a target variable; the elevation reflects persistent restoration of local force-producing capacity, not soreness or perceived difficulty."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "The preacher pad fixes the humerus on an incline so that the forearm approaches horizontal at full elbow extension, placing peak gravitational resistance torque precisely where the elbow flexors are longest — the inverse of a standing curl, where load falls away at the bottom. High active force therefore coincides with near-maximal muscle length throughout the terminal eccentric, a genuine persistent-deficit mechanism. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.6999, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 30.83, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "peak_resistance_torque_coinciding_with_longest_muscle_length"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "It shares the pad's loaded end-range elbow extension, and the supinated grip makes it a primary contributor here, but the humerus resting flexed on the pad shortens it proximally and partially offsets the distal lengthening. This is the opposite arrangement to a Bayesian curl, where shoulder extension amplified biceps lengthening, so a smaller elevation than the monoarticular elbow flexors is warranted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.6797, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.06, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "shoulder_flexed_position_reducing_proximal_lengthening"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Holds the supinated forearm position against the dumbbell at low force with no dynamic rotation under load and no lengthening, so the fatigue generated is transient rather than persistent.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.1076, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.55, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grips the dumbbell at submaximal isometric force with no lengthening under load and no meaningful perfusion restriction; such fatigue resolves rapidly after the set.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.1011, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "With the arm supported on the pad it contributes only positional tension to keep the humerus seated; there is no dynamic excursion and no lengthening under load, so its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "tension_fraction": 0.1477, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular at the elbow, so it experiences the preacher pad's peak-torque-at-full-extension profile without any proximal offset, exactly as brachialis does. Its share of the load is smaller under a supinated grip than a hammer grip, but that magnitude is carried by T_m; the recovery-cost mechanism per unit tension is identical.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.3779, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.24, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "The pad supports the humerus, reducing cuff involvement to negligible glenohumeral centring at very low force relative to its large available capacity, with no loaded lengthening.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0205, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Maintains trunk position over the preacher bench at very low force relative to its large capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; such postural tension leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0235, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "Assists in holding the arm against the pad at low force; it is not moved through a loaded range and is not held at a lengthened position under high force, so its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "tension_fraction": 0.1849, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Provides minimal trunk bracing against the unilateral load at very low force relative to capacity, with no lengthening under load and release between repetitions.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.0245, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Holds scapular position against the pad at very low force with no loaded lengthening; the tension is positional and its fatigue predominantly transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.0531, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Contributes low-force scapular stabilization against the bench with no lengthened-position loading, leaving persistent cost just below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "tension_fraction": 0.0573, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9917, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0641}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 92.18}	{"elbow_joint": 0.7800, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.1800, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.1000}	{"slug": "single_arm_dumbbell_preacher_curl", "notes": ["The 28 kg load is interpreted according to the database bilateral convention despite the exercise being performed one arm at a time.", "Compared with the 16 kg preacher hammer curl, the higher working load and supinated grip produce somewhat greater direct elbow-flexor demand and therefore higher elbow exposure.", "External upper-arm support keeps shoulder exposure low and makes the movement highly localized to the elbow."], "loaded_joints": ["elbow_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "glenohumeral_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow is the dominant loaded articulation. The 28 kg dumbbell creates a large external elbow-extension moment that must be opposed by high biceps brachii, brachialis, and brachioradialis force. Because these muscles act through short internal moment arms, substantial muscle force and elbow-joint reaction are required. The preacher bench fixes the upper arm and prevents shoulder compensation, concentrating the mechanical demand at the elbow. Full intended ROM also exposes the elbow to substantial loading near relatively extended positions, placing this exercise near the high end of conventional direct elbow-flexion loading.", "load_exposure": 0.7800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist must stabilize and transmit the dumbbell load while maintaining a largely neutral or slightly extended position. Grip and forearm co-contraction generate meaningful radiocarpal force transmission, but there is little intended wrist motion or large direct wrist moment.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The upper arm is externally supported by the preacher bench, substantially removing dynamic shoulder motion and stabilization requirements. Some proximal force transmission occurs through the biceps and other tissues crossing the shoulder, but glenohumeral loading remains low relative to unsupported curling variants.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "elbow_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
