ral joint toward the elbow because of greater elbow flexion-extension excursion and a bar path that preserves a larger triceps moment.", "Free-bar execution still produces meaningful shoulder-complex and wrist stabilization demand despite the exercise being elbow-dominant."], "loaded_joints": ["elbow_joint", "glenohumeral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "radiocarpal_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow is the dominant loaded articulation. The California press combines substantial elbow flexion during the lowering phase with forceful elbow extension to return the bar, creating a large external flexion moment that must be opposed by the triceps. High triceps force, particularly from the long and lateral heads, produces substantial elbow-joint reaction and compression. Compared with a conventional bench press, the bar path and greater elbow excursion concentrate more of the mechanical demand at the elbow, placing exposure near the high end of conventional pressing and elbow-extension exercises.", "load_exposure": 0.7800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint remains meaningfully loaded because the barbell must be controlled over the torso while the upper arm changes position during the hybrid press-extension path. Pectoralis major, anterior deltoid, triceps long head, and rotator-cuff musculature generate substantial force and stabilize the humeral head. Shoulder loading is lower than in a standard bench press because the movement shifts more torque toward elbow extension, but free-bar control and the loaded shoulder position still create substantial exposure.", "load_exposure": 0.6000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae must remain controlled against the bench and thorax while the free bar is lowered and pressed through a path combining elbow extension with shoulder motion. Scapular stabilizers provide meaningful force transmission and fixation, although scapular excursion is constrained by the bench and the exercise is less shoulder-dominant than a conventional bench press.", "load_exposure": 0.3600, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "The AC joint transmits force through the shoulder girdle while the bar is controlled over the torso and the humerus moves through the hybrid pressing path. Exposure is meaningful but lower than in conventional bench pressing because the California press places relatively more of the external moment demand on the elbow and less on horizontal shoulder adduction.", "load_exposure": 0.3000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization", "compression"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrists transmit the 50 kg barbell load while forearm musculature stabilizes the bar through a changing forearm orientation. Direct wrist flexion-extension moment remains modest with technically valid positioning, so radiocarpal exposure is secondary despite meaningful free-bar stabilization demand.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "elbow_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
