the floor, but gravitational resistance and external moment decline as the trunk approaches the shortened top position.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 20.77, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "etu_cm2": 9.22, "rationale": "Meaningful dynamic abdominal tension is present, but the mixed bracing role and incomplete alignment with the obliques' full functional ROM make the exposure slightly below the reference condition.", "normalized_etu": 0.1128, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "smh_interpretation": "The obliques participate dynamically in trunk flexion but also provide symmetrical bracing, and the non-rotational crunch does not load them through their broader rotational or lateral-flexion functions.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.71, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "etu_cm2": 5.03, "rationale": "The cervical tension is substantial and partly dynamic but occurs through limited ROM with a shortened-position bias, making it inferior to dedicated dynamic neck-flexion loading.", "normalized_etu": 0.3637, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "smh_interpretation": "The muscle flexes and stabilizes the neck as the head follows the trunk, but tension is concentrated in a shortened cervical-flexion position and is not produced through a dedicated full cervical ROM.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.92, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 1.60, "rationale": "Predominantly isometric circumferential bracing with negligible productive excursion warrants the lower calibration anchor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0728, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "smh_interpretation": "The muscle maintains abdominal-wall stiffness without meaningful shortening or lengthening through a directly resisted ROM.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "etu_cm2": 0.80, "rationale": "Incidental antagonist co-contraction at very low tension with no productive extension ROM represents distinctly low-quality hypertrophic exposure.", "normalized_etu": 0.0075, "contraction_mode": ["eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "The extensors provide only low-level antagonist braking while lengthening during spinal flexion and are not directly overloaded through spinal extension.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "brief", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8000, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.8800, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9497}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9497, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 39.40}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "sternocleidomastoid": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500}	{"slug": "bodyweight_crunch", "notes": ["The distinguishing feature versus loaded trunk flexion is the resistance profile: gravitational torque on the torso falls toward zero as the athlete returns to the floor, so the eccentric is only partially loaded and each repetition ends in complete unloading.", "This is the specific mechanism placing every muscle at 0.95 — brief meaningful tension, minimal eccentric structural stress and good inter-repetition unloading, matching the calibration description for somewhat lower-than-reference persistent cost.", "The very high rectus abdominis tension fraction was deliberately not converted into a positive modifier, since load magnitude is already represented in Active Tension Exposure and no accompanying structural mechanism exists.", "Neck discomfort and the acute abdominal burning associated with crunching were excluded as target variables; both are transient and unrelated to persistent restoration of force-producing capacity.", "A uniform vector is reported deliberately: no muscle in this movement is exposed to a mechanism that would differentiate it, and unsupported spread was avoided."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "The crunch has a short trunk-flexion range and, unlike a cable crunch, resistance falls away as the torso returns toward the floor — the eccentric is only partially loaded and the muscle is unloaded entirely at the bottom of each repetition. Meaningful tension is therefore brief with good inter-repetition unloading and no loaded lengthening, which per the calibration scale is a sub-reference persistent-recovery profile. The very high tension fraction was not converted into a positive modifier because that magnitude is already carried by Active Tension Exposure and no structural mechanism accompanies it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.8551, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 20.77, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Assists trunk flexion and provides compressive control over a short range at low force, with resistance dropping off on the descent and full unloading against the floor. Brief tension without lengthened-position loading places persistent cost slightly below the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.1188, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.71, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["brief_low_force_dynamic_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "sternocleidomastoid", "rationale": "Holds and flexes the head against gravity only, so absolute force is small and the head rests on or near the floor between repetitions, giving complete unloading. Short duration and no loaded lengthening leave less persistent local deficit than the dynamic reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.83, "tension_fraction": 0.4281, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.92, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["brief_low_force_cervical_flexion_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates intra-abdominal pressure at low force isometrically with no contractile lengthening under load and full release each repetition, so its fatigue is predominantly transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.0910, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Acts only as a minimal antagonist controlling segmental spinal position while supine on the floor; tension is very low, brief and non-lengthening, leaving negligible persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0094, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_antagonist_co_contraction"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 0.9500, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9500, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9500}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 39.40}	{"hip_joint": 0.1000, "lumbar_spine": 0.5000}	{"slug": "bodyweight_crunch", "notes": ["The 22 kg load capacity represents the effective moving body-segment mass rather than external resistance.", "Lumbar exposure is materially lower than in a heavy kneeling cable crunch because the external resistance and resulting trunk-flexion moment are smaller.", "Hip exposure assumes strict crunch execution without converting the repetition into a sit-up or dominant hip-flexion movement."], "loaded_joints": ["lumbar_spine", "hip_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The lumbar spine is the primary loaded region. The mass of the head, arms, and upper trunk creates an external extension moment that must be overcome by abdominal flexor force during spinal flexion. This produces meaningful lumbar flexion torque and muscle-generated compressive loading through a deliberately flexed spinal position. Absolute external resistance and moment demand are substantially lower than in externally loaded cable crunches or heavy trunk-flexion exercises, placing exposure near the middle of the lumbar-specific resistance-exercise range.", "load_exposure": 0.5000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "In a technically valid abdominal crunch, movement is dominated by trunk flexion rather than substantial hip flexion. Hip flexors and pelvic stabilizers may contribute minor force transmission and positional control, but the pelvis and lower body remain supported and the hip is not exposed to a large external moment.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "lumbar_spine", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl	24.00	50.70	{"wrist_flexors": 50.70}	{"wrist_flexors": 50.70, "brachioradialis": 0.80, "wrist_extensors": 2.00, "pronators_supinators": 1.00}	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["The supplied contribution vector sums exactly to the systemic propulsive FCSA demand.", "The wrist flexors already capture the complete dynamic wrist-flexion and grip demand and were not expanded mechanically a second time.", "Additional exposure was limited to antagonist wrist control, palms-up forearm-orientation maintenance, and minor proximal forearm fixation.", "Forearm support removes most elbow, shoulder, trunk, and whole-body stabilization demand.", "The exercise is modeled without deliberate elbow flexion, forearm lifting, or momentum."], "added_muscles": ["wrist_extensors", "pronators_supinators", "brachioradialis"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Produces nearly all wrist-flexion torque, eccentrically controls the dumbbells into wrist extension, and maintains finger closure around the handles. The supplied contribution already represents its complete high-force dynamic and gripping state.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control", "grip_maintenance", "multi_joint_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.9314, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 50.70, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 50.70, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Provides low-level antagonist stiffness and regulates the transition between wrist extension and flexion without becoming a meaningful producer of the lifting torque.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "mechanical_roles": ["antagonist_co_contraction", "joint_stabilization", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.0373, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Maintains the palms-up forearm orientation against small rotational disturbances from the independently held dumbbells.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "grip_maintenance", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0164, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Provides minor elbow and proximal forearm fixation while external support bears most of the upper-limb positioning demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.0713, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "technique": "palms-up forearms supported on the thighs or bench with movement isolated to the wrists", "execution_scope": "bilateral", "range_of_motion": "full intended wrist-flexion ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.0749, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 50.70, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 50.70, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 54.50}	{"wrist_flexors": 55.77, "brachioradialis": 0.68, "wrist_extensors": 1.60, "pronators_supinators": 0.85}	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["The wrist flexors receive a clear positive modifier because the supported dumbbell curl isolates them under very high recruitment through broad ROM from a substantially lengthened wrist-extension position.", "The modifier remains below the upper calibration range because the dumbbell resistance profile still varies with wrist and implement geometry and does not represent specialized eccentric overload.", "The wrist extensors receive the minimum modifier because their exposure consists primarily of antagonist stabilization and movement reversal.", "Forearm-orientation and proximal forearm contributions receive reduced modifiers because they are predominantly isometric and low-ROM.", "ETU values equal active tension exposure multiplied by the listed H_m values within rounding tolerance."], "etu_sum_cm2": 58.90, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 55.77, "rationale": "Near-maximal recruitment, stable forearm support, controlled eccentric loading, broad isolated ROM, and substantial tension in the lengthened wrist-extension position justify a clear positive modifier.", "normalized_etu": 1.0252, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "broad", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "The wrists descend into substantial extension under dumbbell load, lengthening the wrist flexors before they flex the wrists through the available supported range. Finger flexion also remains active to retain the implements.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 50.70, "resistance_profile_alignment": "very_good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "substantially_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "etu_cm2": 1.60, "rationale": "Low-level antagonist co-contraction and joint stabilization with negligible productive extension ROM warrant the lower calibration anchor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0298, "contraction_mode": ["isometric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "smh_interpretation": "The wrist extensors primarily regulate joint position and reversal without directly resisted wrist-extension work through a productive range.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "etu_cm2": 0.85, "rationale": "Forearm-orientation maintenance is mechanically necessary but predominantly isometric and low-ROM.", "normalized_etu": 0.0140, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "smh_interpretation": "The forearms remain supinated without directly resisted pronation or supination through meaningful ROM.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "etu_cm2": 0.68, "rationale": "The exposure is low-level, positional, and predominantly isometric.", "normalized_etu": 0.0606, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "smh_interpretation": "The elbow and proximal forearm remain nearly fixed while external support removes most dynamic demand.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.9000, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0807}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 1.0807, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 54.50}	{"wrist_flexors": 1.0000, "brachioradialis": 0.9500, "wrist_extensors": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500}	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["The prime mover sits at the neutral reference through offsetting factors rather than an absence of features: near-maximal relative tension and end-range loaded lengthening push upward, while very brief meaningful tension and low absolute force in a short-range isolation push downward.", "The very high tension fraction was deliberately not converted into a modifier on its own, since load magnitude is already carried by Active Tension Exposure and the specification warns against assigning a large R_m solely for high tension fraction.", "Wrist extensors were held at 0.95 despite lengthening during wrist flexion, because they are co-contracting antagonists at low force rather than resisting the load at long length.", "The intense acute burning typical of direct forearm work was excluded as a target variable; it resolves quickly and does not represent persistent restoration of force-producing readiness.", "This exercise was kept at the same prime-mover value as the palms-down wrist extension, since no identifiable mechanism distinguishes their persistent recovery profiles."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Two factors offset here. The near-maximal tension fraction of 0.93 would ordinarily support a small positive modifier for the nonlinear residual cost of operating close to available capacity, and the forearm resting on a bench with the wrist free does keep meaningful resistance present as the wrist extends into a lengthened position. Against this, the movement is a very short-range isolation with brief meaningful tension per repetition and low absolute forces relative to the group's structural tolerance, which the calibration scale treats as a sub-reference feature. With one factor pointing up and one down, the neutral reference is the correct holistic estimate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.9320, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 50.70, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue", "near_maximal_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Acts as a low-force antagonist providing wrist joint stability; although it is lengthened as the wrist flexes, it is not resisting the load, so no high-force lengthening applies and its brief tension leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0373, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_antagonist_co_contraction"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Holds the supinated forearm position against the bench at very low force with no dynamic rotation under load; the fatigue produced is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0164, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Contributes minimal elbow stabilization with the forearm supported on the bench; no excursion or loaded lengthening occurs, so persistent recovery cost per unit tension falls just below reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.0713, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9625, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9982}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 54.50}	{"elbow_joint": 0.1000, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.8400}	{"slug": "seated_dumbbell_wrist_curl", "notes": ["This is a highly localized wrist-flexion exercise with minimal proximal-joint involvement because the forearms are supported.", "The radiocarpal score is slightly higher than the corresponding palms-down wrist-extension variant because the larger 24 kg working load produces greater direct wrist moment and flexor-force transmission under the same supported geometry."], "loaded_joints": ["radiocarpal_joint", "elbow_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The radiocarpal joint is the primary loaded articulation. With the forearms supported and the dumbbells acting through the hands, the resistance creates a direct wrist-extension moment that must be opposed by the wrist flexors. The relatively large external hand moment arm and high flexor force generated through short internal moment arms produce substantial joint reaction and compression. Full intended ROM also exposes the wrist to meaningful loading in extension and flexion. Relative to conventional wrist-loading exercises, a 24 kg bilateral working load places this exercise near the high end of direct wrist-flexion mechanical exposure.", "load_exposure": 0.8400, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "loaded_end_range"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbows remain effectively fixed while the forearms are externally supported, so little elbow flexion or extension moment is required. Forearm flexor forces are transmitted proximally and create minor elbow-region mechanical exposure, but the elbow is not a meaningful load-bearing articulation in the exercise.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
side_facing_reverse_pec_deck	20.00	108.17	{"obliques": 12.34, "rhomboids": 16.53, "brachialis": 4.63, "rotator_cuffs": 14.71, "biceps_brachii": 3.42, "erector_spinae": 6.28, "trapezius_lower": 7.19, "trapezius_upper": 3.71, "trapezius_middle": 16.90, "deltoid_posterior": 17.66, "serratus_anterior": 2.27, "transverse_abdominis": 2.53}	{"obliques": 12.34, "rhomboids": 16.53, "brachialis": 4.63, "teres_major": 1.50, "rotator_cuffs": 14.71, "wrist_flexors": 5.00, "biceps_brachii": 3.42, "erector_spinae": 6.28, "deltoid_lateral": 8.00, "trapezius_lower": 7.19, "trapezius_upper": 3.71, "latissimus_dorsi": 5.00, "rectus_abdominis": 2.00, "trapezius_middle": 16.90, "deltoid_posterior": 17.66, "serratus_anterior": 2.27, "pronators_supinators": 3.00, "transverse_abdominis": 2.53}	{"slug": "side_facing_reverse_pec_deck", "notes": ["Supplied vector sums to 108.17, matching systemic_propulsive_fcsa_demand exactly.", "The supplied vector contained brachialis and biceps_brachii, which cannot be propulsive in a fixed-elbow fly. Per the input-handling rules their values were retained as an established lower bound of active tension and reclassified as isometric load transmission rather than removed. This is recorded as an inconsistency in the naming of the previous model's output, not an arithmetic error.", "The large obliques and erector_spinae entries are consistent with the side-facing single-arm geometry, which converts the resistance into a sustained unilateral trunk rotation moment. No increase was applied because the supplied values already appear to represent that full demand.", "Six muscles were added, all along specific pathways absent from the supplied vector: lateral deltoid torque past the frontal plane, lat and teres major transverse extension, grip and forearm rotation control, and anterior trunk bracing.", "Tension expansion ratio of 1.2265 is modest, reflecting a seat-supported machine in which the trunk demand was already largely captured by the previous model.", "Pectoralis major and anterior deltoid were considered as antagonists but excluded; their activity during a controlled reverse fly is incidental rather than mechanically required, which does not meet the evidence standard.", "load_capacity_kg of 20 is read as the total bilateral-convention load for this single-arm exercise. It was not used to recalculate any mechanical value, since the supplied vector is authoritative."], "added_muscles": ["deltoid_lateral", "latissimus_dorsi", "wrist_flexors", "pronators_supinators", "rectus_abdominis", "teres_major"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Prime mover for glenohumeral horizontal abduction against the pad or handle. Supplied value retained; no additional mechanical demand exists beyond the torque already accounted for.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7165, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 17.66, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 17.66, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Produces scapular retraction, which is a task-relevant component of the movement rather than mere fixation. Retraction torque and scapular stabilization are one force state here, so the supplied value is retained without addition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.6455, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 16.90, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.90, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Retraction with downward rotation contribution, working through a genuine scapular excursion. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "scapular_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.5851, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 16.53, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.53, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Infraspinatus and teres minor contribute directly to horizontal abduction and external rotation while the whole cuff centres the humeral head against the distraction created by the lateral handle pull. Both roles are expressed by a single force state, so no increase is applied on top of the supplied value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.0861, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 14.71, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 14.71, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "In the side-facing single-arm configuration the resistance vector acts unilaterally in the transverse plane, generating a continuous trunk rotation moment toward the machine. The obliques resist that rotation isometrically. The comparatively high supplied value is mechanically sound for this setup and is retained as the complete tension estimate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.1510, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 12.34, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 12.34, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Added because the posterior fibres of the lateral deltoid lie behind the glenohumeral axis of horizontal abduction and contribute measurable torque once the arm passes the frontal plane, while the whole head also supports the abducted arm position against gravity. The previous model appears to have attributed the entire deltoid demand to the posterior head.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "limb_position_maintenance"], "tension_fraction": 0.1749, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes to retraction while opposing the scapular elevation tendency generated by upper trapezius and by the handle line of pull. Supplied value already reflects both; retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.4800, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 7.19, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.19, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Works with the obliques to hold the spine against the unilateral rotation and lateral flexion moment. Extensor tension also prevents the thoracic flexion that would otherwise accompany the pull. Retained at the supplied value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0590, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.28, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.28, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Added because at the abducted arm position the lat contributes a transverse extension component and resists humeral distraction at the glenohumeral joint. Magnitude kept modest since its moment arm for horizontal abduction is small at this arm height.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.1140, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Added because the entire resistance is transmitted through the hand on the handle. Grip force is the necessary link in the kinetic chain and was absent from the supplied vector.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.0919, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "The elbow angle is fixed throughout a reverse fly, so this muscle produces no task-relevant elbow torque and cannot be propulsive despite its position in the supplied vector. Its force isometrically maintains the elbow angle and transmits handle reaction force from forearm to humerus. The supplied value is retained as the mandated lower bound and reclassified rather than removed.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.1051, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 4.63, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.63, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "Supports the shoulder girdle and assists upward rotation while the arm is held at shoulder height. Low-excursion positional tension; retained at the supplied value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1916, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.71, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.71, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "As with brachialis, no elbow torque is produced as task output. The long head additionally resists anterior humeral head translation at the glenohumeral joint. Supplied value retained and reclassified as isometric load transmission.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "mechanical_roles": ["isometric_fixation", "load_transmission", "joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.0859, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.42, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.42, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Added because the handle applies a rotational moment about the forearm long axis that must be resisted to keep the grip orientation constant through the arc. Small magnitude, specific pathway.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "mechanical_roles": ["limb_position_maintenance", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0493, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates intra-abdominal pressure supporting the anti-rotation demand carried mainly by the obliques. Retained at supplied value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.1152, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 2.53, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.53, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "As a protractor it is antagonist to the primary retraction action; its low-level tension keeps the scapula against the thorax and contributes upward rotation so the glenoid tracks the moving humerus. Correctly small in the supplied vector; retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "antagonist_co_contraction"], "tension_fraction": 0.0630, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 2.27, "contraction_character": ["co_contractile"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.27, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Added as the anterior partner to erector spinae in maintaining a rigid trunk cylinder against the rotational load. Deliberately small, since the oblique and transverse abdominal contributions carry the specific anti-rotation demand.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0823, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Added as a small synergist to latissimus dorsi in transverse extension and humeral head stabilization. Its tension fraction is relatively high only because its maximum FCSA is very small.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.3333, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.2265, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 108.17, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 108.17, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 132.67}	{"obliques": 10.49, "rhomboids": 15.70, "brachialis": 3.94, "teres_major": 1.35, "rotator_cuffs": 13.97, "wrist_flexors": 4.25, "biceps_brachii": 2.91, "erector_spinae": 5.34, "deltoid_lateral": 7.60, "trapezius_lower": 6.47, "trapezius_upper": 3.15, "latissimus_dorsi": 4.50, "rectus_abdominis": 1.70, "trapezius_middle": 16.06, "deltoid_posterior": 18.54, "serratus_anterior": 1.93, "pronators_supinators": 2.55, "transverse_abdominis": 2.15}	{"slug": "side_facing_reverse_pec_deck", "notes": ["No muscle received the 0.80 floor. Every included muscle either performs dynamic work or carries meaningful sustained load, so none meets the description of incidental co-contraction or trivial postural fixation.", "Only deltoid_posterior exceeds 1.00, and only by one step, on the strength of the extended cross-body start position that the single-arm side-facing configuration allows.", "The scapular retractors are deliberately separated from the posterior deltoid at 0.95: they reach peak demand at their shortest length over a small excursion, which is the opposite length profile to the working humerus.", "Hypertrophic quality ratio of 0.9241 is below the global target of approximately 1.00, driven by the large number of low-excursion isometric entries the mechanical model required. The tension-weighted figure sits well above the unweighted mean of 0.8917 because the near-reference dynamic muscles carry most of the tension.", "Normalized ETU reaches 0.7521 for posterior deltoid and 0.6134 for middle trapezius, the only muscles approaching a substantial share of a full reference effective-repetition equivalent.", "Teres major shows a normalized ETU of 0.3000 despite the smallest absolute tension in the vector; this is an artefact of its very small maximum FCSA and should not be read as a large stimulus."], "etu_sum_cm2": 122.60, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "etu_cm2": 18.54, "rationale": "The single-arm side-facing setup permits the arm to travel further across the body at the start than a chest-supported bilateral version, loading the posterior deltoid in a modestly lengthened position. Resistance is present from the first degrees of the arc rather than appearing only near retraction. Bonus limited to 1.05 because the cam still delivers appreciable torque demand at the shortened end and the arc is short in absolute terms.", "normalized_etu": 0.7521, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "smh_interpretation": "No SMH factor supplied. The starting position places the humerus across the midline with the posterior deltoid at meaningful length under load, which supports a small bonus but not a large one.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 17.66, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "etu_cm2": 16.06, "rationale": "Peak retraction demand coincides with maximum scapular adduction, the shortest position for this muscle, and total scapular excursion is small relative to the arm's arc. Reduced modestly to 0.95 for shortened bias with limited high-tension ROM; it stays near reference because the contraction is genuinely dynamic and well loaded.", "normalized_etu": 0.6134, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "smh_interpretation": "Scapular retractors are shortest exactly where the machine demands most torque, so no stretch-mediated case exists.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.90, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rhomboids", "etu_cm2": 15.70, "rationale": "Shares the retraction length profile: highest demand at end-range adduction where the muscle is shortest, over a small excursion. 0.95.", "normalized_etu": 0.5558, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "smh_interpretation": "Same shortened-at-peak profile as middle trapezius.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.53, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 13.97, "rationale": "Placed above the pure stabilizer band because a genuine dynamic external rotation and horizontal abduction component exists, not merely positional fixation. Held below reference at 0.95 because a substantial share of the tension is centring isometry with little excursion.", "normalized_etu": 0.0818, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "The external rotators shorten through the movement while the anterior cuff lengthens, giving no net length advantage across the group.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 14.71, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "obliques", "etu_cm2": 10.49, "rationale": "The tension is meaningful and sustained rather than incidental, which places it above the floor, but it is isometric with essentially no muscle excursion. 0.85 per the standard treatment of meaningful low-ROM stabilization.", "normalized_etu": 0.1284, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "smh_interpretation": "Anti-rotation work holds a fixed mid-range length; no lengthened loading.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 12.34, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "etu_cm2": 7.60, "rationale": "Part dynamic contribution to horizontal abduction, part isometric support of the abducted arm. Slightly below reference because only the posterior portion of the head is meaningfully loaded and its excursion is small.", "normalized_etu": 0.1662, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "smh_interpretation": "Held near mid-length at shoulder height throughout; no length advantage either way.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9000, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "etu_cm2": 6.47, "rationale": "More isometric than middle trapezius, since much of its force opposes elevation rather than producing the retraction excursion, and its high-tension range is short and shortened-biased. 0.90.", "normalized_etu": 0.4319, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "smh_interpretation": "Shortens as the scapula retracts and depresses; no stretch-mediated case.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.19, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "etu_cm2": 5.34, "rationale": "Meaningful sustained anti-rotation and anti-flexion isometry with no excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0501, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "smh_interpretation": "Static spinal position; no length variation.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.28, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9000, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "etu_cm2": 4.50, "rationale": "Assists transverse extension over a short range at a length far from its productive lengthened position, with a poor moment arm at shoulder height. 0.90.", "normalized_etu": 0.1026, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "smh_interpretation": "The lat's productive lengthened position requires shoulder flexion and elevation, which never occurs in this movement.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 4.25, "rationale": "Sustained isometric grip, meaningful but with zero excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0781, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "Static grip with no length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "brachialis", "etu_cm2": 3.94, "rationale": "Held at a single mid-range length with no excursion for the whole set. Meaningful load transmission rather than incidental co-contraction, so 0.85 rather than the floor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0894, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "smh_interpretation": "The elbow angle is fixed, so the muscle occupies one length for the entire repetition.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.63, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "etu_cm2": 3.15, "rationale": "Low-excursion isometric girdle support in a shortened position. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.1627, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "smh_interpretation": "Shortened positional support of the shoulder girdle throughout.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.71, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "etu_cm2": 2.91, "rationale": "Isometric elbow fixation and humeral head restraint with no excursion at either joint it crosses. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0731, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "smh_interpretation": "Fixed elbow and a shoulder that extends rather than flexes give no lengthened loading at either joint.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.42, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "etu_cm2": 2.55, "rationale": "Sustained low-ROM isometric control of forearm orientation. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0419, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "smh_interpretation": "Forearm rotation is held constant; no length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 2.15, "rationale": "Sustained bracing isometry without excursion. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0979, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "smh_interpretation": "Pressure generation involves negligible length change.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.53, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "etu_cm2": 1.93, "rationale": "Antagonist co-contraction that occurs at a genuinely lengthened position, which lifts it off the floor value, but at a very small fraction of capacity. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0536, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "smh_interpretation": "Scapular retraction does lengthen the serratus, but the co-contractile tension is far too low to convert that into a reference-quality stimulus.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.27, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "etu_cm2": 1.70, "rationale": "Low-ROM isometric bracing. 0.85.", "normalized_etu": 0.0700, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "smh_interpretation": "Static trunk position throughout.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.9000, "muscle": "teres_major", "etu_cm2": 1.35, "rationale": "Small dynamic synergist working over a short range away from its productive length. 0.90, matching latissimus dorsi.", "normalized_etu": 0.3000, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "smh_interpretation": "Like the lat, its lengthened position requires shoulder flexion, which does not occur here.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "shortened"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.8500, "unweighted_mean": 0.8917, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9241}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9241, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 132.67}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "rhomboids": 1.0000, "brachialis": 0.9500, "teres_major": 0.9500, "rotator_cuffs": 1.0000, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 0.9500, "erector_spinae": 0.9500, "deltoid_lateral": 1.0000, "trapezius_lower": 1.0000, "trapezius_upper": 1.0000, "latissimus_dorsi": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 1.0000, "deltoid_posterior": 1.0000, "serratus_anterior": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500}	{"slug": "side_facing_reverse_pec_deck", "notes": ["No muscle warranted an above-reference value: reverse fly loads are light in absolute terms, the scapular and horizontal-abduction ranges are short, and the machine path removes deceleration demands, so no high-force lengthening or persistent-deficit mechanism is present.", "The posterior deltoid was deliberately not matched to the 1.10 assigned to the pectoralis in a chest fly — although both are single-joint flies, the absolute force and the achievable lengthened range are much smaller here, and torso contact limits horizontal adduction.", "Rotator cuffs, upper/lower trapezius and lateral deltoid were placed at the reference rather than 0.95 because in this movement they act as dynamic agonists moving through the arc, not as low-level positional stabilizers.", "The single-arm side-facing setup creates a genuine anti-rotation demand, but at low force relative to trunk capacity, so trunk musculature remains at 0.95 rather than elevated.", "Teres major's moderate tension fraction is an artefact of its very small FCSA and was not converted into a modifier."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "A single-joint fly does maintain resistance into the adducted starting position where the posterior deltoid is lengthened, but the absolute forces involved in reverse fly work are modest and the achievable horizontal adduction range is anatomically limited by the torso, so the lengthened-position exposure does not reach the severity seen in a loaded chest fly. Conventional dynamic tension with a controlled eccentric places this at the neutral reference; the high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "tension_fraction": 0.7164, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 17.66, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Performs scapular retraction dynamically through a short range with a controlled eccentric into protraction. Scapular excursion is small and the loads are light in absolute terms, so no high-force lengthening mechanism is present and the reference value applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "tension_fraction": 0.6455, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.90, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Dynamic scapular retraction against machine resistance with an ordinary eccentric return; the short scapular range and modest absolute load mean nothing distinguishes this from the conventional reference condition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.5851, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.53, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Unlike its purely stabilizing role in pressing patterns, here the infraspinatus and teres minor act as genuine dynamic agonists of external rotation and horizontal abduction, moving through the range against resistance rather than holding position. This dynamic contribution places it at the reference rather than the sub-reference value assigned to low-level isometric stabilization.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0861, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 14.71, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "The single-arm side-facing setup creates a rotational torque the trunk must resist, but at low force relative to the obliques' available capacity and with the trunk held at neutral length. This is stabilizing isometry with inter-repetition unloading, producing predominantly transient fatigue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.1510, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 12.34, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_anti_rotation_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Its posterior fibres contribute dynamically to horizontal abduction through the movement arc rather than merely holding position, so this is conventional dynamic tension with a controlled eccentric at the reference value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "tension_fraction": 0.1749, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Contributes actively to scapular retraction and depression through the movement range rather than holding a static position, giving a conventional dynamic profile with no distinguishing recovery mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.4800, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.19, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Resists the rotational and lateral pull of the single-arm setup at low force relative to its large capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; such postural tension leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0590, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.28, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Contributes low-force humeral and torso stabilization rather than driving the horizontal abduction arc; the tension is submaximal, non-lengthening under load and releases between repetitions.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.1140, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm