rotational excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.86, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "etu_cm2": 1.28, "rationale": "Grip tension is mechanically necessary but predominantly isometric and low-ROM.", "normalized_etu": 0.0235, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "smh_interpretation": "The hand maintains a fixed dumbbell grip without meaningful wrist or finger excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "etu_cm2": 0.85, "rationale": "The exposure supports wrist stability but remains isometric and accessory.", "normalized_etu": 0.0158, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "smh_interpretation": "The wrist extensors stabilize a nearly fixed wrist beneath the dumbbell.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8000, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 0.64, "rationale": "Low-level co-contractile joint stabilization with negligible productive rotational ROM warrants the lower calibration anchor.", "normalized_etu": 0.0037, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "The cuff provides low-level humeral-head centring while the preacher bench externally stabilizes the upper arm.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "etu_cm2": 0.51, "rationale": "The exposure is low-level, positional, and predominantly isometric.", "normalized_etu": 0.0167, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "smh_interpretation": "The anterior deltoid provides minor humeral-position stability without meaningful loaded shoulder-flexion excursion.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.60, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.8500, "median": 0.8500, "maximum": 1.0500, "minimum": 0.8000, "unweighted_mean": 0.9000, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9898}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9898, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 51.21}	{"brachialis": 1.1000, "rotator_cuffs": 0.9500, "wrist_flexors": 0.9500, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "brachioradialis": 1.1000, "wrist_extensors": 0.9500, "deltoid_anterior": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500}	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["The preacher pad's angle is the identifiable mechanism: peak gravitational resistance torque occurs at or near full elbow extension, so unlike a standing curl the load does not fall away where the elbow flexors are longest.", "The monoarticular elbow flexors (brachialis, brachioradialis) receive 1.10 while the biceps receives only 1.05, because the pad holds the humerus flexed and thereby shortens the biceps proximally — the inverse of the Bayesian curl arrangement, where the shoulder position amplified biceps lengthening instead.", "The neutral hammer grip shifts load toward brachialis and brachioradialis and away from the biceps, which is reflected in the supplied tension exposure rather than in R_m.", "Eccentric structural stress and lengthened-position loading were combined into one mechanism per muscle rather than counted as two full penalties, which caps the values at 1.10.", "The pronounced acute discomfort at the stretched bottom position of preacher work was excluded as a target variable; the elevation reflects persistent restoration of local force-producing capacity, not soreness or perceived difficulty."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "The preacher pad is the defining feature: with the humerus fixed on an inclined pad, the forearm approaches horizontal at full elbow extension, so gravitational resistance torque is at its maximum precisely where the elbow flexors are longest. This is the opposite of a standing curl, where load falls away at the bottom, and it means high active force coincides with near-maximal muscle length throughout the terminal eccentric — a genuine persistent-deficit mechanism. Eccentric stress and lengthened-position loading are treated as one combined mechanism, capping the value at 1.10.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.5818, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 25.63, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "peak_resistance_torque_coinciding_with_longest_muscle_length"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "It shares the preacher pad's loaded end-range elbow extension, but the pad holds the humerus in flexion, which shortens the biceps proximally and partially offsets the distal lengthening — the opposite arrangement to a Bayesian curl. The neutral hammer grip further reduces its contribution. A smaller elevation than the monoarticular elbow flexors is therefore warranted.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.2831, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 11.27, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "shoulder_flexed_position_reducing_proximal_lengthening"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "The neutral hammer grip places it in its strongest position, so it carries a substantial share of the load, and being monoarticular at the elbow it experiences the preacher pad's peak-torque-at-full-extension profile without any proximal offset. A tension fraction of 0.67 adds a small nonlinear residual to this lengthened-position loading mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6729, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 7.55, "eccentric_structural_stress": "emphasized", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_elbow_extension", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "pronounced"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Holds the neutral forearm position against the dumbbell at low force with no dynamic rotation under load and no lengthening, so the fatigue generated is transient rather than persistent.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0470, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.86, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grips the dumbbell at submaximal isometric force with no lengthening under load and no meaningful perfusion restriction; such fatigue resolves rapidly after the set.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.0276, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at very low force with no excursion and no loaded lengthening, leaving minimal persistent local deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0186, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "The preacher pad supports the humerus, so cuff involvement is reduced to negligible glenohumeral centring at very low force relative to its large capacity, with no loaded lengthening.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0047, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.80, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "With the arm resting on the pad it contributes only minimal positional tension; there is no dynamic excursion and no lengthening under load, so its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "tension_fraction": 0.0197, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.60, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 0.9500, "median": 0.9500, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 1.0000, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0723}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 51.21}	{"elbow_joint": 0.7200, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.1800, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.1000}	{"slug": "preacher_dumbbell_hammer_curl", "notes": ["The 16 kg load is interpreted according to the database bilateral convention despite the exercise being performed one arm at a time.", "The neutral grip shifts elbow-flexor demand toward brachialis and brachioradialis but does not fundamentally change the elbow-joint load class relative to other preacher-curl variants.", "External upper-arm support keeps shoulder exposure low and makes the exercise predominantly a localized elbow-flexion loading pattern."], "loaded_joints": ["elbow_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "glenohumeral_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow is the primary loaded articulation. The dumbbell creates a large external elbow-extension moment through the forearm that must be opposed by elbow-flexor force, with brachialis and brachioradialis strongly contributing in the neutral-grip configuration. High flexor force acting through short internal moment arms produces substantial elbow-joint reaction and compression. The preacher bench fixes the upper arm and places meaningful resistance on the elbow through a large portion of the intended ROM, including relatively extended elbow positions, while substantially reducing stabilization demand.", "load_exposure": 0.7200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrist must maintain a neutral hammer-grip position while transmitting the dumbbell load through the hand. Grip and forearm stabilization generate meaningful radiocarpal force transmission, but there is little intended wrist flexion, extension, or deviation, keeping exposure clearly secondary to the elbow.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The preacher bench externally supports the upper arm and largely removes the need for dynamic shoulder motion or substantial glenohumeral stabilization. Some force is transmitted proximally through muscles crossing or attaching near the shoulder, but direct shoulder loading remains low.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "minimal", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "elbow_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
