e reference rather than 0.95 because in a pulldown they act as dynamic agonists moving through a loaded range, not as positional stabilizers.", "Teres major's very high tension fraction is an artefact of its small FCSA and was deliberately not converted into a modifier."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "The weight stack maintains full resistance as the arms return overhead, so the lat resists meaningful force while lengthened at maximal shoulder elevation with scapular upward rotation — the load does not fall away at the stretched position as gravitational resistance would. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.79 contributing a small nonlinear residual, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 rather than higher because the guided cable path removes deceleration demand and the eccentric is controlled rather than overloaded.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.7868, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 34.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["loaded_eccentric_into_full_overhead_shoulder_elevation", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a conventional range with a controlled eccentric; the overhead position that lengthens the biceps has no effect on it, and it is never loaded at an unusually lengthened elbow position. The high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.7442, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.78, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grip tension is continuous and substantial here, but it is purely isometric force transmission with no lengthening and no contractile excursion under load. Per the isometry guidance, such fatigue is predominantly intra-set and resolves once the handle is released, so it does not warrant a persistent-recovery penalty despite the tension magnitude, which is already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.5101, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.75, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_gripping_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Being biarticular, it is lengthened at both ends in the top position — elbow extended and shoulder fully elevated — while the stack still applies meaningful resistance. This combined two-joint lengthening under load is the specific mechanism separating it from the monoarticular elbow flexors, warranting a small elevation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.6109, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 24.32, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["biarticular_lengthening_at_overhead_elbow_extension", "conventional