ery profile and pull in opposite directions: the dead-stop reset removes the loaded eccentric from every upper-body muscle, while the rigidly held horizontal torso imposes near-maximal sustained isometry on the posterior chain.", "Erector spinae is the one muscle meeting the specification's threshold for an isometry-based penalty: tension fraction of 0.90 held continuously with real perfusion constraint, at a lengthened spinal position resisting a heavy flexion moment — plausible residual deficit rather than purely intra-set fatigue.", "Hip extensors were held at the reference rather than elevated because, unlike the erectors, they work at a shortened-to-neutral length with no lengthening component; the biarticular hamstrings received 1.05 because the hinged position holds them near end-range length under load.", "Upper-body pulling muscles sit at the reference rather than above it precisely because the bar resets on the floor: there is no loaded eccentric into the stretched position, which is the mechanism that elevated the lat in the cable row and pulldown.", "Grip received 0.95 despite substantial tension, since it is isometric force transmission with genuine inter-repetition release at the floor.", "The explosive reputation of the Pendlay row was not encoded as a force-development factor, since the standardized effective repetition specifies controlled conventional tempo; systemic and cardiovascular cost was also excluded as belonging to separate models."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "gluteus_maximus", "rationale": "In a Pendlay row the torso is held rigidly horizontal, so the hip extensors work isometrically at a fixed hip angle rather than moving through a range — there is no active lengthening at all. Sustained high-force isometry with no unloading phase within the repetition lifts this above the sub-reference value given to low-level stabilizers, and a tension fraction of 0.73 supports that. It is not elevated further because isometric fatigue without a lengthening component substantially resolves once tension is released, and the specification cautions against giving isometry a large persistent penalty.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 143.08, "tension_fraction": 0.7268, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 103.99, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_fixed_hip_angle", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.1000, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "This is the case where sustained isometry does justify elevation. The spinal extensors hold a horizontal torso against a heavy barbell at a tension fraction of 0.90 — near-maximal — continuously, with no unloading phase and materially restricted local perfusion at that intensity. The hip-hinged position also holds them at a lengthened position while they resist the flexion moment, so the tension is not merely postural. The combination makes a genuine post-exercise force deficit plausible rather than purely intra-set fatigue. Capped at 1.10 because much isometric fatigue still resolves on release and the isometric and lengthened-position factors are treated as one combined mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.9025, "perfusion_constraint": "high", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "very_high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 96.10, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["near_maximal_sustained_isometry_with_perfusion_constraint", "high_force_resistance_to_spinal_flexion_at_lengthened_position", "near_maximal_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "adductor_magnus", "rationale": "Acts with the gluteus maximus to hold the hinged hip angle isometrically against the load. Continuous substantial tension with no unloading phase places it above the low-level stabilizer value, but the absence of any lengthening or excursion under load means the fatigue is predominantly intra-set, so the neutral reference is appropriate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 105.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6136, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 64.57, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_fixed_hip_angle"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "semimembranosus", "rationale": "The deeply hinged hip with relatively extended knees holds the biarticular hamstrings near end-range length for the entire repetition while they carry substantial isometric force against the torso's flexion moment. Tension at long muscle length is the distinguishing factor against the gluteal and adductor entries, which are loaded at a shortened-to-neutral position. This warrants a small elevation; the absence of active lengthening excursion keeps it well below the values assigned to dynamic lengthened-position work.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 88.78, "tension_fraction": 0.5814, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 51.62, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_substantially_lengthened_hamstring_position"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "The defining feature of the Pendlay row is that the bar is reset on the floor each repetition, so unlike a cable row or a suspended barbell row there is no loaded eccentric into the stretched position — the muscle unloads completely at the bottom. Concentric-dominant dynamic tension with full unloading would ordinarily suggest a sub-reference value, but the tension is high-force through a full range, so the neutral reference is the appropriate holistic estimate.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.7672, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 33.64, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a concentric-dominant range with the load released at the floor between repetitions; no lengthened-position loading and no eccentric emphasis. The high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.7269, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.02, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Grip tension is substantial but purely isometric force transmission with no contractile excursion, and the bar returning to the floor each repetition allows genuine release between reps — unlike a hanging or continuous-tension row. Such fatigue is predominantly intra-set, and the magnitude is already carried by T_m.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.5779, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.44, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["gripping_isometric_tension_with_inter_repetition_release"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_femoris_long_head", "rationale": "Held near end-range length by the deep hip hinge with relatively extended knees while carrying substantial isometric force throughout the repetition. Tension at long muscle length distinguishes it from the gluteal and adductor entries, warranting a small elevation without the larger value that active lengthening excursion would justify.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.70, "tension_fraction": 0.5814, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.22, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_substantially_lengthened_hamstring_position"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Performs dynamic scapular retraction against heavy resistance, but with the bar resetting on the floor each repetition there is no loaded eccentric into protraction; concentric-dominant high-force contraction sits at the neutral reference.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.7434, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 21.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "The bar rests on the floor between repetitions, so the biceps is never held under load at the extended-elbow position that produces lengthened-position stress in continuous-tension rows. Conventional concentric-dominant elbow flexion applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.5147, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 20.49, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Drives scapular retraction dynamically through the pulling arc with full unloading at the floor; high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already in T_m, and no structural mechanism accompanies it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "tension_fraction": 0.7639, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 20.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "Contributes dynamically to horizontal abduction and shoulder extension through the pull; it shortens rather than lengthens under load and unloads at the floor, so no lengthened-position mechanism applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "tension_fraction": 0.6540, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.12, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "A free barbell has no fixed path, so the cuff actively controls humeral head position and bar trajectory through an unguided arc rather than passively centring, placing it at the reference rather than the sub-reference stabilizer value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0878, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "semitendinosus", "rationale": "Shares the hamstring group's exposure: held near end-range length by the hinged position throughout the repetition while carrying substantial isometric force. Tension at long muscle length justifies the same small elevation as the other biarticular hamstrings.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 25.59, "tension_fraction": 0.5451, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 13.95, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_isometry_at_substantially_lengthened_hamstring_position"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Braces the trunk against rotation and lateral flexion at low force relative to its large capacity, with the trunk at neutral length; per the isometry guidance, low-level stabilization leaves little persistent deficit despite the continuous duty cycle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "tension_fraction": 0.1346, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 11.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Actively depresses and retracts the scapula through the pulling range rather than holding position, giving a conventional dynamic profile with complete unloading at the floor.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.6008, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Assists elbow flexion concentrically with the bar released at the floor each repetition; no lengthened-position loading or eccentric emphasis is present.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.5811, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.52, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_upper", "rationale": "As an elevator it stabilises rather than drives the retraction pattern, holding scapular position at low force without loaded lengthening; its fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 19.36, "tension_fraction": 0.2841, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates intra-abdominal pressure to support the loaded hinge at low force with no contractile lengthening under load, producing predominantly transient fatigue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.2276, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Contributes anti-extension bracing rather than resisting the primary flexion moment, at low force and without lengthening under load, so its fatigue is largely transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.29, "tension_fraction": 0.2058, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at low force with no excursion and no loaded lengthening, and releases at the floor each repetition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0932, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "deep_neck_extensors", "rationale": "Holds head position in the hinged stance at very low force relative to its large capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; the fatigue produced is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 84.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0412, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_postural_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Works as a dynamic shoulder extensor and adductor alongside the lat with full unloading at the floor. Its high tension fraction is an artefact of its very small FCSA and was not converted into a modifier.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "tension_fraction": 0.7267, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.27, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "brief", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "Holds forearm rotation fixed against the bar at very low force with no dynamic rotation under load; the fatigue generated is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0493, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 1.0000, "median": 1.0000, "maximum": 1.1000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_mean": 0.9938, "tension_weighted_mean": 1.0231}, "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 607.95}	{"hip_joint": 0.4200, "elbow_joint": 0.6200, "lumbar_spine": 0.8200, "radiocarpal_joint": 0.2000, "glenohumeral_joint": 0.7400, "acromioclavicular_joint": 0.1800, "scapulothoracic_articulation": 0.6500}	{"slug": "barbell_pendlay_row", "notes": ["The defining distinction from chest-supported rowing is the very large isometric trunk-extension requirement created by the nearly horizontal torso and unsupported barbell.", "Lumbar exposure is high despite minimal spinal movement because external trunk-flexion torque, erector force, compression, and shear are all substantial.", "Upper-limb exposure remains high, but the exercise distributes load across the shoulder, scapular complex, elbow, grip, hip, and lumbar spine rather than concentrating it in one articulation."], "loaded_joints": ["lumbar_spine", "glenohumeral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "elbow_joint", "hip_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "acromioclavicular_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "lumbar_spine", "rationale": "The lumbar spine experiences a very large external trunk-flexion moment because the torso is held nearly horizontal while the 90 kg bar is positioned anterior to the lumbar region. High erector-spinae force and abdominal co-contraction are required to maintain a rigid trunk, producing substantial spinal compression and meaningful shear despite minimal intended lumbar motion. Relative to conventional resistance exercises, this is a high lumbar-loading pattern because large external leverage and heavy force coincide with prolonged unsupported trunk fixation.", "load_exposure": 0.8200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "shear", "co_contraction", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "very_high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "very_high"}, {"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joint undergoes substantial shoulder extension and horizontal-abduction loading as the bar is accelerated toward the torso. Latissimus dorsi, posterior deltoid, teres major, and rotator-cuff musculature generate high force, producing substantial joint reaction and humeral-head stabilization. The unsupported free bar and bent-over position increase control demands relative to a chest-supported row.", "load_exposure": 0.7400, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "translation", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae must retract and remain firmly controlled against the thorax while large pulling forces are transmitted from the arms into the trunk. Rhomboids, trapezial musculature, serratus anterior, and associated stabilizers generate substantial force throughout the pull. Free-bar execution and the absence of chest support make scapular fixation and coordinated retraction mechanically important.", "load_exposure": 0.6500, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbow flexors generate substantial force to oppose the external extension moment created by the 90 kg bar. Brachialis, brachioradialis, and biceps contribute to elbow flexion and generate meaningful joint reaction and compression. Elbow exposure is substantial but remains below direct curling exercises because shoulder and scapular musculature share much of the pulling task.", "load_exposure": 0.6200, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "high", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "hip_joint", "rationale": "The hips remain flexed while the posterior chain resists the tendency of the trunk and barbell to rotate the pelvis forward. Gluteal, hamstring, and adductor forces contribute substantial isometric hip-extension demand and joint compression. Because the hip does not move through a large dynamic ROM and the load is shared with the lumbar spine, exposure is moderate rather than high.", "load_exposure": 0.4200, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["net_joint_moment", "compression", "muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrists remain near neutral while transmitting grip force to a heavy free bar. Forearm musculature must generate substantial grip and positional stabilization, producing meaningful radiocarpal force transmission, but direct wrist flexion-extension moment remains modest.", "load_exposure": 0.2000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "Large pulling forces are transmitted through the shoulder girdle while the scapulae retract, producing some AC-joint force transmission and stabilization demand. However, the joint is not placed under the same elevated or heavily compressed shoulder-girdle conditions seen in pressing or overhead-loading exercises.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "mid_range", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "lumbar_spine", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}}
