atigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "tension_fraction": 0.1388, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "pronators_supinators", "rationale": "The forearm rotation in an Arnold press occurs primarily at the shoulder rather than the radioulnar joint, so these muscles only hold grip orientation at very low force with no loaded lengthening; their fatigue is transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 60.88, "tension_fraction": 0.0246, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "intermittent", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Generates intra-abdominal pressure at low force with no contractile lengthening under load, producing predominantly transient fatigue.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "tension_fraction": 0.0683, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "moderate", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_bracing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}], "modifier_distribution": {"mode": 1.0000, "median": 1.0000, "maximum": 1.0000, "minimum": 0.9500, "unweighted_m