tion cautions against giving sustained isometry a large persistent-recovery penalty.", "All shoulder-girdle, scapular, trunk and neck involvement operates at low force relative to available capacity, which the specification treats as having little effect on R_m, hence 0.95 despite continuous duty cycles.", "Joint and connective-tissue decompression effects commonly attributed to hanging were excluded, as they belong to separate recovery models rather than local skeletal-muscle recovery cost."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "This is the case where the isometry guidance permits a modest penalty rather than the usual sub-reference treatment. Grip tension is the entire exercise: high force at a tension fraction of 0.75, held genuinely continuously with no unloading phase and marked local perfusion restriction throughout. That combination makes a residual post-exercise force deficit plausible rather than purely intra-set fatigue. The elevation is kept small because the tension is pure isometric force transmission with no contractile excursion and no lengthening, and much of this fatigue still resolves once the bar is released.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.7539, "perfusion_constraint": "high", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 41.01, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_high_force_gripping_isometry_with_marked_perfusion_constraint", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Maintains glenohumeral centring against distraction at low force relative to its very large available capacity, with no active lengthening under meaningful load. Per the isometry guidance, low-level stabilization produces fatigue that resolves largely on release, so it sits below the dynamic reference despite the continuous duty cycle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0351, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "wrist_extensors", "rationale": "Stabilises wrist position as a grip antagonist at very low force relative to its capacity, without the high-force perfusion constraint that distinguishes the flexors here; such low-level tension leaves little persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0745, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Provides scapular depression against downward distraction at low force with no dynamic excursion and no loaded lengthening; the resulting fatigue resolves largely on release rather than persisting.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.2003, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_rec