nt position, tensile reaction force, and continuous stabilization."], "loaded_joints": ["glenohumeral_joint", "scapulothoracic_articulation", "acromioclavicular_joint", "radiocarpal_joint", "elbow_joint"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "joint_evaluation": [{"joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "rationale": "The glenohumeral joints continuously transmit the suspended bodyweight while the arms are maintained in an overhead position. Although there is little dynamic shoulder torque, substantial tensile joint reaction and rotator-cuff stabilization are required to control humeral-head translation under the approximately 85 kg bodyweight load. The overhead position is mechanically demanding, making the dead hang a substantial shoulder-loading exercise even without active pressing or pulling.", "load_exposure": 0.6800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "translation", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae remain elevated and upwardly rotated while suspended bodyweight is transferred from the upper limbs to the thorax. Scapular stabilizers must control position and prevent uncontrolled shoulder-girdle displacement throughout the hang. Functional scapulothoracic exposure is therefore substantial despite minimal dynamic scapular movement.", "load_exposure": 0.5600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "high", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "high"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "Suspended bodyweight is transmitted through the scapula-clavicle complex while the shoulder girdle remains elevated and upwardly rotated. This creates meaningful tensile and translational force transmission through the AC joint, although loading remains below heavy overhead pressing patterns where large muscular forces are superimposed on the elevated shoulder position.", "load_exposure": 0.3600, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "translation", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "moderate", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "moderate", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrists transmit grip force required to support the full bodyweight while remaining approximately neutral around the bar. Finger-flexor and forearm forces create meaningful radiocarpal force transmission, but direct wrist flexion or extension torque is limited. Exposure is therefore low-to-moderate despite the very high grip demand.", "load_exposure": 0.2400, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "elbow_joint", "rationale": "The elbows remain near full extension and perform essentially no dynamic flexion or extension work. Bodyweight force is transmitted through the radius and ulna, but local elbow muscular demand and net joint moment remain small compared with pulling or curling exercises.", "load_exposure": 0.1000, "loaded_joint_position": "favorable", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "minimal", "stabilization_requirement": "low", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "minimal", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}], "maximum_exposure_joint": "glenohumeral_joint", "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "prox