ut cannot be propulsive, since it has no humeral attachment. Retained at its supplied value as the mandated lower bound and reclassified.", "gluteus_maximus was excluded here despite being included in the barbell bench press. No leg drive or arch maintenance is required in a seated machine press, so no specific mechanical pathway justifies hip extensor tension.", "rectus_abdominis was excluded, as the inclined seat holds the trunk and no lumbar extension moment requires active anterior resistance."], "added_muscles": ["wrist_flexors", "trapezius_middle", "rhomboids", "erector_spinae", "trapezius_lower", "obliques", "latissimus_dorsi", "transverse_abdominis"], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "rationale": "Generates horizontal adduction torque against the handles. Its value sits below the flat bench figure, consistent with the inclined path shifting share toward the clavicular fibres and anterior deltoid. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.5965, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 31.90, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.90, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "rationale": "Elbow extension torque through the pressing arc. Supplied value retained; the machine imposes no separate stabilizing demand on this muscle.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7349, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 26.53, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 26.53, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "rationale": "Co-prime mover for humeral flexion, with a larger share than on a flat bench because the inclined press path aligns more closely with its line of action. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7597, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 23.14, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 23.14, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "triceps_long_head", "rationale": "Contributes elbow extension while spanning the shoulder, where it also resists the flexion moment. One force state serving both joints; supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "multi_joint_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.4944, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 21.53, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 21.53, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow extensor active across the full extension range. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7351, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 11.46, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 11.46, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "rationale": "The inclined path matches the upward and inward fibre direction of the clavicular head, which explains its markedly higher value than on the flat bench. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.7263, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 9.82, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.82, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_propulsive"}, {"muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "Increased modestly from 6.32 to 8.00. Humeral head centring is required against the superior shear that deltoid contraction produces at an inclined angle, and the elevated humeral position narrows the subacromial space. The increase is small because the fixed converging path constrains the handles in all planes but the intended one, removing most of the restraint demand that made this the largest addition in the free-weight rows.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0468, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.32, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.00, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Holds the scapula against the thorax so the pectoral and deltoid force acts from a stable origin, with a small protraction component as the converging handles travel forward at lockout. Supplied value retained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1699, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 6.12, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "dynamic_concentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.12, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Added because the load passes from handle to forearm through the hand and the wrist must be held against the extension moment. Lower than the barbell bench value, since the machine handles constrain wrist position rather than balancing a free bar.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "mechanical_roles": ["grip_maintenance", "load_transmission"], "tension_fraction": 0.0919, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 5.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "deltoid_lateral", "rationale": "Its anterior fibres assist humeral elevation on the inclined path. Supplied value retained; the fixed handle path removes the plane-control demand present in free-weight pressing.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 45.73, "mechanical_roles": ["propulsive", "joint_stabilization"], "tension_fraction": 0.0853, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 3.90, "contraction_character": ["dynamic_concentric", "isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.90, "input_contribution_classification": "mixed_propulsive_and_stabilizing"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Added because the scapula must be held retracted against the seat back while the handles drive it toward protraction. Isometric throughout with no excursion.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1337, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Shares the scapular retraction fixation demand with middle trapezius. The seat provides a reaction surface, but retraction must still be generated actively.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1239, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Added to maintain thoracic extension and keep the ribcage elevated so the pectorals act from a stable base. Deliberately half the barbell bench value, since the inclined backrest supports the trunk directly and no arch is actively held.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0282, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Added because scapular depression opposes the elevation tendency the inclined load creates and completes the fixation couple with the retractors.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.1669, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "obliques", "rationale": "Added for low-level trunk rigidity against the reaction force pushing the torso back into the seat. Small, since the load is bilaterally symmetric and the seat absorbs most of it.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 81.71, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0245, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Added for low-level control of the humerus during the eccentric return and maintenance of a packed shoulder position. Substantially smaller than the barbell bench value because the machine path controls the descent, leaving little restraint requirement.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "mechanical_roles": ["joint_stabilization", "eccentric_control"], "tension_fraction": 0.0456, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric", "dynamic_eccentric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 2.00, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "transverse_abdominis", "rationale": "Added for intra-abdominal pressure supporting the braced trunk. Low-level and sustained.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 21.97, "mechanical_roles": ["trunk_bracing"], "tension_fraction": 0.0683, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.00, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 1.50, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}, {"muscle": "pectoralis_minor", "rationale": "Attaches to the coracoid rather than the humerus and produces no pressing torque; its force stabilizes the scapula. Correctly small in the supplied vector and retained, though reclassified as stabilizing rather than propulsive, matching the barbell bench treatment.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 10.81, "mechanical_roles": ["scapular_control", "isometric_fixation"], "tension_fraction": 0.0888, "propulsive_fcsa_cm2": 0.96, "contraction_character": ["isometric"], "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 0.96, "input_contribution_classification": "primarily_stabilizing_or_load_transmitting"}], "saturated_muscles": [], "standardized_repetition": {"tempo": "controlled conventional tempo", "range_of_motion": "full intended ROM", "proximity_to_failure": "effective repetition"}, "tension_expansion_ratio": 1.1742, "propulsive_vector_sum_cm2": 141.68, "propulsive_fcsa_demand_cm2": 141.68, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 166.36}	{"obliques": 1.70, "rhomboids": 2.98, "rotator_cuffs": 6.80, "wrist_flexors": 4.25, "erector_spinae": 2.55, "deltoid_lateral": 3.71, "trapezius_lower": 2.13, "deltoid_anterior": 24.30, "latissimus_dorsi": 1.80, "pectoralis_minor": 0.82, "trapezius_middle": 2.98, "serratus_anterior": 5.20, "triceps_long_head": 21.53, "triceps_medial_head": 11.46, "transverse_abdominis": 1.28, "triceps_lateral_head": 26.53, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 33.50, "pectoralis_major_clavicular": 11.29}	{"slug": "plate_loaded_converging_incline_chest_press", "notes": ["Hypertrophic quality ratio of 0.9907 sits essentially at the global calibration target, closely matching the barbell bench press at 0.9870.", "pectoralis_major_clavicular at 1.15 is the highest modifier assigned to that muscle across this series and the only value above 1.10 in this row. The justification is specific to the converging incline geometry: the resistance vector matches the clavicular fibre direction across the entire arc, producing a broad high-tension range rather than a narrow peak. Its normalized ETU of 0.8351 is the highest in this row.", "pectoralis_major_sternal is deliberately placed one step below its flat barbell bench value. The converging path maintains load as the arms come together, which broadens the range but reduces the lengthened-position emphasis that justified 1.10 there. This is the analytically important contrast of the row: the same machine feature helps the clavicular head and slightly dilutes the advantage for the sternal head.", "rotator_cuffs at 0.85 is one step below the value assigned in both free-weight pressing rows. A guided converging path removes the humeral restraint and eccentric control demands that a barbell or dumbbell imposes, leaving pure centring isometry.", "All three triceps heads sit at the neutral 1.00, consistent with the flat bench and in contrast to the overhead press where the long head earned 1.10 from shoulder-mediated lengthening.", "Tension expansion ratio of 1.1742 alongside a near-reference quality ratio illustrates the intended behaviour of the model: the machine reduces total tension breadth relative to free weights without penalising the quality of the tension its prime movers receive.", "No muscle received the 0.80 floor."], "etu_sum_cm2": 164.81, "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_sternal", "etu_cm2": 33.50, "rationale": "Retains a favourable bottom-loaded profile, but placed one step below the 1.10 given on the flat barbell bench. The converging design deliberately adds resistance at the shortened end where the arms converge, which broadens the high-tension range but dilutes the lengthened-position emphasis that earned the barbell press its larger bonus.", "normalized_etu": 0.6264, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.48, "smh_interpretation": "No SMH factor supplied. The stretched start position is loaded, but the converging path means the handles come together at lockout, taking the muscle into a shortened position while still under meaningful load.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 31.90, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "triceps_lateral_head", "etu_cm2": 26.53, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic elbow extension with high recruitment and controlled phases, and no length or resistance-profile feature in either direction. The neutral reference value, matching the flat bench treatment.", "normalized_etu": 0.7349, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.10, "smh_interpretation": "Monoarticular; elbow angle alone sets length, and the machine's fixed path limits depth of elbow flexion at the start.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 26.53, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "deltoid_anterior", "etu_cm2": 24.30, "rationale": "Shares the favourable bottom-loaded profile, with the incline placing the humerus at a longer position than a flat press does. Matched to the flat bench value at 1.05, since the incline's length advantage is offset by the converging path maintaining load at the shortened end.", "normalized_etu": 0.7977, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 30.46, "smh_interpretation": "The inclined bottom position takes the humerus into extension behind the torso plane under load, lengthening the muscle where demand is high.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 23.14, "resistance_profile_alignment": "good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "triceps_long_head", "etu_cm2": 21.53, "rationale": "Not separated from the other heads, consistent with the flat bench treatment and in deliberate contrast to the overhead press where shoulder elevation lengthened it proximally at the moment of peak elbow demand. An incline flexes the humerus forward instead, so no such mechanism exists.", "normalized_etu": 0.4944, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.55, "smh_interpretation": "The biarticular case is neutral. Elbow flexion lengthens it distally at the start while the flexed humeral position of an incline shortens it proximally, and the two effects offset.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 21.53, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 1.1500, "muscle": "pectoralis_major_clavicular", "etu_cm2": 11.29, "rationale": "The highest modifier in this row and the one muscle for which the converging incline design is unambiguously optimal. Its fibre direction matches the resistance vector across the whole arc, so tension stays high from the lengthened bottom position through the shortened convergence rather than falling off at either end. This broad, well-distributed high-tension range combined with favourable resistance alignment justifies 1.15. Contrast with the overhead press, where this muscle received 0.90 for a genuinely brief loading window, and the flat bench at 1.05 where the horizontal path suits it less well.", "normalized_etu": 0.8351, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "broad", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 13.52, "smh_interpretation": "The inclined path aligns with the upward and inward fibre direction, and the converging arc keeps tension on these fibres from the stretched start through to full adduction.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 9.82, "resistance_profile_alignment": "very_good", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "moderately_lengthened"}, {"H_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "triceps_medial_head", "etu_cm2": 11.46, "rationale": "Conventional dynamic elbow extension with no muscle-specific feature. The neutral reference value.", "normalized_etu": 0.7351, "contraction_mode": ["concentric", "eccentric"], "high_tension_rom": "substantial", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 15.59, "smh_interpretation": "Identical elbow-driven length profile to the lateral head.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 11.46, "resistance_profile_alignment": "moderate", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "moderate", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mid_range"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "etu_cm2": 6.80, "rationale": "Reduced to 0.85 rather than the 0.90 used in the barbell bench and overhead press. The guided handle path removes the eccentric restraint role the cuff performs when controlling a free implement, leaving centring isometry with essentially no excursion.", "normalized_etu": 0.0398, "contraction_mode": ["isometric"], "high_tension_rom": "brief", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "smh_interpretation": "Individual cuff muscles occupy opposing lengths through the arc, leaving no net advantage for the group.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 8.00, "resistance_profile_alignment": "poor", "duration_of_meaningful_tension": "sustained", "muscle_length_under_high_tension": "mixed"}, {"H_m": 0.8500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "etu_cm2": 5.20, "rationale": "Predominantly fixation with only a minor protraction component at the top. Matched to the barbell bench value and sharply below the 1.00 it earned in the overhead press, where genuine upward rotation excursion occurred throughout.", "normalized_etu": 0.1443, "contraction_mode": ["isometric", "concentric"], "high_tension_rom": "limited", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "smh_interpretation": "The small protraction excursion at lockout shortens it while load remains, and no lengthened loading occurs.", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.12, "r