structural mechanism accompanies it.", "Standing barbell curls are commonly performed with body English, but the standardized effective repetition excludes momentum and technical breakdown, so no rapid force-development factor was applied.", "The pronounced acute pump characteristic of arm training was excluded as a target variable; it resolves quickly and does not represent persistent restoration of force-producing readiness."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a conventional range with a controlled eccentric. Standing curls have their weakest leverage in the mid-range, and resistance torque falls off substantially at the bottom where the arm is extended, so the brachialis is not exposed to high force at long length. The high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m and no accompanying structural mechanism exists, so the neutral reference applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.7487, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 32.98, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Unlike the monoarticular elbow flexors, the biceps crosses the shoulder as well, so the terminal eccentric position — full elbow extension with the humerus hanging neutral or slightly behind — places it at a genuinely long length while meaningful force is still resisted. This mild high-force lengthening, combined with a tension fraction of 0.70 contributing a small nonlinear residual, justifies a modest elevation. It stays at 1.05 because standing barbell curls unload substantially at full extension and the shoulder is not held in extension as it would be in an incline or drag variant.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.7001, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 27.87, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["biarticular_lengthening_across_elbow_and_shoulder_during_eccentric", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachioradialis", "rationale": "Assists elbow flexion through a conventional dynamic range with a controlled eccentric and no lengthened-position loading; the supinated barbell grip reduces its mechanical advantage but does not alter its recovery profile. Nothing distinguishes this exposure from the reference condition.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 11.22, "tension_fraction": 0.6106, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.85, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "erector_spinae", "rationale": "Resists the forward-shifting load moment at low force relative to its large capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; per the isometry guidance, low-level postural stabilization leaves little persistent deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 106.48, "tension_fraction": 0.0591, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 6.29, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "reference", 