ed_mean": 0.9095, "tension_weighted_mean": 0.9484}, "hypertrophic_quality_ratio": 0.9484, "active_tension_exposure_sum_cm2": 215.49}	{"obliques": 0.9500, "iliopsoas": 0.9500, "rhomboids": 1.0000, "brachialis": 1.0000, "teres_major": 1.0000, "rotator_cuffs": 1.0000, "wrist_flexors": 1.0000, "biceps_brachii": 1.0500, "brachioradialis": 1.0000, "trapezius_lower": 1.0000, "wrist_extensors": 0.9500, "latissimus_dorsi": 1.0500, "pectoralis_minor": 0.9500, "rectus_abdominis": 0.9500, "trapezius_middle": 1.0000, "deltoid_posterior": 1.0000, "serratus_anterior": 0.9500, "deep_neck_extensors": 0.9500, "pronators_supinators": 0.9500, "transverse_abdominis": 0.9500, "pectoralis_major_sternal": 0.9500}	{"slug": "wide_pronated_grip_pull_up", "notes": ["The above-reference mechanism is bodyweight never unloading, so the lat and the biarticular biceps resist meaningful force while lengthened in the dead hang — the same mechanism identified in the lat pulldown, and the reason both receive 1.05.", "Grip was given the reference rather than the 0.95 assigned to barbell rowing and machine pulling: hanging requires genuinely continuous gripping with no release point and real perfusion constraint, but it was not elevated because isometric force transmission without excursion resolves substantially on release.", "Contrast with the Pendlay row, where the bar resets on the floor: there the loaded eccentric into the stretched position is absent entirely, which is why its lat sits at the reference while this one is elevated.", "Monoarticular elbow flexors were held at the reference because the overhead position affects only the biarticular biceps — the same anatomical discrimination applied to curling variants.", "Scapular retractors, depressors, posterior deltoid and cuff act as dynamic agonists moving through a loaded range here, so they sit at the reference rather than the sub-reference stabilizer value.", "The wide pronated grip's reputation for shoulder stress was excluded, as joint and tendon recovery belong to separate models; only local skeletal-muscle recovery cost was estimated."], "reconciliation": "PASS", "muscle_evaluation": [{"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "latissimus_dorsi", "rationale": "Bodyweight never unloads, so the lat resists full body mass while lengthened at maximal shoulder elevation in the dead hang — the load does not fall away at the stretched position as it would with a gravitational free-weight row. Combined with a tension fraction of 0.78 contributing a small nonlinear residual, this warrants a modest elevation. It is held at 1.05 rather than higher because the eccentric is controlled rather than overloaded and the wide pronated grip shortens the effective range of shoulder adduction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 43.85, "tension_fraction": 0.7824, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 34.31, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["high_force_active_lengthening_at_full_overhead_hang", "high_relative_tension_nonlinear_residual"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "moderate"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "wrist_flexors", "rationale": "Unlike machine or barbell pulling where the handle can be released or the bar rested, hanging from a bar requires genuinely continuous gripping at substantial force with no unloading phase within the repetition, and sustained gripping materially restricts local perfusion. This lifts it above the sub-reference value assigned to intermittent gripping. It is not elevated because the tension is pure isometric force transmission with no contractile excursion, and such fatigue substantially resolves once the bar is released.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 54.40, "tension_fraction": 0.5355, "perfusion_constraint": "moderate", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 29.13, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["sustained_gripping_isometric_tension_with_no_unloading_phase"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "brachialis", "rationale": "Monoarticular elbow flexion through a conventional dynamic range with a controlled eccentric; the overhead hang that lengthens the biceps has no effect on it, and it is never loaded at an unusually lengthened elbow position.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 44.05, "tension_fraction": 0.6552, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 28.86, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rhomboids", "rationale": "Performs dynamic scapular downward rotation and retraction against bodyweight with a controlled eccentric; the scapular excursion is moderate and no lengthened-position loading mechanism warrants elevation.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 28.25, "tension_fraction": 0.5841, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_middle", "rationale": "Drives scapular retraction dynamically through the pulling arc rather than holding position; the high tension fraction reflects load magnitude already carried by T_m with no accompanying structural mechanism.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 26.18, "tension_fraction": 0.6303, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 16.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0500, "muscle": "biceps_brachii", "rationale": "Being biarticular, it is lengthened at both ends in the dead hang — elbow extended and shoulder fully elevated — while bodyweight applies continuous meaningful resistance. This combined two-joint lengthening under load is the specific mechanism separating it from the monoarticular elbow flexors, matching the treatment given to the lat pulldown.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 39.81, "tension_fraction": 0.3801, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "moderate", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 15.13, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["biarticular_lengthening_at_overhead_dead_hang", "conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "mild"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "deltoid_posterior", "rationale": "The wide grip recruits it for horizontal abduction and shoulder extension through a conventional dynamic range; it shortens rather than lengthens under load as the elbows drive down, so no lengthened-position mechanism applies.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 24.65, "tension_fraction": 0.4998, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "substantial", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 12.32, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "rotator_cuffs", "rationale": "The free-hanging body has no fixed path, so the cuff actively controls humeral head position and body trajectory through an unguided arc rather than passively centring against a machine track, placing it at the reference rather than the sub-reference stabilizer value.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 170.80, "tension_fraction": 0.0703, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 12.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "trapezius_lower", "rationale": "Actively depresses and downwardly rotates the scapula from the elevated hang position through the movement range against resistance; this is dynamic contraction rather than positional holding, so the reference value applies despite the high tension fraction.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 14.98, "tension_fraction": 0.7009, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 10.50, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["conventional_contractile_fatigue"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "rectus_abdominis", "rationale": "Holds the trunk against swing and extension during the hang at low force relative to capacity, with no lengthening under load; per the isometry guidance, such stabilization leaves little persistent deficit despite the continuous duty 