inst the bar as a grip antagonist at very low force relative to its capacity, without dynamic excursion or loaded lengthening; such low-level sustained tension leaves little persistent force deficit.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 53.67, "tension_fraction": 0.0745, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_sustained_stabilizing_isometric_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 0.9500, "muscle": "serratus_anterior", "rationale": "Acts as a low-force antagonist controlling scapular position against downward distraction rather than driving the pull; the tension is submaximal and non-lengthening under load, so its fatigue is largely transient.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 36.03, "tension_fraction": 0.1110, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "low", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 4.00, "eccentric_structural_stress": "minimal", "meaningful_tension_duration": "continuous", "sustained_isometric_tension": "sustained", "dominant_recovery_mechanisms": ["low_level_scapular_stabilizing_tension"], "high_force_lengthening_at_long_length": "none"}, {"R_m": 1.0000, "muscle": "teres_major", "rationale": "Works as a dynamic shoulder adductor and extensor alongside the lat through the pulling range. Its high tension fraction is an artefact of its very small FCSA and was not converted into a modifier.", "maximum_fcsa_cm2": 4.50, "tension_fraction": 0.7644, "perfusion_constraint": "low", "force_development_profile": "controlled", "relative_tension_severity": "high", "active_tension_exposure_cm2": 3.44, "eccentric_structural_stress": "standard", "meaningful_tension_duration": "prolonged", "sustained_isometric_tension": "none", "dominan