umeral head in the elevated position. Direct shoulder motion is limited, so exposure remains secondary to the elbow.", "load_exposure": 0.3400, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "scapulothoracic_articulation", "rationale": "The scapulae must remain upwardly rotated and controlled against the thorax while the upper arms are maintained overhead. Serratus anterior and trapezial musculature provide ongoing fixation and force transmission, but the articulation performs little large-amplitude dynamic work.", "load_exposure": 0.3000, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "moderate"}, {"joint": "acromioclavicular_joint", "rationale": "Maintaining the arms overhead requires the scapula and clavicle to remain in an elevated and upwardly rotated shoulder-girdle configuration. Cable and muscle forces are therefore transmitted through the AC joint, but the magnitude is modest compared with heavy overhead pressing.", "load_exposure": 0.1800, "loaded_joint_position": "demanding", "primary_loading_modes": ["muscle_force_transmission", "rotation", "stabilization"], "net_joint_moment_demand": "low", "stabilization_requirement": "moderate", "shear_translation_or_rotation": "low", "muscle_force_and_joint_reaction": "low"}, {"joint": "radiocarpal_joint", "rationale": "The wrists mainly transmit rope tension while maintaining a near-neutral position. Forearm musculature stabilizes the rope handles, but direct wrist flexion-extension or deviation torque remains small.", "load_exposure": 0.1200, "loaded_joint_position": "favo